// 3 ADA-aligned DAT practice tests, zero overlap.
// Bio: 150 rewritten items (filtered pool). QR: 8 expert items prioritized, 1,010 fallback items included for pacing.
window.DAT_MOCK_TESTS = [{"id":"MOCK_1","name":"Practice Test 1","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"BIO_cm075","section":"BIO","topic":"organelles","difficulty":2,"q":"Highly active cells (e.g., pancreatic acinar cells, lymphocytes) feature a prominent dense substructure visible inside the nucleus. This sub-organelle is the site of synthesis of one specific class of RNA and the assembly of another major cellular machine. What is the function of the nucleolus?","opts":["Site of DNA replication during S phase","Site of ribosomal RNA (rRNA) synthesis and assembly of pre-ribosomes (combining rRNA with ribosomal proteins)","Holds transcription factors that regulate mRNA expression","Storage compartment for excess histones","Site of mRNA splicing and 5' cap addition"],"correct":1,"why":"The nucleolus is a dense subnuclear region where RNA polymerase I transcribes rRNA genes and where rRNA is assembled with ribosomal proteins to form pre-ribosomal subunits. These are exported to the cytoplasm for final maturation. Its prominence reflects ribosome demand.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm094","section":"BIO","topic":"mitosis","difficulty":2,"q":"After DNA replication in S phase, each chromosome consists of two identical sister chromatids held together along their length, with the strongest attachment at the centromere. A multiprotein complex physically clamps the sister chromatids together until anaphase, when it is cleaved by separase. Which protein complex is this?","opts":["The kinetochore, which attaches chromosomes to microtubules","Cohesin, a ring-shaped complex that holds sister chromatids together along their entire length until anaphase","Condensin, which compacts mitotic chromosomes","Lamins, which form the nuclear lamina","Histone H1, the linker histone"],"correct":1,"why":"Cohesin is a ring-shaped protein complex that physically encircles sister chromatids, holding them together from S phase until anaphase. At the metaphase-anaphase transition, the spindle assembly checkpoint is satisfied and separase cleaves cohesin, allowing sisters to separate. Condensin compacts chromosomes (a separate role).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm002","section":"BIO","topic":"biomolecules","difficulty":2,"q":"A biochemist hydrolyzes an unknown polymer and isolates monomers each composed of a five-carbon sugar, a phosphate group, and a nitrogenous base. Which class of biological macromolecule did the polymer belong to?","opts":["Carbohydrate (polysaccharide)","Lipid (triglyceride)","Protein (polypeptide)","Nucleic acid (DNA or RNA)","Glycoprotein"],"correct":3,"why":"Nucleotides — the monomers of DNA and RNA — are composed of a pentose sugar (deoxyribose or ribose), a phosphate group, and one of five nitrogenous bases. Carbohydrates are built from monosaccharides, proteins from amino acids, and lipids lack a regular monomer structure.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm068","section":"BIO","topic":"enzymes","difficulty":2,"q":"Many enzymes require additional non-protein components for activity. Cofactors are inorganic (e.g., Zn²⁺, Mg²⁺, Fe²⁺), while coenzymes are organic small molecules. Which statement about coenzymes is most accurate?","opts":["Coenzymes are inorganic ions, often metals (Zn²⁺, Mg²⁺)","Coenzymes are organic molecules, often vitamin-derived (e.g., NAD⁺ from niacin/B3, FAD from riboflavin/B2, CoA from pantothenate/B5)","Coenzymes are always proteins themselves","Coenzymes are permanently covalently bound to the enzyme and cannot dissociate","Coenzymes are denatured forms of the enzyme"],"correct":1,"why":"Coenzymes are organic helpers (often vitamin-derived: NAD⁺ from B3, FAD from B2, CoA from B5, biotin from B7). They typically transfer chemical groups or electrons (e.g., NAD⁺/NADH transfers electrons). Cofactors (the broader term) include both inorganic ions AND coenzymes. Some coenzymes are permanently bound (prosthetic groups) but most are not.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm023","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"A medical technologist places a fresh sample of human red blood cells into a 5% NaCl solution (markedly hypertonic compared to plasma's 0.9% saline) and observes them under a microscope after 30 seconds. Which morphological change is expected?","opts":["The cells swell and lyse, releasing hemoglobin into the surrounding medium","The cells shrink and develop a crenated appearance as water flows out by osmosis","The cells remain morphologically unchanged, since RBCs are insensitive to ionic concentration","The cells actively pump in NaCl to equalize osmotic pressure","The cells undergo apoptosis, with characteristic membrane blebbing"],"correct":1,"why":"RBCs in a hypertonic solution lose water by osmosis (water flows from low solute → high solute), causing the cells to shrink and develop a wrinkled, spiky appearance called crenation. In a hypotonic solution, the opposite occurs — cells swell and may lyse (hemolysis).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm031","section":"BIO","topic":"krebs","difficulty":2,"q":"Pyruvate enters the mitochondrion and is converted to acetyl-CoA by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex. The acetyl group then enters a cyclic series of eight reactions that generate NADH, FADH2, GTP, and CO2. Where in the mitochondrion does this Krebs (citric acid) cycle take place?","opts":["Outer mitochondrial membrane, which is freely permeable to small molecules","Intermembrane space, where the proton gradient accumulates","Mitochondrial matrix, the innermost compartment surrounded by the inner membrane","Inner mitochondrial membrane (cristae), which houses the ETC complexes","Cytoplasm, in association with mitochondrial outer membrane proteins"],"correct":2,"why":"The Krebs cycle takes place in the mitochondrial matrix — the soluble fluid inside the inner membrane. The ETC complexes and ATP synthase are embedded in the inner membrane (cristae); pumped protons accumulate in the intermembrane space; and glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm027","section":"BIO","topic":"signaling","difficulty":2,"q":"Epinephrine binds to β-adrenergic receptors (a class of G-protein-coupled receptor) on cardiac muscle. The activated Gαs subunit then activates adenylyl cyclase, which converts ATP to a small intracellular signaling molecule that activates protein kinase A. Which second messenger does adenylyl cyclase produce?","opts":["NADH — the reduced form of nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide","Acetyl-CoA — the entry molecule into the citric acid cycle","Cyclic AMP (cAMP), generated from ATP by adenylyl cyclase","Pyruvate — the end product of glycolysis","Phosphoinositide PIP2, hydrolyzed by phospholipase C"],"correct":2,"why":"GPCRs coupled to Gαs activate adenylyl cyclase, which cyclizes ATP into cAMP. cAMP activates PKA, which phosphorylates downstream targets to amplify the original hormone signal. (PIP2 → IP3/DAG is a separate Gαq pathway; NADH and acetyl-CoA are metabolic intermediates, not signaling messengers.)","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm076","section":"BIO","topic":"cytoskeleton","difficulty":2,"q":"Microtubules form the longest and stiffest filaments of the cytoskeleton. They organize the mitotic spindle, serve as tracks for kinesin/dynein motor proteins, and form the core of cilia and flagella. Microtubules are polymers of which protein dimer?","opts":["Actin, which polymerizes into thin microfilaments","α-tubulin and β-tubulin heterodimers, which polymerize into hollow tubes ~25 nm in diameter","Keratin, an intermediate filament protein","Vimentin, an intermediate filament in mesenchymal cells","Lamin, an intermediate filament forming the nuclear lamina"],"correct":1,"why":"Microtubules are hollow tubes (~25 nm diameter) built from protofilaments of α/β-tubulin heterodimers. They have a polarity (+/- end), bind GTP, and serve as motor protein tracks. Actin → microfilaments (~7 nm); keratin/vimentin/lamin → intermediate filaments (~10 nm).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm059","section":"BIO","topic":"proteins","difficulty":2,"q":"Disulfide bonds are critical to the tertiary structure of many extracellular proteins (e.g., insulin, antibodies). They form by oxidation of two thiol (-SH) side chains, creating a covalent S-S bridge. Between which two amino-acid residues do disulfide bonds form?","opts":["Two cysteine residues, each contributing an -SH group that oxidizes to -S-S-","Two methionine residues (which contain a thioether, not a thiol)","One cysteine and one methionine","Two serine residues (contain -OH, not -SH)","Cysteine and selenocysteine"],"correct":0,"why":"Only cysteine has a thiol (-SH) side chain that can oxidize to form a covalent disulfide (-S-S-) with another cysteine. Methionine has a thioether (-S-CH3) that cannot. Disulfide bonds form most readily in oxidizing environments (extracellular space, ER lumen) and stabilize tertiary/quaternary structure.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm028","section":"BIO","topic":"glycolysis","difficulty":2,"q":"Glycolysis converts one molecule of glucose into two molecules of pyruvate through 10 enzymatic steps in the cytoplasm. Two ATP are invested early (priming) and four ATP are produced later (payoff), along with reduction of two NAD+ to NADH. What is the NET yield from one glucose molecule entering glycolysis?","opts":["2 ATP, 2 NADH, and 2 pyruvate molecules","4 ATP and 2 NADH (gross production, ignoring the 2 ATP investment)","1 ATP, 1 NADH, and 1 pyruvate (per glucose)","4 ATP, 4 NADH, and 4 pyruvate, since glycolysis duplicates","36 ATP per glucose, including downstream oxidative phosphorylation"],"correct":0,"why":"Glycolysis: 2 ATP invested in priming, 4 ATP produced in payoff = NET 2 ATP. Two NAD+ reduced to 2 NADH. One glucose → 2 pyruvate. The 36-38 ATP figure includes all of cellular respiration (Krebs + ETC), not glycolysis alone.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm119","section":"BIO","topic":"cell-types","difficulty":2,"q":"A clinical microbiology laboratory uses Gram staining to differentiate two major bacterial groups. Gram-positive bacteria stain purple/violet, while Gram-negative bacteria appear pink after counterstaining. The difference reflects fundamental cell-wall architecture. What is the structural distinction?","opts":["Different DNA structure (linear vs circular)","Gram-positive: thick peptidoglycan wall, no outer membrane. Gram-negative: thin peptidoglycan + outer membrane with lipopolysaccharide (LPS, which is endotoxic)","Different ribosome size — 70S in one, 80S in the other","Different plasma membrane composition (cholesterol vs no cholesterol)","Both have identical cell walls but different cytoplasmic pH"],"correct":1,"why":"Gram-positive bacteria have a single thick peptidoglycan cell wall (retains crystal violet → purple). Gram-negative bacteria have a thin peptidoglycan layer between an inner plasma membrane and an outer membrane containing LPS (lose crystal violet → counterstain pink). LPS is the endotoxin responsible for septic shock; the outer membrane also makes Gram-negative bacteria more antibiotic-resistant.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm112","section":"BIO","topic":"repair","difficulty":2,"q":"Just after replication, occasional mismatched bases (A paired with C, for example) escape the polymerase's proofreading. A specialized DNA repair pathway then identifies the newly synthesized strand (which contains the error), excises a short patch around the mismatch, and resynthesizes correctly. Which type of damage does mismatch repair primarily fix?","opts":["UV-induced thymine dimers, removed by nucleotide excision repair","Base mispairs that escape proofreading immediately after DNA replication","Double-strand breaks, repaired by homologous recombination or non-homologous end joining","DNA methylation marks, which are not damage at all","Crosslinks between adjacent strands"],"correct":1,"why":"Mismatch repair targets base-base mispairs and small insertion/deletion loops left after DNA replication. In E. coli, MutS recognizes the mismatch, MutL/MutH cleave the unmethylated (newly synthesized) strand, and a polymerase + ligase complete the repair. Inherited mutations in MMR genes (MSH2, MLH1) cause Lynch syndrome (hereditary colon cancer).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm092","section":"BIO","topic":"photo","difficulty":2,"q":"Hot, sunny climates increase the oxygenase activity of RuBisCO (photorespiration) which wastes carbon and energy. C4 plants such as corn and sugarcane have evolved a specialized anatomy and biochemistry to minimize this. How do C4 plants suppress photorespiration?","opts":["By directly inhibiting RuBisCO with a small-molecule inhibitor","By spatially separating CO₂ capture (by PEP carboxylase in mesophyll cells) from CO₂ fixation (by RuBisCO in bundle-sheath cells), where local CO₂ is concentrated and outcompetes O₂","By closing stomata permanently to exclude O₂","By performing photosynthesis only at night (CAM strategy)","By abandoning the Calvin cycle entirely"],"correct":1,"why":"C4 plants use PEP carboxylase (no O₂ side reaction) in mesophyll cells to fix CO₂ into oxaloacetate. This 4-carbon molecule travels to the bundle-sheath cells, where decarboxylation releases CO₂ at high local concentration around RuBisCO. The high CO₂:O₂ ratio suppresses RuBisCO's oxygenase activity, minimizing photorespiration.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm063","section":"BIO","topic":"nucleic","difficulty":2,"q":"Nitrogenous bases in DNA are categorized into two structural families based on ring count. Pyrimidines (cytosine, thymine, uracil) have a single six-membered ring, while purines have a fused two-ring system (a six-membered + a five-membered ring). Which of the following is a PURINE?","opts":["Cytosine — a single-ring pyrimidine","Thymine — a single-ring pyrimidine found only in DNA","Adenine — a fused two-ring purine that pairs with thymine via two H-bonds","Uracil — a single-ring pyrimidine found in RNA","Inosine — a modified single-ring base"],"correct":2,"why":"Purines (Adenine and Guanine) have two fused rings; pyrimidines (Cytosine, Thymine, Uracil) have one ring. Mnemonic: 'Pure As Gold' (purines = A, G); 'CUT the Py' (pyrimidines = C, U, T). The size mismatch is why purine-pyrimidine pairing keeps the DNA helix uniform in width.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm102","section":"BIO","topic":"transcription","difficulty":2,"q":"Approximately 25-35 nucleotides upstream of the transcription start site, many eukaryotic protein-coding genes contain a conserved sequence rich in adenine and thymine. The TATA-binding protein (TBP, part of TFIID) recognizes this sequence to position RNA polymerase II. Where is the TATA box located relative to the gene?","opts":["Within the coding sequence, in the middle of an exon","In the core promoter, upstream of the transcription start site, where it positions the pre-initiation complex","In the terminator, where transcription stops","Only in the 5' untranslated region of the mature mRNA","In an enhancer thousands of base pairs away from the gene"],"correct":1,"why":"The TATA box is a core-promoter element ~25 bp upstream of the transcription start site. TBP binds the AT-rich sequence and bends the DNA, recruiting other general transcription factors and RNA Pol II. Not all genes have a TATA box; some use alternative core elements (e.g., TATA-less CpG-island promoters).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm058","section":"BIO","topic":"lipids","difficulty":2,"q":"Animal cell membranes maintain consistent fluidity across a wide temperature range. At high temperatures, the membrane resists becoming too fluid; at low temperatures, it resists becoming too rigid. One specific lipid component sits between phospholipids and contributes both effects. Which lipid is this?","opts":["Phospholipid (phosphatidylcholine), the main bilayer lipid","Cholesterol, which buffers membrane fluidity at both temperature extremes","Glycoprotein, which provides cell-cell recognition","Free fatty acid, the building block of triglycerides","Sphingomyelin, found primarily in lipid rafts"],"correct":1,"why":"Cholesterol intercalates between phospholipids and acts as a fluidity buffer: at high temperatures it restrains lipid movement (less fluid), at low temperatures it disrupts ordered packing (more fluid). Without cholesterol, animal membranes would solidify in cold or melt in heat.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm098","section":"BIO","topic":"replication","difficulty":2,"q":"Telomeres are repetitive DNA sequences (TTAGGG in vertebrates) at chromosome ends that shorten slightly with each replication cycle. Cells with long-term proliferative potential maintain telomere length using a specific reverse-transcriptase enzyme that carries its own RNA template. In which cell types is this telomerase enzyme MOST highly expressed?","opts":["Differentiated somatic cells — stable, fully matured tissues","Stem cells, germline cells, and ~90% of cancer cells — populations that need long-term proliferation","Aging tissues approaching senescence","Bacteria, which use telomerase to seal circular chromosomes","Red blood cells, which have no nucleus"],"correct":1,"why":"Telomerase is highly expressed in stem cells (for self-renewal), germline cells (transmitting full-length telomeres to offspring), and most cancer cells (~90%, which need to bypass replicative senescence). Most differentiated somatic cells repress telomerase, so telomeres shorten with each division — eventually triggering senescence (Hayflick limit).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm048","section":"BIO","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"A cell biologist wants to visualize the internal architecture of a mitochondrion at a resolution sufficient to count individual cristae and resolve ribosomes within the matrix. Which microscopy technique provides the highest resolution for visualizing INTERNAL ultrastructure of a sectioned organelle?","opts":["Light (compound) microscopy — limited to ~200 nm resolution","Fluorescence microscopy — useful for localizing specific molecules but not for ultrastructure","Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) — best for surface topology of whole specimens","Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) — passes electrons through ultrathin sections to image internal structure at < 1 nm resolution","Phase contrast microscopy — visualizes living cells but at light-microscopy resolution"],"correct":3,"why":"TEM passes a beam of electrons through ultrathin sections and is the best technique for resolving INTERNAL ultrastructure (cristae, ribosomes, membranes) at sub-nanometer resolution. SEM scans a surface and shows topology of intact specimens. Light/fluorescence/phase-contrast are all limited by the diffraction limit of visible light (~200 nm).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm074","section":"BIO","topic":"organelles","difficulty":2,"q":"Animal cells contain a microtubule-organizing center (MTOC) located near the nucleus. During cell division, this structure organizes the mitotic spindle. It contains a pair of perpendicular cylindrical bodies, each composed of nine triplets of microtubules. Where are these centrioles found?","opts":["Inside mitochondria, alongside the matrix","Within the centrosome, the cell's main microtubule-organizing center","Inside the lysosome, where they regulate hydrolysis","Inside the nucleus, attached to the nucleolus","Free in the cytoplasm with no organizing structure"],"correct":1,"why":"Centrioles are paired cylindrical structures in the centrosome — the primary MTOC of animal cells. The centrosome nucleates microtubules and during mitosis duplicates and migrates to opposite poles to organize the spindle. Plant cells have MTOCs but lack discrete centrioles.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm095","section":"BIO","topic":"mitosis","difficulty":2,"q":"During mitosis, replicated chromosomes (each consisting of two sister chromatids) undergo a precisely orchestrated series of movements directed by the spindle apparatus. There is a specific phase in which all chromosomes are aligned along the equator of the cell, with each centromere attached to spindle fibers from BOTH poles. Which phase is this?","opts":["Prophase, when chromosomes condense and the nuclear envelope dissolves","Metaphase, when chromosomes align at the metaphase plate (cell equator), each attached to spindle fibers from both poles via kinetochores","Anaphase, when sister chromatids separate and migrate to opposite poles","Telophase, when nuclear envelopes reform around the daughter nuclei","Cytokinesis, when the cytoplasm physically divides"],"correct":1,"why":"Metaphase is defined by chromosome alignment at the metaphase plate. Each sister chromatid pair attaches to spindle fibers from both poles (bi-orientation). Only when all chromosomes are bi-oriented and tension is sensed does the spindle assembly checkpoint allow progression to anaphase.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm053","section":"BIO","topic":"biomolecules","difficulty":2,"q":"When ranking common intermolecular forces from strongest to weakest among biological systems, four major types are typically considered: ion-dipole, hydrogen bonds, dipole-dipole, and London dispersion forces. Which is the STRONGEST of these intermolecular forces?","opts":["Hydrogen bond (a strong dipole-dipole between H attached to N/O/F and another N/O/F)","London dispersion (instantaneous fluctuations in electron density)","Ion-dipole, which involves a full ionic charge interacting with a dipole","Dipole-dipole, between permanent dipoles in neutral molecules","Van der Waals (a synonym for London dispersion forces)"],"correct":2,"why":"Among intermolecular forces, ion-dipole is strongest because a full ionic charge generates much stronger electrostatic attraction with a dipole than mere partial charges do. The order from strongest to weakest: ion-ion > ion-dipole > H-bond > dipole-dipole > London dispersion.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm144","section":"BIO","topic":"enzymes","difficulty":2,"q":"On a Lineweaver-Burk (double-reciprocal) plot, a non-competitive inhibitor produces a characteristic graphical signature compared to the uninhibited reaction. Specifically, the y-intercept changes (since Vmax is reduced) but the x-intercept does not (since Km is unchanged). Which axis intercept changes for a non-competitive inhibitor?","opts":["Same y-intercept, different x-intercept (this is the competitive pattern)","Different y-intercept, same x-intercept (non-competitive: 1/Vmax increases since Vmax decreased; 1/Km unchanged)","Both intercepts change (uncompetitive pattern)","Neither intercept changes (no inhibition)","The slope is unchanged but both intercepts shift"],"correct":1,"why":"Non-competitive inhibitor: Vmax decreases (so 1/Vmax = y-intercept goes up), Km unchanged (so 1/Km = x-intercept unchanged). Lines intersect on the x-axis. Competitive: Vmax unchanged (same y-intercept), Km increases (different x-intercept). Uncompetitive: both Vmax and Km decrease (parallel lines).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm024","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"A neuron expends roughly 70% of its ATP budget maintaining a steep electrochemical gradient across its plasma membrane: 3 Na+ ions are pumped out and 2 K+ ions are pumped in for each ATP hydrolyzed. Which transport process is being described — and which is the only one that DIRECTLY requires ATP?","opts":["Simple diffusion of Na+ and K+ down their gradients","Facilitated diffusion through a Na+/K+ co-transporter","Osmosis driven by water concentration","The Na+/K+-ATPase, primary active transport that directly hydrolyzes ATP","Endocytosis of Na+ and K+ in transport vesicles"],"correct":3,"why":"The Na+/K+-ATPase (sodium-potassium pump) is a primary active transporter that directly couples ATP hydrolysis to ion movement against electrochemical gradients (3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in per ATP). Simple/facilitated diffusion and osmosis are passive (no ATP). Secondary active transport uses gradients, not ATP directly.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm127","section":"BIO","topic":"krebs","difficulty":2,"q":"Pyruvate produced by glycolysis enters the mitochondrial matrix and is converted to acetyl-CoA by a large multienzyme complex (pyruvate dehydrogenase, PDH). This step links glycolysis to the citric acid cycle and is considered functionally irreversible. What does PDH catalyze?","opts":["Pyruvate → lactate (cytoplasmic, anaerobic)","Pyruvate + CoA + NAD+ → acetyl-CoA + CO₂ + NADH (oxidative decarboxylation in the mitochondrial matrix)","Pyruvate → glucose (gluconeogenesis, multistep, requires energy)","Acetyl-CoA → citrate (this is citrate synthase, a Krebs enzyme)","Pyruvate → alanine (transamination)"],"correct":1,"why":"PDH catalyzes oxidative decarboxylation: pyruvate is decarboxylated (loses CO₂), the remaining 2-carbon acetyl group is attached to CoA-SH, and NAD+ is reduced to NADH. The reaction is exergonic and irreversible, locking pyruvate carbons into the Krebs cycle. PDH activity is heavily regulated by phosphorylation (PDK inactivates, PDP activates).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm125","section":"BIO","topic":"signaling","difficulty":2,"q":"Protein kinases add phosphate groups to specific serine, threonine, or tyrosine residues, often turning a target enzyme on or off. The reverse reaction is catalyzed by a different family of enzymes that physiologically opposes kinase action. Which enzymes remove phosphate groups from proteins?","opts":["Acetyltransferases (HATs), which transfer acetyl groups to lysines","Methyltransferases, which add methyl groups to histones","Protein phosphatases, which hydrolyze phosphoester bonds and remove phosphate groups (e.g., PP1, PP2A, PTP1B)","Glycosyltransferases, which add sugars to proteins","Sumoylation enzymes, which add SUMO to lysines"],"correct":2,"why":"Protein phosphatases reverse the action of kinases by hydrolyzing the phosphoester bond. They are equally critical to cellular signaling — kinase activation must be reversible to give the cell a switch rather than a stuck-on state. The major families are Ser/Thr phosphatases (PP1, PP2A, calcineurin) and tyrosine phosphatases (PTP1B, SHP2).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm077","section":"BIO","topic":"cytoskeleton","difficulty":2,"q":"The thinnest filaments of the cytoskeleton (~7 nm in diameter) form a dynamic network beneath the plasma membrane and drive amoeboid cell motility, contraction in muscle (paired with myosin), and the cleavage furrow during cytokinesis. Which protein polymerizes into these filaments?","opts":["α/β-tubulin, the building block of microtubules","Actin (G-actin monomers polymerize into F-actin filaments)","Keratin, an intermediate filament protein","Lamin, found primarily in the nuclear lamina","Myosin, a motor protein that walks along the filaments"],"correct":1,"why":"Microfilaments are polymers of actin: G-actin (globular) monomers polymerize into F-actin (filamentous) double helices ~7 nm in diameter. Myosin is the motor protein that walks along actin (e.g., in muscle contraction). Tubulin makes microtubules; keratin/lamin make intermediate filaments.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm062","section":"BIO","topic":"proteins","difficulty":2,"q":"Insulin is secreted by pancreatic β-cells in response to elevated blood glucose. It consists of two short polypeptide chains (A: 21 residues, B: 30 residues) linked by disulfide bonds. After binding its membrane receptor (a receptor tyrosine kinase), insulin triggers GLUT4 mobilization. To which class of hormone does insulin belong?","opts":["Steroid hormone (e.g., cortisol)","Peptide (protein) hormone, derived from a polypeptide precursor","Amine hormone derived from tyrosine (e.g., epinephrine)","Eicosanoid (e.g., prostaglandin)","Thyroid hormone, an iodinated tyrosine derivative"],"correct":1,"why":"Insulin is a 51-amino-acid peptide hormone, processed from preproinsulin → proinsulin → mature insulin. Like other peptide hormones, it cannot cross the lipid bilayer and binds cell-surface receptors (insulin receptor is an RTK), triggering intracellular signaling. Steroid and thyroid hormones are lipophilic and cross membranes.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm085","section":"BIO","topic":"glycolysis","difficulty":2,"q":"During intense exercise, skeletal muscle outpaces oxygen delivery. Glycolysis continues at high rate, producing pyruvate and NADH, but mitochondrial respiration cannot keep up. Lactate fermentation kicks in: pyruvate is reduced to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase. What is the primary purpose of this conversion?","opts":["To generate additional ATP from pyruvate","To regenerate NAD+ from NADH so glycolysis can continue (otherwise NAD+ would be depleted, halting the GAPDH step)","To produce pyruvate from amino acids during fasting","To convert lactate to glucose (gluconeogenesis)","To generate CO₂ for buffering"],"correct":1,"why":"Glycolysis depletes NAD+ rapidly at the GAPDH step (NAD+ → NADH). Without aerobic respiration to reoxidize NADH, glycolysis would halt within seconds. Lactate fermentation regenerates NAD+ by transferring electrons from NADH to pyruvate, allowing glycolysis to continue producing 2 ATP per glucose anaerobically.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm017","section":"BIO","topic":"cell-types","difficulty":2,"q":"A microscopist examines two cell types and finds that one has a clearly defined membrane-bound nucleus while the other has DNA distributed in a region called the nucleoid but lacks any internal compartmentalization. Which of the following is uniquely characteristic of EUKARYOTIC cells but absent in PROKARYOTIC cells?","opts":["Ribosomes for protein synthesis","A plasma membrane separating cytoplasm from environment","A membrane-bound nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles (mitochondria, ER, Golgi)","DNA as the genetic material","Cytoplasm containing dissolved proteins and metabolites"],"correct":2,"why":"Both eukaryotes and prokaryotes have ribosomes, plasma membranes, DNA, and cytoplasm. The defining hallmark of eukaryotes is internal compartmentalization — DNA is sequestered in a membrane-bound nucleus, and other reactions occur in mitochondria, ER, and Golgi. Prokaryotes have neither.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm021","section":"BIO","topic":"organelles","difficulty":2,"q":"A hepatologist examines liver biopsies from two patients. Patient A is a chronic alcohol user; patient B has no history of substance use. Compared to patient B, patient A's hepatocytes show dramatic enlargement of one organelle that contains the cytochrome P450 family of enzymes. Which organelle is enlarged?","opts":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum, where cytochrome P450 enzymes detoxify alcohol and drugs","Nucleolus, which expands during ribosome biosynthesis","Lysosome, the site of intracellular hydrolytic digestion","Mitochondrion, where ATP is generated"],"correct":1,"why":"Smooth ER is the site of lipid biosynthesis and drug/toxin detoxification via the cytochrome P450 enzyme system. Chronic alcohol exposure induces hepatocyte smooth-ER expansion (and corresponding tolerance) as the cell upregulates its detoxification machinery. Rough ER (with ribosomes) handles protein synthesis.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm038","section":"BIO","topic":"mitosis","difficulty":2,"q":"A primary cell-cycle checkpoint integrates extracellular growth signals, nutrient availability, cell size, and DNA damage. If conditions are unfavorable, the cell exits the cycle and enters G0 rather than committing to DNA synthesis. Which checkpoint is this?","opts":["Spindle assembly checkpoint, monitoring kinetochore attachment","G1/S restriction point, where the cell decides to commit to DNA replication","M-phase metaphase-anaphase transition, monitoring chromatid separation","Cytokinesis checkpoint, monitoring contractile ring formation","S-phase damage checkpoint, monitoring stalled replication forks"],"correct":1,"why":"The G1/S checkpoint (also called the restriction point) is the major decision: enough nutrients, growth factors, and intact DNA → enter S and commit to DNA synthesis; otherwise the cell enters G0. Loss of this checkpoint (e.g., loss of Rb tumor suppressor) is a hallmark of many cancers.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm054","section":"BIO","topic":"biomolecules","difficulty":2,"q":"A peptide bond between two amino acids in a polypeptide is a covalent linkage formed by dehydration synthesis. To cleave this bond and recover the two free amino acids, an enzyme must drive the reverse reaction. What does hydrolysis of a peptide bond require?","opts":["Release of one water molecule, the same as in dehydration synthesis","Addition of one water molecule across the C-N bond — the OH joins the carbonyl carbon and the H joins the amide nitrogen","Neither addition nor release of water; it is purely thermal cleavage","Removal of one CO₂ molecule, similar to a decarboxylation","Addition of one molecule of NH₃ across the bond"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydrolysis is the reverse of dehydration synthesis: a water molecule is split across the C-N peptide bond, regenerating a free carboxyl on one amino acid and a free amino on the other. Proteases (e.g., trypsin) catalyze this in proteins; lysosomal hydrolases do this in cells.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm016","section":"BIO","topic":"enzymes","difficulty":2,"q":"Catalase converts hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen in roughly 10^-7 seconds — about a million times faster than the uncatalyzed reaction. Despite this dramatic acceleration, the equilibrium constant of the reaction is unchanged. Which statement best describes how catalase (and enzymes generally) achieves this?","opts":["Enzymes raise the activation energy and increase ΔG of the reaction","Enzymes shift the equilibrium of the reaction toward products by changing ΔG°","Enzymes lower the activation energy of the reaction without changing ΔG° or the equilibrium constant","Enzymes are consumed during the reaction and must be replaced","Enzymes increase the temperature of the system, accelerating molecular collisions"],"correct":2,"why":"Enzymes accelerate reactions by lowering the activation energy (Ea), making the transition state more accessible. They do not change ΔG° (which depends only on initial and final states), so equilibrium and Keq are unaffected. Enzymes are not consumed and can catalyze many reaction cycles.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm025","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"Glucose enters most human cells via GLUT family transporters. The transporter binds glucose on the outside, undergoes a conformational change, and releases glucose on the inside, all without consuming ATP. Glucose movement always proceeds down its concentration gradient. Which transport mechanism is this?","opts":["Primary active transport, since a membrane protein is involved","Facilitated diffusion — passive movement through a carrier protein down the concentration gradient, no ATP required","Osmosis, since water often follows glucose","Endocytosis, with glucose engulfed in a vesicle","Secondary active transport powered by the Na+ gradient"],"correct":1,"why":"GLUT transporters use facilitated diffusion: a membrane protein provides a hydrophilic channel for glucose to cross down its gradient, with no ATP involvement. (SGLT transporters in intestine and kidney use secondary active transport — they couple glucose to the Na+ gradient — but GLUT is purely passive.)","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm030","section":"BIO","topic":"krebs","difficulty":2,"q":"In aerobic cellular respiration, electrons donated by NADH and FADH2 flow through a series of protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane (the electron transport chain). After moving through Complexes I-IV, the electrons are passed to a final acceptor that is reduced to water. Which molecule is this final electron acceptor?","opts":["NAD+ — recycled back into the citric acid cycle","FAD — recycled back to Complex II","Molecular oxygen (O2), reduced to water at Complex IV","Pyruvate, the end product of glycolysis","Coenzyme Q (ubiquinone), which shuttles electrons between Complexes"],"correct":2,"why":"Molecular oxygen is the terminal electron acceptor of the ETC. At Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase), oxygen accepts four electrons and four protons to form 2 H2O. This is why aerobic respiration requires oxygen — without it, the ETC backs up, NADH/FADH2 cannot be reoxidized, and oxidative phosphorylation halts.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm082","section":"BIO","topic":"signaling","difficulty":2,"q":"Growth-factor binding to a receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) like the EGF receptor causes receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation of cytoplasmic tyrosines. Adapter proteins (Grb2-Sos) recruited to phosphorylated tyrosines then activate a small GTPase, which activates a downstream three-tier kinase cascade. What is this cascade?","opts":["Adenylyl cyclase generation of cAMP","Phospholipase C hydrolysis of PIP₂ → IP₃ + DAG","The Ras-Raf-MEK-ERK MAPK cascade, ultimately driving gene transcription and cell-cycle progression","Direct gene transcription by the receptor itself","JAK-STAT activation, used by cytokine receptors"],"correct":2,"why":"RTK activation typically triggers the MAPK cascade: Ras (small GTPase activated by Sos) → Raf (a kinase) → MEK (another kinase) → ERK (the terminal MAPK), which translocates to the nucleus and phosphorylates transcription factors. This pathway drives proliferation; mutations in Ras (e.g., K-RasG12D) are common oncogenic drivers.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm020","section":"BIO","topic":"organelles","difficulty":2,"q":"A pancreatic β cell is secreting insulin. The newly translated insulin precursor passes through the rough ER, undergoes proper folding, and arrives in the next organelle for final modification — including glycosylation, sulfation, and sorting into transport vesicles destined for the plasma membrane. Which organelle handles this final processing and sorting step?","opts":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Golgi apparatus","Lysosome","Peroxisome"],"correct":2,"why":"The Golgi apparatus is the final processing and sorting hub for secreted proteins. It modifies proteins (glycosylation, sulfation, phosphorylation), packages them into transport vesicles, and directs vesicles to their destinations (plasma membrane, lysosomes, etc.). The rough ER handles initial synthesis and folding.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm096","section":"BIO","topic":"mitosis","difficulty":2,"q":"A transcription factor encoded by the TP53 gene is upregulated in response to DNA damage. It activates downstream genes that arrest the cell cycle (e.g., p21, a CDK inhibitor) or trigger apoptosis if the damage cannot be repaired. Mutation of this gene is found in over half of human cancers. By what nickname is this protein known?","opts":["A cyclin, the regulatory subunit of CDK complexes","A CDK (cyclin-dependent kinase), the catalytic subunit of cell-cycle drivers","The 'guardian of the genome' — p53, a tumor suppressor that arrests the cell cycle or triggers apoptosis in response to DNA damage","A histone, the protein that packages DNA","A G-protein-coupled receptor"],"correct":2,"why":"p53 (TP53 protein) is famously called the 'guardian of the genome.' It is activated by DNA damage and other stresses; downstream, it transcribes p21 (a CDK inhibitor that arrests the cell cycle at G1/S) and apoptosis genes (BAX, PUMA). Loss of p53 function is one of the most common alterations in human cancers.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm003","section":"BIO","topic":"biomolecules","difficulty":2,"q":"Two glucose molecules combine to form maltose. The reaction releases one molecule of water. This same chemical principle is repeated when many glucose units link to form glycogen or cellulose. What general type of reaction is this?","opts":["Dehydration synthesis (condensation), in which a covalent bond forms between two monomers and water is released","Hydrolysis, in which a polymer is cleaved by addition of water across the bond","Oxidation, where electrons are transferred to NAD+ during bond formation","Phosphorylation, where a phosphate group is added to one of the sugar carbons","Saponification, where alkaline conditions cleave an ester bond"],"correct":0,"why":"Polymerization of monosaccharides into disaccharides or polysaccharides is a dehydration synthesis: the OH from one sugar and H from the other combine to form H2O, leaving a glycosidic bond between the sugars. Hydrolysis is the reverse — splitting a polymer with water.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm142","section":"BIO","topic":"organelles","difficulty":2,"q":"Tay-Sachs disease is caused by a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in HEXA, encoding the lysosomal enzyme β-hexosaminidase A. As a result, the lipid GM2-ganglioside accumulates in lysosomes (especially in neurons), causing developmental regression and early death. What general type of disorder does this represent?","opts":["Lysosomal storage disease — missing a single hydrolase, so its substrate accumulates progressively in lysosomes, distending them and disrupting cell function","A disease caused by too many lysosomes per cell","A pH imbalance disorder, where lysosomes are no longer acidic","A membrane defect causing lysosomes to leak hydrolases into the cytoplasm","A metabolic disease unrelated to lysosomes"],"correct":0,"why":"Lysosomal storage diseases (Tay-Sachs, Gaucher, Fabry, Niemann-Pick, Hurler) are caused by single-hydrolase deficiencies. The undegraded substrate accumulates inside lysosomes, swelling them and disrupting cell function. Tissues with high turnover of the affected substrate (often neurons, given their lipid-rich membranes) are most affected. Treatments include enzyme replacement therapy and substrate reduction.","_rewritten":true}],"gc":[{"id":"GC_p6","section":"GC","topic":"periodic","difficulty":1,"q":"Period 2 atoms N (Z=7), O (Z=8), F (Z=9), and Ne (Z=10) are compared in their ground states at 298 K. As Zeff increases left to right across a period, atomic radius shrinks. Which of these has the SMALLEST atomic (covalent) radius?","opts":["Nitrogen","Oxygen","Fluorine","Neon","All four are equal"],"correct":3,"why":"Across period 2, increasing Zeff pulls electrons closer, so radius decreases. Ne, at the far right, has the highest Zeff and the smallest radius among these. Note: Ne's atomic radius is van der Waals-defined, but for periodic-trend questions it is treated as smallest in period 2.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_if1","section":"GC","topic":"imf-rank","difficulty":1,"q":"Liquid water at 25°C has an unusually high boiling point (100°C) and high heat capacity (4.18 J/g·°C) for such a small, light molecule (MW = 18 g/mol). Considering the O-H bonds and the lone pairs on oxygen, what is the dominant intermolecular force between two H₂O molecules?","opts":["London dispersion only","Dipole-dipole only","Hydrogen bonding","Ionic attraction","Ion-dipole attraction"],"correct":2,"why":"Hydrogen bonding occurs when H is covalently bound to N, O, or F and interacts with a lone pair on another N/O/F atom. Each water molecule can donate 2 H-bonds and accept 2, forming an extensive 3D network — this gives water its anomalously high boiling point relative to H₂S, H₂Se, or H₂Te.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_qn1","section":"GC","topic":"quantum-numbers","difficulty":1,"q":"A transition-metal cation has an unpaired electron occupying a 3d orbital, characterized by principal quantum number n = 3 and angular momentum quantum number l = 2. Magnetic quantum number m_l ranges from -l to +l in integer steps. How many distinct m_l values (and therefore degenerate orbitals at the same energy) does the 3d subshell contain?","opts":["3","5","7","9","10"],"correct":1,"why":"The number of m_l values per subshell equals 2l + 1, so the d subshell (l = 2) has 2(2) + 1 = 5 orbitals. These five orbitals are degenerate in a free atom and split in a ligand field.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_gl-e6","section":"GC","topic":"gas-laws","difficulty":2,"q":"An ideal gas occupies 11.2 L at a pressure of 1.0 atm and temperature of 273 K (STP), where R = 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K). Using the ideal-gas equation PV = nRT to determine the chemical amount in the sample, how many moles of gas are present?","opts":["0.25 mol","0.50 mol","1.0 mol","1.5 mol","2.0 mol"],"correct":1,"why":"n = PV/RT = (1.0)(11.2)/(0.0821 x 273) = 0.50 mol. Equivalently, 11.2 L at STP = half of 22.4 L (one molar volume), so n = 0.5 mol.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_at-h2","section":"GC","topic":"atomic","difficulty":3,"q":"Chromium (Z = 24) is one of two well-known first-row transition metals (the other being Cu) that violate the Aufbau rule. Instead of the predicted [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴ configuration, its actual ground-state configuration places only one electron in the 4s orbital. What is the correct ground-state electron configuration of neutral Cr?","opts":["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁴","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁵","[Ar] 4s⁰ 3d⁶","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁴"],"correct":1,"why":"A half-filled 3d⁵ subshell has extra exchange-energy stability, so promoting one 4s electron to 3d gives the lower-energy configuration [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵. This makes Cr's six valence electrons all unpaired (one in 4s, five in 3d).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lc6","section":"GC","topic":"eq-lechat","difficulty":3,"q":"The contact-process equilibrium 2 SO2(g) + O2(g) ⇌ 2 SO3(g) is exothermic (ΔH = -198 kJ) and decreases moles of gas from 3 to 2. To MAXIMIZE the equilibrium yield of SO3 (while ignoring kinetic considerations), which combination of changes should be applied?","opts":["Raise T, raise P","Lower T, raise P","Raise T, lower P","Lower T, lower P","Add inert gas at constant V"],"correct":1,"why":"Exothermic forward direction is favored by lowering T (Le Chatelier removes heat). Δn_gas = -1, so increasing P pushes the system toward fewer moles of gas (forward). Combined: low T + high P maximizes SO3 yield (industrially limited by kinetics, hence the V2O5 catalyst at ~700 K).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_mc-q2","section":"GC","topic":"mole-conv","difficulty":2,"q":"A combustion analysis shows a hydrocarbon is 80.0% carbon and 20.0% hydrogen by mass. Assume a 100.0 g sample, then convert each mass to moles using M(C) = 12 g/mol and M(H) = 1 g/mol. After dividing both by the smaller number, what is the empirical formula?","opts":["CH","CH2","CH3","CH4","C2H6"],"correct":2,"why":"Moles C = 80/12 = 6.67; moles H = 20/1 = 20. Divide both by 6.67: C = 1, H = 3. Empirical formula = CH3 (the molecular formula could be C2H6 ethane, but the question asks for empirical).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_rl5","section":"GC","topic":"rate-laws","difficulty":2,"q":"A second-order reaction has rate law rate = k[A]² with rate measured in M/s and concentration in M. Solving for k's units: k = rate/[A]² = (M/s)/M². What are the units of k for a second-order rate law?","opts":["s⁻¹","M·s⁻¹","M⁻¹·s⁻¹","M⁻²·s⁻¹","unitless"],"correct":2,"why":"k = (M·s⁻¹)/(M²) = M⁻¹·s⁻¹ for 2nd order. General rule: units of k = M^(1−n)·s⁻¹ where n is reaction order. So 0-order: M·s⁻¹; 1st: s⁻¹; 2nd: M⁻¹·s⁻¹; 3rd: M⁻²·s⁻¹.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_el1","section":"GC","topic":"electrolysis","difficulty":2,"q":"An electroplating cell contains 0.50 M CuSO4(aq) and runs at a constant current of 1.00 A for 1.00 hour at 25°C. The cathode half-reaction is Cu^2+ + 2e- -> Cu(s). Using F = 96485 C/mol e- and M(Cu) = 63.5 g/mol, calculate the mass of copper deposited on the cathode.","opts":["0.59 g","1.18 g","2.37 g","118 g","0.30 g"],"correct":1,"why":"Charge Q = It = (1.00 A)(3600 s) = 3600 C. Moles of e- = 3600/96485 = 0.0373 mol; moles of Cu = 0.0373/2 = 0.01866 mol; mass = 0.01866 x 63.5 ~ 1.18 g. Forgetting to divide by n = 2 gives the doubled distractor 2.37 g.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_cal-q1","section":"GC","topic":"calorimetry","difficulty":2,"q":"A 250.0 g aluminum block (specific heat c = 0.90 J/(g·K)) is removed from a 100.0°C bath and allowed to cool to 25.0°C in a calorimeter. Apply q = m·c·ΔT (using ΔT = T_final - T_initial) to determine the heat lost by the aluminum.","opts":["8.4 kJ","16.9 kJ","22.5 kJ","33.8 kJ","75 kJ"],"correct":1,"why":"ΔT = 25 - 100 = -75 K. q = m·c·ΔT = 250 x 0.90 x (-75) = -16,875 J ~ -16.9 kJ. The aluminum loses 16.9 kJ; the magnitude (without sign) is 16.9 kJ.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lw-e8","section":"GC","topic":"lewis","difficulty":2,"q":"Boron trifluoride (BF₃) is a Lewis acid because boron's three valence electrons form three B-F bonds, leaving boron with only 6 electrons in its valence shell rather than the usual octet. How many electrons does the central boron have around it in this structure?","opts":["4","6","8","10","12"],"correct":1,"why":"B forms 3 B-F bonds (3 × 2 e⁻ = 6 bonding electrons around B) and has 0 lone pairs. Total around B = 6 electrons (sextet, not an octet). The empty p-orbital on B is what allows BF₃ to accept an electron pair from a Lewis base.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_sf-q3","section":"GC","topic":"sig-figs","difficulty":2,"q":"A multistep calculation reports (12.0 x 0.30)/6.00. The calculator output is 0.6. Apply the multiplication/division rule: the answer keeps the smallest number of significant figures among the values used. Which value should be reported?","opts":["0.6","0.60","0.600","0.6000","0.60000"],"correct":1,"why":"Sig figs: 12.0 has 3, 0.30 has 2, 6.00 has 3. The minimum is 2, so the result keeps 2 sig figs. (12.0 x 0.30)/6.00 = 0.60.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ar-e3","section":"GC","topic":"arrhenius","difficulty":2,"q":"Two reactions A and B share the same pre-exponential factor A and are studied at the same temperature 298 K, but reaction A has E_a = 50 kJ/mol while reaction B has E_a = 100 kJ/mol. Using k = A·exp(−E_a/RT), how does k for reaction B compare with k for reaction A?","opts":["k_B is larger","k_B is the same","k_B is smaller","k_B is double","k_B is independent of E_a"],"correct":2,"why":"Higher E_a makes −E_a/(RT) more negative, so exp(−E_a/RT) is much smaller. Therefore k_B (higher E_a) is smaller than k_A at the same temperature.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lr1","section":"GC","topic":"lim-react","difficulty":2,"q":"For the Haber process N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g), 28.0 g of N₂ (molar mass 28.0 g/mol) is mixed with 9.0 g of H₂ (molar mass 2.0 g/mol) at 500 K. Convert each to moles and compare to the 1:3 stoichiometric ratio. Which is the limiting reactant?","opts":["N₂","H₂","Both consumed equally","Cannot be determined without K","Neither is limiting"],"correct":0,"why":"n(N₂) = 28/28 = 1.0 mol; n(H₂) = 9/2 = 4.5 mol. Stoichiometry needs 3 mol H₂ per 1 mol N₂, so 1.0 mol N₂ requires only 3.0 mol H₂. Excess H₂ is present, so N₂ runs out first and is limiting.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_v-q2","section":"GC","topic":"vsepr","difficulty":2,"q":"Four molecules with central atoms of differing geometries are compared: CH4 (109.5°), NH3 (107°), H2O (104.5°), and BF3 (120°). Lone-pair to bonding-pair repulsions reduce the ideal geometry's bond angles in NH3 and H2O. Which molecule has the LARGEST X-Y-X bond angle?","opts":["CH4","NH3","H2O","BF3","All equal at 109.5°"],"correct":3,"why":"BF3 is AX3 (trigonal planar, no lone pairs on B), so F-B-F = 120°, the largest among these. CH4 (tetrahedral) is 109.5°, while NH3 (107°) and H2O (104.5°) are compressed by lone pairs.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_xp012","section":"GC","topic":"liquids","difficulty":3,"q":"Two liquids X (BP 60 °C, polar) and Y (BP 80 °C, non-polar) are mixed. Which deviation from Raoult's law is most likely, and how does this affect the boiling point of the mixture vs. ideal behavior?","opts":["Positive deviation; the mixture has a LOWER boiling point than predicted by Raoult's law because polar/nonpolar molecules disrupt favorable interactions in the pure liquids","Negative deviation; mixture boils higher than ideal","No deviation; polar/nonpolar mix ideally","Both deviations cancel; the boiling point matches ideal exactly","The system is azeotropic at 1:1 ratio"],"correct":0,"why":"Polar + non-polar mixtures show positive deviation from Raoult's law: the heterogeneous interactions are weaker than the average of the homogeneous ones, so vapor pressures are higher than predicted → mixture boils LOWER than ideal would suggest."},{"id":"GC_ek-e6","section":"GC","topic":"eq-kc","difficulty":3,"q":"Consider the gas-phase reaction 2 NO(g) + O2(g) ⇌ 2 NO2(g) at 298 K and 1 atm, with all species in the gas phase. The change in moles of gas, Δn(g), is used to relate Kp and Kc and to predict pressure-shift behavior. What is Δn(g) for this reaction as written?","opts":["−2","−1","0","+1","+2"],"correct":1,"why":"Δn(g) = mol products(g) − mol reactants(g) = 2 − (2 + 1) = −1. The total gas moles decrease by 1 from reactants to products.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_nr4","section":"GC","topic":"nernst","difficulty":3,"q":"For the standard Daniell-style cell Zn(s) + Cu^2+(aq) -> Zn^2+(aq) + Cu(s) at 25°C, n = 2 and E°cell = +1.10 V. Setting E = 0 in the Nernst equation E = E° - (0.0592/n)·log Q corresponds to the reaction reaching equilibrium (Q = K). Solve for the value of Q at which E = 0.","opts":["1","10","10^-37","10^37","10^-18"],"correct":3,"why":"Set E = 0: 0 = 1.10 - (0.0592/2)·log Q, so log Q = 1.10·2/0.0592 = 37.16, giving Q = 10^37. At equilibrium Q = K, confirming the reaction goes essentially to completion.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_nu5","section":"GC","topic":"nuclear","difficulty":3,"q":"Carbon-11 is a positron emitter (t1/2 = 20.4 min) used in PET imaging. A proton in the C-11 nucleus converts into a neutron with the emission of a positron (and a neutrino), via 11-6 C -> X + 0+1 e + neutrino. Identify the daughter nuclide X.","opts":["11-5 B","11-7 N","10-5 B","12-5 B","11-6 C"],"correct":0,"why":"Positron emission converts a proton to a neutron, so A is unchanged and Z decreases by 1. From 11-6 C, the daughter is 11-5 B (boron-11). Mass and charge are conserved across the equation.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ks-e4","section":"GC","topic":"ksp","difficulty":3,"q":"At 25 °C, Ksp(PbCl2) = 1.6x10^(-5) for PbCl2(s) ⇌ Pb^(2+) + 2 Cl-. A solution is prepared with [Pb^(2+)] = 0.010 M and [Cl-] = 0.010 M. Calculate Q = [Pb^(2+)][Cl-]^2 and compare to Ksp to predict whether a precipitate forms.","opts":["Yes, ppt forms (Q > Ksp)","No, no ppt (Q < Ksp)","Exactly at saturation (Q = Ksp)","Cannot be determined","Yes, but only after evaporation"],"correct":1,"why":"Q = (0.010)(0.010)^2 = 1.0x10^(-6). Compare to Ksp = 1.6x10^(-5): Q < Ksp, so the solution is unsaturated and no precipitate forms. Forgetting the square on [Cl-] gives Q = 10^(-4) (wrongly predicting ppt).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ic-e4","section":"GC","topic":"eq-ice","difficulty":3,"q":"For the gas-phase reaction A(g) ⇌ B(g) at 298 K with Kc = 4.0, a 1.0 L flask is initially loaded with [A]_0 = 1.0 M and [B]_0 = 0. Setting up an ICE table with change −x for A and +x for B, solve for the equilibrium concentration [B]_eq.","opts":["0.20 M","0.50 M","0.67 M","0.80 M","1.0 M"],"correct":3,"why":"Kc = [B]/[A] = x/(1.0 − x) = 4.0 gives x = 4.0 − 4.0x -> 5.0x = 4.0 -> x = 0.80 M. Therefore [B]_eq = 0.80 M and [A]_eq = 0.20 M.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lf007","section":"GC","topic":"solutions","difficulty":3,"q":"A 0.050 mol sample of glucose (C6H12O6, non-electrolyte) is dissolved in 250 g of water. The Kf for water is 1.86 °C/m. What is the freezing point of the resulting solution?","opts":["−0.186 °C","−0.372 °C","−0.558 °C","−1.86 °C","0 °C (freezing point unchanged for non-electrolytes)"],"correct":1,"why":"m = mol/kg = 0.050/0.250 = 0.200 m. ΔTf = i·Kf·m = (1)(1.86)(0.200) = 0.372 °C. Freezing point depression: 0 − 0.372 = −0.372 °C."},{"id":"GC_xp003","section":"GC","topic":"acid-base","difficulty":3,"q":"Phosphoric acid (H₃PO₄) has Ka1 = 7.5 × 10⁻³, Ka2 = 6.2 × 10⁻⁸, Ka3 = 4.8 × 10⁻¹³. A buffer is prepared from 0.100 M H₂PO₄⁻ and 0.100 M HPO₄²⁻. What is the pH?","opts":["pH = 7.21","pH = 2.13","pH = 12.3","pH = 4.67","pH = 9.54"],"correct":0,"why":"Buffer of conjugate pair H₂PO₄⁻ / HPO₄²⁻ uses pKa2. pH = pKa2 + log([HPO₄²⁻]/[H₂PO₄⁻]) = 7.21 + log(1) = 7.21."},{"id":"GC_p-q2","section":"GC","topic":"periodic","difficulty":3,"q":"An unknown main-group element shows successive ionization energies (kJ/mol) of 786, 1577, 3232, 4356, 16,091, 19,805, ... A huge gap appears between IE4 and IE5. The size of this jump signals that the 5th electron is being removed from a much more tightly bound (core) shell. To which group does the element belong?","opts":["Group 1 (1 valence e-)","Group 14 (4 valence e-)","Group 16 (6 valence e-)","Group 17 (7 valence e-)","Group 2 (2 valence e-)"],"correct":1,"why":"A 'huge jump' between IE_n and IE_(n+1) reveals n valence electrons; the (n+1)-th comes from a noble-gas core. Here n = 4, so the element has 4 valence electrons - Group 14 (silicon's IE pattern, in fact).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_if4","section":"GC","topic":"imf-rank","difficulty":2,"q":"Hydrogen fluoride (HF, MW = 20) boils at 19.5°C, while hydrogen chloride (HCl, MW = 36.5) boils at -85°C. HCl is nearly twice as massive — by simple London-force reasoning it 'should' boil higher. Why does HF actually boil over 100°C higher than HCl?","opts":["HF is denser than HCl","HF forms strong hydrogen bonds (F-H···F); HCl does not because Cl is too large/diffuse","HCl is ionic in the liquid state","Their boiling points are actually equal at 1 atm","HF has much greater London dispersion than HCl"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydrogen bonding requires H bonded to N, O, or F. F's small size and high electronegativity give a very strong, localized H-bond network in HF; Cl's larger and less electronegative atom does not meet the H-bond criterion. The H-bond energy easily outweighs the London advantage HCl gets from its higher mass.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_qn3","section":"GC","topic":"quantum-numbers","difficulty":2,"q":"A laser emits a photon of wavelength 400 nm (4.00 x 10^-7 m), used to excite a molecule's pi-pi* transition. Given Planck's constant h = 6.63 x 10^-34 J·s and c = 3.00 x 10^8 m/s, calculate the energy carried by a single 400 nm photon using E = hc/lambda.","opts":["2.0 x 10^-19 J","5.0 x 10^-19 J","3.3 x 10^-20 J","7.5 x 10^-25 J","8.0 x 10^-27 J"],"correct":1,"why":"Using E = hc/lambda = (6.63e-34)(3.0e8)/(4.0e-7) = (1.989e-25)/(4.0e-7) = 4.97 x 10^-19 J ~ 5.0 x 10^-19 J. Doubling the wavelength would halve the photon energy.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_gl-h1","section":"GC","topic":"gas-laws","difficulty":3,"q":"An ideal gas sample is initially at P₁ = 2.00 atm, T₁ = 300 K (27°C), and V₁ = 5.00 L in a sealed cylinder. The cylinder is heated to T₂ = 600 K (327°C) and the gas is allowed to expand against a piston to V₂ = 10.0 L. What is the final pressure P₂?","opts":["0.50 atm","1.0 atm","2.0 atm","4.0 atm","8.0 atm"],"correct":2,"why":"Use combined gas law: P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂ → P₂ = P₁(V₁/V₂)(T₂/T₁) = 2.00 × (5/10) × (600/300) = 2.00 × 0.5 × 2 = 2.00 atm. The doubling of T exactly cancels the doubling of V, so pressure is unchanged.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_xp008","section":"GC","topic":"atomic","difficulty":3,"q":"The first four ionization energies (kJ/mol) of an element are: IE1 = 738, IE2 = 1450, IE3 = 7732, IE4 = 10,540. Which group of the periodic table does this element MOST LIKELY belong to, and why?","opts":["Group 2 (alkaline earth metals); large jump between IE2 and IE3 indicates losing the third electron breaks into a noble-gas core","Group 1 (alkali metals); only one valence electron","Group 13; jump after IE3","Group 14; equal IEs","Group 17 (halogens); high IEs throughout"],"correct":0,"why":"The dramatic jump (1,450 → 7,732 kJ/mol) between IE2 and IE3 indicates that two valence electrons are removed easily but the third comes from a noble-gas-like core. That signature belongs to Group 2 elements (e.g., Mg has IE values close to these)."},{"id":"GC_lc-e4","section":"GC","topic":"eq-lechat","difficulty":3,"q":"Consider the gas-phase equilibrium H2(g) + I2(g) ⇌ 2 HI(g) at 700 K, in which the moles of gas are equal on both sides (Δn(gas) = 0). The system is compressed isothermally to half its original volume, doubling the total pressure. How does the equilibrium position shift?","opts":["Forward (toward HI)","Reverse (toward H2 + I2)","No shift in either direction","Equally toward both","Cannot be determined"],"correct":2,"why":"Pressure shifts only matter when Δn(g) ≠ 0. Here Δn(g) = 2 − 2 = 0, so changing P (or V) does not shift the equilibrium position. Q and K both change identically.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_xp010","section":"GC","topic":"periodic","difficulty":3,"q":"Arrange the following ions by INCREASING ionic radius (smallest first), all of which are isoelectronic with Ar: K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺, S²⁻, P³⁻.","opts":["Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻ < P³⁻","P³⁻ < S²⁻ < Cl⁻ < K⁺ < Ca²⁺","Cl⁻ < K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < S²⁻ < P³⁻","K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < Cl⁻ < P³⁻ < S²⁻","All five have the same ionic radius (isoelectronic)"],"correct":0,"why":"Isoelectronic species: more positive charge → smaller radius (greater Zeff pulling same electrons in tighter). Order by nuclear charge: P (15) < S (16) < Cl (17) < K (19) < Ca (20). So radius decreases as Z increases: P³⁻ > S²⁻ > Cl⁻ > K⁺ > Ca²⁺. Smallest first: Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻ < P³⁻."}],"qr":[{"id":"QR_fd2","section":"QR","topic":"frac-dec","difficulty":1,"q":"5/8 = ?","opts":["0.625","0.58","0.375","0.65"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_dec1094","section":"QR","topic":"dec-arith","difficulty":2,"q":"6.9 × 2.5 = ?","opts":["172.5","1.73","17.25","17.35"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_rat1364","section":"QR","topic":"rate-time","difficulty":2,"q":"A car drives 20 mph for 3 hr. Distance?","opts":["60","40","6.67","80"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_hm1","section":"QR","topic":"harm","difficulty":2,"q":"Boat 30 mph downstream, 20 mph upstream, same distance. Avg speed?","opts":["24 mph","25 mph","26 mph","22 mph"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_int1425","section":"QR","topic":"interest","difficulty":2,"q":"$1000 at 6.00% compound for 10 years. Final?","opts":["1060","1000.6","600","1790.85"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_le3","section":"QR","topic":"lin-eq","difficulty":2,"q":"Solve: (x + 4)/3 = 5.","opts":["x = 11","x = 15","x = 1","x = 19"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_exp1713","section":"QR","topic":"exps","difficulty":3,"q":"Simplify: 2^4 ÷ 2^4.","opts":["65536","1","256","32"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_fmm5","section":"QR","topic":"frac-mul","difficulty":3,"q":"(3/4) ÷ (1/2) ÷ (3/8) = ?","opts":["4","9/64","3/16","16/3"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_pom4","section":"QR","topic":"pct-of","difficulty":3,"q":"0.5% of 600 = ?","opts":["3","30","300","0.5"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_pct1143","section":"QR","topic":"pct-chg","difficulty":1,"q":"A $200 item is increased by 50%. New price?","opts":["150","100","300","250"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_est1190","section":"QR","topic":"estim","difficulty":1,"q":"Estimate 969 + 28.","opts":["1080","997","1030","980"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_pem1294","section":"QR","topic":"pemdas","difficulty":2,"q":"Evaluate: 4 + 5 × 3","opts":["11","19","27","23"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_rat1340","section":"QR","topic":"ratios","difficulty":2,"q":"In a 4:3 ratio totaling 14, what's the smaller part?","opts":["6","8","9","7"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_ds1688","section":"QR","topic":"ds","difficulty":2,"q":"Slope from (-6,-6) to (-10,-7)?","opts":["-5","-0.25","0.25","4"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_abs1728","section":"QR","topic":"absval","difficulty":2,"q":"|-19| = ?","opts":["-38","-19","19","-18"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_log1751","section":"QR","topic":"logs","difficulty":2,"q":"log_2(16) = ?","opts":["4","8","5","3"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_mix1409","section":"QR","topic":"mixture","difficulty":2,"q":"Mix 100mL of 20% with 50mL of 15%. Final concentration?","opts":["18.33","17.5","26.25","27.5"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_zsc1627","section":"QR","topic":"zscore","difficulty":2,"q":"μ = 150, σ = 5, x = 140. z?","opts":["-2","2","-0.07","-10"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_dis1283","section":"QR","topic":"distrib","difficulty":3,"q":"Expand and evaluate 5(6 - 3).","opts":["20","10","15","3"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_cou1574","section":"QR","topic":"count-pc","difficulty":3,"q":"How many ways to choose 3 of 8 items (no order)?","opts":["11","56","336","24"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_pfl1519","section":"QR","topic":"p-flip","difficulty":3,"q":"8 fair coin flips. P(exactly one head)?","opts":["0.97","0.06","0.03","0.02"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_men1228","section":"QR","topic":"mental","difficulty":3,"q":"Mentally compute 16 × 45.","opts":["710","720","730","820"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_uni1472","section":"QR","topic":"units","difficulty":3,"q":"2.1 m in cm?","opts":["102.1","0.02","21","210"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_gu4","section":"QR","topic":"guess","difficulty":3,"q":"On a 5-choice question, two answers look almost identical (e.g., \"4\" and \"4.0\"). Pick which?","opts":["Likely the simpler one","Always the longer","The decimal version","Skip — it's a trap"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_xp001","section":"QR","topic":"algebra","difficulty":4,"q":"A factory produces widgets. Fixed costs are $4,000 per day; variable cost is $3 per widget. Each widget sells for $7. The factory operates 250 days a year. What is the MINIMUM number of widgets per day that must be sold to break even on the year (i.e., total annual revenue equals total annual cost)?","opts":["1,000 widgets/day","4,000 widgets/day","750 widgets/day","1,250 widgets/day","600 widgets/day"],"correct":0,"why":"Daily margin per widget = $7 − $3 = $4. Daily loss without sales = $4,000. Break-even per day: 4,000 / 4 = 1,000 widgets/day."},{"id":"QR_dis1599","section":"QR","topic":"discr","difficulty":4,"q":"For -1x² - 2x + 4 = 0, how many real roots?","opts":["two imaginary roots","two real roots","one real root","no real roots"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_fd5","section":"QR","topic":"frac-dec","difficulty":1,"q":"7/8 = ?","opts":["0.875","0.78","0.125","0.95"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_dec1097","section":"QR","topic":"dec-arith","difficulty":2,"q":"16.5 - 4.5 = ?","opts":["1.2","12.1","120","12"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_rtm3","section":"QR","topic":"rate-time","difficulty":3,"q":"Car A leaves at 50 mph. Car B leaves 1 hr later, same direction at 70 mph. When does B catch A?","opts":["2.5 hr after B left","5 hr","1 hr","3 hr after A left"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_hm3","section":"QR","topic":"harm","difficulty":3,"q":"Drove 60 mph for 1 hr, then 40 mph for 1 hr. Avg speed?","opts":["50 mph","48 mph","100 mph","45 mph"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_int1426","section":"QR","topic":"interest","difficulty":2,"q":"$5000 at 5.00% compound for 2 years. Final?","opts":["500","5512.5","5000.1","5250"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_lin1256","section":"QR","topic":"lin-eq","difficulty":3,"q":"Solve: 2x - 9 = -1x + 10","opts":["-6.33","6.33","5.33","7.33"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_exp1714","section":"QR","topic":"exps","difficulty":3,"q":"Simplify: 2^2 ÷ 2^3.","opts":["0.5","32","12","64"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_fra1059","section":"QR","topic":"frac-mul","difficulty":4,"q":"1/5 ÷ 4/7 = ?","opts":["4/12","5/35","7/20","3/5"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_pct1135","section":"QR","topic":"pct-of","difficulty":4,"q":"68% of 348 = ?","opts":["236.64","416","23664","23.66"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_fra1076","section":"QR","topic":"frac-dec","difficulty":3,"q":"Convert 1/6 to a decimal (3 dp).","opts":["0.167","0.333","0.5","0.25"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_dec1101","section":"QR","topic":"dec-arith","difficulty":3,"q":"26.6 + 7.1 = ?","opts":["33.8","3.37","33.7","337"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_rat1373","section":"QR","topic":"rate-time","difficulty":3,"q":"A car drives 40 mph for 3.5 hr. Distance?","opts":["140","180","100","11.43"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_fra1080","section":"QR","topic":"frac-dec","difficulty":4,"q":"Convert 1/9 to a decimal (3 dp).","opts":["0.111","0.25","0.5","0.333"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_dec1106","section":"QR","topic":"dec-arith","difficulty":4,"q":"23.6 + 3.2 = ?","opts":["2.68","26.8","268","26.9"],"correct":1}],"ochem":[{"id":"OC_ar067","section":"OCHEM","topic":"arom-eas","difficulty":3,"q":"An aryl diazonium salt (ArN2+ Cl-) prepared at 0 degC from an aniline + NaNO2/HCl is treated with CuBr (a Sandmeyer reagent). The major product is:","opts":["Ar-OH (would require H2O/heat, not CuBr)","Ar-Br (Sandmeyer: copper(I) bromide replaces -N2+ with -Br via radical mechanism)","Ar-CN (would require CuCN)","Ar-H (from H3PO2 reduction)","Ar-Ar (biaryl coupling)"],"correct":1,"why":"Sandmeyer reactions use Cu(I) salts to replace -N2+ on the ring: CuBr -> ArBr; CuCl -> ArCl; CuCN -> ArCN. They proceed via a single-electron transfer mechanism that releases N2 gas. CuI does not work — KI is used instead.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_lf001","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":3,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms","q":"(R)-2-bromobutane is treated with sodium hydroxide in DMSO at 25°C. The reaction is found to follow second-order kinetics with rate = k[substrate][NaOH]. Which of the following best describes the product and the mechanism?","opts":["(R)-2-butanol via SN1 with retention","(S)-2-butanol via SN2 with inversion at the chiral center","Racemic 2-butanol via SN1 with racemization","2-butene via E2 elimination","(R)-2-butanol via SN2 with retention"],"correct":1,"why":"Second-order kinetics in a polar aprotic solvent with a 2° substrate and a strong nucleophile points to SN2. Backside attack inverts the stereocenter: (R) → (S)."},{"id":"OC_lab260","section":"OCHEM","topic":"lab","difficulty":3,"q":"A chemist must separate a mixture of structurally similar compounds that differ mainly in polarity. Which technique provides the highest resolution for analytical or preparative-scale separations based on polarity?","opts":["Decantation (separates immiscible liquid phases)","Vacuum filtration (removes solids from liquids)","Simple distillation (relies on boiling-point differences)","Column chromatography on silica gel with an organic eluent","Liquid-liquid extraction (relies on partition coefficient)"],"correct":3,"why":"Silica-gel column chromatography separates compounds by competitive adsorption between a polar stationary phase and a less polar mobile phase. Tuning eluent polarity controls retention, allowing resolution of analytes with subtle polarity differences.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_abo278","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":3,"q":"Acetic acid, ethanol, and ethane are dissolved in DMSO. Which has the most acidic proton, and which factor makes the largest contribution to the difference in acidity?","opts":["Ethane C-H; sp3 hybridization","Ethanol O-H; alkoxide stability","Acetic acid O-H; resonance stabilization of the carboxylate spreads charge over two oxygens","Acetic acid alpha C-H; enolate is most stable","All three protons are equivalent"],"correct":2,"q_smiles":"CC(=O)O","opts_smiles":["CC","CCO","CC(=O)O",null],"why":"Acetate's negative charge is delocalized over two equivalent oxygen atoms by resonance, lowering acetic acid's pKa to ~4.76. Ethoxide has no resonance (pKa ~16), and ethanide (~50) leaves charge on carbon.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_ste035","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":1,"q":"Tartaric acid in its (2R,3S) form has two stereocenters connected by an internal mirror plane that exchanges them. What stereochemical category describes this isomer of tartaric acid?","opts":["A meso compound (achiral despite chirality centers)","A racemic mixture","An enantiomer of (2R,3R)-tartaric acid","A diastereomer with no symmetry","A geometric (E/Z) isomer"],"correct":0,"why":"An internal mirror plane between two stereocenters relates the halves and makes the molecule superimposable on its mirror image. That is a meso compound; (2R,3S)-tartaric acid is the textbook example.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_car157","section":"OCHEM","topic":"carbonyl","difficulty":2,"q":"An aldehyde is heated with 2 equivalents of ethanol and a catalytic amount of p-TsOH in dry toluene, with water removed by a Dean-Stark trap to drive the equilibrium forward. What is the functional class of the major product?","opts":["Acetal (R-CH(OR')2)","Ester","Hemiacetal (R-CH(OR')OH)","Simple ether (R-O-R')","Enol ether"],"correct":0,"q_smiles":"CCO","why":"The acetal-formation equilibrium (aldehyde + 2 ROH = acetal + H2O) is normally unfavorable, but a Dean-Stark trap continuously removes water, pushing the equilibrium toward the acetal by Le Chatelier's principle. Two ethoxy groups end up on the original carbonyl carbon, giving R-CH(OEt)2.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec026","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":3,"q":"Enantiopure (R)-2-iodobutane reacts with NaSCH3 in DMSO at 30 degrees C. The product is the corresponding sulfide with a measured optical rotation opposite in sign to the starting material. What stereochemical outcome at the reactive carbon explains this?","opts":["Racemization through a flat carbocation intermediate","No change in configuration; the sign change is solvent-induced","Walden inversion via concerted backside SN2 attack","Retention of configuration via a frontside displacement","Diastereomerization through a meso intermediate"],"correct":2,"why":"A strong nucleophile, a polar aprotic solvent, and a secondary substrate favor SN2. The nucleophile attacks 180 degrees from the leaving group, inverting all three remaining substituents (Walden inversion) and flipping the sign of rotation.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_spe224","section":"OCHEM","topic":"spec-ir-nmr","difficulty":4,"q":"A neat-liquid IR spectrum displays a sharp, strong absorption at 1715 cm^-1 with no broad O-H from 2500 - 3300 cm^-1 and no aldehydic C-H stretch near 2720 cm^-1. Considering frequency, intensity, and bandshape, which functional group best fits the 1715 cm^-1 band?","opts":["C identical C of a terminal alkyne","O-H of a carboxylic acid","N-H of an amide","C=O of a saturated ketone","C-O of an ester"],"correct":3,"why":"Saturated ketones display a sharp, strong C=O stretch close to 1715 cm^-1. The absence of broad O-H or aldehyde C-H bands excludes the acid and aldehyde alternatives, leaving the ketone.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_ab006","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":4,"q":"The CARDIO mnemonic prioritizes factors that stabilize negative charge (and therefore raise acidity). Which of the following lists the priority in the correct order?","opts":["Atom > Charge > Resonance > Dipole > Orbitals","Charge > Atom > Resonance > Dipole/Induction > Orbitals","Resonance > Atom > Charge > Dipole > Orbitals","Charge > Resonance > Atom > Dipole > Orbitals","Orbitals > Dipole > Resonance > Atom > Charge"],"correct":1,"why":"CARDIO ranks factors as: Charge (formal charge dominates), then Atom (electronegativity within a row, size within a column), then Resonance, then Dipole/Induction, then Orbital hybridization. Use the highest-priority discriminator that differs between two species.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_st059","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":2,"q":"A solution contains a 50:50 mixture of (R)- and (S)-2-bromobutane (a racemic mixture). Light from a sodium D line (589 nm) is passed through a polarimeter cell of fixed length. The observed optical rotation is:","opts":["+45 deg","-45 deg","0 deg, because equal and opposite contributions cancel","Depends linearly on total concentration","Cannot be measured"],"correct":2,"why":"Each enantiomer rotates plane-polarized light by an equal magnitude in opposite directions. With equal concentrations (50:50), the rotations cancel exactly, giving an observed rotation of 0 — the defining feature of a racemic mixture.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_car165","section":"OCHEM","topic":"carbonyl","difficulty":3,"q":"Two equivalents of acetone are stirred with a catalytic amount of dilute aqueous NaOH at 30 degC and then warmed to drive off water. Which products best describe the base-catalyzed aldol condensation that follows?","opts":["An imine of acetone (Schiff base)","Diacetone alcohol (beta-hydroxy ketone), then mesityl oxide after dehydration","Methyl acetate via Claisen condensation","Acetic acid via haloform oxidation","Acetone cyanohydrin"],"correct":1,"q_smiles":"CC(=O)C","opts_smiles":[null,null,null,"CC(=O)O"],"why":"An aldol condensation is two stages: (1) addition - enolate attacks a second carbonyl giving a beta-hydroxy ketone (diacetone alcohol from acetone), and (2) dehydration - heat triggers E1cb loss of water to give the conjugated alpha,beta-unsaturated enone (mesityl oxide). Conjugation provides the thermodynamic driving force for dehydration.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec022","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":3,"q":"1-Iodopentane reacts with 1.0 M NaCN in DMSO at 25 degrees C to produce hexanenitrile cleanly. Doubling either reactant doubles the rate. Which mechanism is operating, and why?","opts":["SN2: primary substrate, strong nucleophile, polar aprotic solvent, second-order kinetics","SN1: a primary carbocation is uniquely stable in DMSO","E2: cyanide acts as a base on the beta-hydrogen","E1: ionization precedes loss of a proton","Radical chain substitution catalyzed by light"],"correct":0,"why":"Primary alkyl halides don't form stable cations, so SN1/E1 are not viable. A strong nucleophile in a polar aprotic solvent (which de-shields the nucleophile) and bimolecular kinetics are textbook SN2 conditions.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_spe205","section":"OCHEM","topic":"spec-ir-nmr","difficulty":1,"q":"A combustion analysis returns the empirical formula CxHy for a pure hydrocarbon. To plan its NMR interpretation, the analyst first needs the degrees of unsaturation. Which expression yields the correct number of rings plus pi bonds?","opts":["y / x","(2x + 2 - y) / 2","x + y/2","Just count rings on the structure","(y - 2x) / 2"],"correct":1,"why":"Starting from the saturated formula CnH2n+2, every ring or pi bond eliminates two hydrogens. The general expression (2x + 2 - y) / 2 gives the count of rings plus pi bonds for a hydrocarbon CxHy.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_abo267","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":1,"q":"Compare the C-H acidity of a terminal alkyne (HC≡C-R) to that of a vinyl C-H on an alkene (H2C=CHR). Which pair of approximate pKa values is correct, and which is more acidic?","opts":["Both are ~25; equally acidic","Both are ~50; equally weak","Alkyne ~25, alkene ~44 — alkyne is more acidic (sp vs sp2)","Alkene ~25, alkyne ~44 — alkene is more acidic","Alkyne ~10, alkene ~30"],"correct":2,"why":"Acidity tracks with s-character of the C-H bond: sp (50% s) holds the electron pair closer to the nucleus than sp2 (33% s). Terminal alkynes have pKa ~25, while vinyl C-H on alkenes is ~44.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_ste049","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":3,"q":"A student examines a Fischer-style drawing where the lowest-priority H sits on a wedge pointing toward them. The 1->2->3 CIP priorities sweep clockwise. What is the actual configuration of the stereocenter?","opts":["R, taken directly from the drawing","S, because viewing the wrong side flips the apparent rotation","Achiral","Meso","Configuration cannot be determined"],"correct":1,"why":"When the lowest-priority group is on a wedge pointing toward you, the visible direction of rotation is opposite the true direction. Apparent clockwise therefore corresponds to true counterclockwise (S).","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_syn246","section":"OCHEM","topic":"synth","difficulty":4,"q":"Ethene is to be converted into ethylene glycol by a single-step syn-dihydroxylation, with both hydroxyl groups added to the same face of the alkene. Which reagent system achieves this without C-C bond cleavage?","opts":["HBr with peroxides","Cold, dilute KMnO4 (or OsO4 / NMO) for syn-dihydroxylation","Hot, concentrated KMnO4 (oxidative cleavage)","H2 over Pd/C","Br2 in water"],"correct":1,"q_smiles":"C=C","opts_smiles":["Br[H]",null,null,null],"why":"Cold, dilute KMnO4 or OsO4 (with a co-oxidant such as NMO) creates a cyclic mangante or osmate ester that hydrolyzes to give the syn-1,2-diol. Hot KMnO4 over-oxidizes and cleaves the C=C; H2/Pd reduces only.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_arr005","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-arrows","difficulty":3,"q":"tert-butyl chloride converts to tert-butanol in aqueous solution (SN1). Given the starting material and product below, which curved-arrow set correctly describes the rate-determining step?","q_svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 160\" width=\"360\" height=\"160\"><style>\n  .bond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .dbond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .atom { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; fill: #1a1d20; text-anchor: middle; }\n  .lp   { fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .chg  { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .arrow { stroke: #cf3b3b; stroke-width: 1.8; fill: none; }\n  .arrowhead { fill: #cf3b3b; }\n  .lbl { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #6a737a; }\n</style><defs>\n  <marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" class=\"arrowhead\"/>\n  </marker>\n  <marker id=\"ah-half\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5\" class=\"arrowhead\" stroke=\"#cf3b3b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  </marker>\n</defs>\n    <!-- t-BuCl on left -->\n    <text x=\"60\" y=\"80\" class=\"atom\">C</text>\n    <text x=\"60\" y=\"50\" class=\"atom\">CH3</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"60\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"55\"/>\n    <text x=\"30\" y=\"100\" class=\"atom\">CH3</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"55\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"95\"/>\n    <text x=\"90\" y=\"100\" class=\"atom\">CH3</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"65\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"95\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"68\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <text x=\"115\" y=\"84\" class=\"atom\">Cl</text>\n    <text x=\"160\" y=\"84\" class=\"atom\">→</text>\n    <!-- t-BuOH on right -->\n    <text x=\"220\" y=\"80\" class=\"atom\">C</text>\n    <text x=\"220\" y=\"50\" class=\"atom\">CH3</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"220\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"55\"/>\n    <text x=\"190\" y=\"100\" class=\"atom\">CH3</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"215\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"95\"/>\n    <text x=\"250\" y=\"100\" class=\"atom\">CH3</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"225\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"95\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"228\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <text x=\"275\" y=\"84\" class=\"atom\">OH</text>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"140\" class=\"lbl\">Slow step is C-Cl ionization (RDS); fast water attack follows</text>\n  </svg>","opts":["One arrow from C–Cl bond to Cl, generating t-Bu+ and Cl−","One arrow from H2O lone pair to C with simultaneous C–Cl breakage (concerted)","Two arrows: one from H2O to C, one from C–Cl to Cl, both at the same time","No arrows needed — proton transfer only"],"correct":0,"why":"SN1 RDS = unimolecular ionization. The single arrow from C-Cl bond to Cl produces a tertiary carbocation. Water attack is the fast second step.","_ada_category":"Mechanisms","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)O","opts_smiles":[null,"O","O",null]},{"id":"OC_lb026","section":"OCHEM","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"On a normal-phase TLC plate (silica = polar stationary phase) developed with a nonpolar mobile phase such as hexane/ethyl acetate (90:10), where does the most polar compound in a mixture end up after development?","opts":["Travels furthest from the baseline (highest Rf)","Stays close to the baseline (lowest Rf) because it adsorbs strongly to the polar silica","Doesn't interact with the plate","Runs off the top edge","Becomes invisible to UV"],"correct":1,"why":"‘Like dissolves like’ on TLC: polar compounds bind tightly to polar silica and resist being carried along by a nonpolar solvent, giving low Rf values. Less polar species are pulled along with the solvent front and have higher Rf.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_ab012","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":2,"q":"Boron trifluoride reacts with ammonia in the gas phase to form the adduct F3B-NH3. Identify the Lewis acid and the Lewis base, and explain the bonding.","opts":["BF3 is the Lewis acid (empty p orbital on boron accepts the lone pair); NH3 is the Lewis base donor","NH3 is the Lewis acid; BF3 is the Lewis base","Both are Lewis acids; no bond forms","Both are Lewis bases; product is ionic","Neither — this is a Brønsted equilibrium"],"correct":0,"why":"Lewis acid = electron-pair acceptor; Lewis base = electron-pair donor. BF3 has only six valence electrons on boron and an empty p orbital; NH3 has a lone pair on N. Donation from N into B forms a dative bond.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_ste050","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":4,"q":"At a single stereocenter, the four substituents are -OH, -NH2, -CH3, and -H. Using Cahn-Ingold-Prelog rules and atomic number at the first point of difference, what is the priority order from highest to lowest?","opts":["NH2 > OH > CH3 > H","OH > NH2 > CH3 > H","OH > CH3 > NH2 > H","H > CH3 > NH2 > OH","CH3 > NH2 > OH > H"],"correct":1,"why":"First-atom priorities go by atomic number: O (8) > N (7) > C (6) > H (1). So -OH is highest, then -NH2, then -CH3, then -H. The order does not depend on what is further down the chain when the first atoms already differ.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_aci179","section":"OCHEM","topic":"acids","difficulty":2,"q":"Methyl benzoate is heated with aqueous NaOH (1.1 equiv) in ethanol/water at reflux for 4 hours. After cooling, what are the immediate organic products of this saponification, prior to any acidic workup?","opts":["Benzaldehyde and methanol","Sodium benzoate and methanol","Benzoic acid and sodium methoxide","Methyl benzoate hydrate and NaOH","Phenol and sodium acetate"],"correct":1,"opts_smiles":[null,null,"O",null],"why":"Saponification is base-promoted ester hydrolysis. Hydroxide attacks the carbonyl, the tetrahedral intermediate expels methoxide, and the carboxylic acid is rapidly deprotonated by base to give the carboxylate salt. The reaction is irreversible because of this final deprotonation step.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec010","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":2,"q":"Two elimination experiments on 2-bromobutane are run: one with concentrated NaOEt in ethanol, and one in aqueous acetone with no added base. Which mechanism's experimentally measured rate law contains the base concentration?","opts":["Both E1 and E2 contain [base] in their rate laws","E1, because the carbocation must be deprotonated by base","E2, because the base removes a beta-proton in the rate-limiting step","Neither, because elimination is always solvent-driven","Only the SN2 competing pathway includes base"],"correct":2,"why":"E2 is concerted and bimolecular, so the rate equals k[RX][base]. E1 has a slow ionization step that does not involve base; the base is consumed only after the rate-limiting step, so it does not appear in the rate law.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_sp017","section":"OCHEM","topic":"spec-ir-nmr","difficulty":3,"q":"Compute the degrees of unsaturation (index of hydrogen deficiency) for the molecular formula C5H10. Use the formula DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2.","opts":["0 (saturated acyclic alkane)","1 (one ring or one pi bond)","2 (two rings, two pi bonds, or one triple bond)","3","4"],"correct":1,"why":"DoU = (2(5) + 2 - 10)/2 = 2/2 = 1. One degree of unsaturation means the molecule contains exactly one ring or one pi bond — for example, a pentene or a cyclopentane.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_alk076","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":3,"q":"Treating propene with one equivalent of Br2 in CCl4 at 0 degrees C in the dark gives anti addition. Identify the major product and explain the stereochemical outcome.","opts":["1,1-Dibromopropane via gem-dihalide","1,2-Dibromopropane formed via anti opening of a bridged bromonium ion","2,2-Dibromopropane via Markovnikov dihalide","3-Bromopropene via radical allylic bromination","Propylene oxide via cyclization"],"correct":1,"q_smiles":"CC=C","why":"Br2 first forms a cyclic bromonium ion, then bromide attacks from the opposite face. The result is a vicinal dibromide with anti stereochemistry. On propene, this is 1,2-dibromopropane.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_arr007","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-arrows","difficulty":3,"q":"3-methyl-2-butanol + HBr (excess). Arrows show carbocation formation and subsequent hydride shift. Predict the major product.","q_svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 160\" width=\"360\" height=\"160\"><style>\n  .bond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .dbond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .atom { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; fill: #1a1d20; text-anchor: middle; }\n  .lp   { fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .chg  { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .arrow { stroke: #cf3b3b; stroke-width: 1.8; fill: none; }\n  .arrowhead { fill: #cf3b3b; }\n  .lbl { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #6a737a; }\n</style><defs>\n  <marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" class=\"arrowhead\"/>\n  </marker>\n  <marker id=\"ah-half\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5\" class=\"arrowhead\" stroke=\"#cf3b3b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  </marker>\n</defs>\n    <!-- 3-methyl-2-butanol -->\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"30\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"65\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"100\"/>\n    <text x=\"65\" y=\"65\" class=\"atom\">OH</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"65\" y1=\"73\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"65\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"100\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"135\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"100\"/>\n    <text x=\"135\" y=\"65\" class=\"atom\">CH3</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"135\" y1=\"73\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"65\"/>\n    <text x=\"190\" y=\"84\" class=\"atom\">→</text>\n    <text x=\"220\" y=\"84\" class=\"atom\">[+H, -H2O, ~H]</text>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"140\" class=\"lbl\">Secondary carbocation rearranges via 1,2-hydride shift to tertiary</text>\n  </svg>","opts":["2-bromo-3-methylbutane (no rearrangement)","2-bromo-2-methylbutane (after hydride shift to 3° cation)","3-bromo-3-methylbutane","1-bromo-3-methylbutane"],"opts_smiles":["CC(Br)C(C)C","CC(Br)(C)CC",null,null],"correct":1,"why":"Initial 2° carbocation undergoes 1,2-hydride shift to form a more stable 3° cation. Br− attacks → 2-bromo-2-methylbutane.","_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_spe225","section":"OCHEM","topic":"spec-ir-nmr","difficulty":4,"q":"A neat-liquid IR spectrum has a sharp, strong absorption at 1715 cm^-1, no broad O-H from 2500 - 3300 cm^-1, and no diagnostic aldehyde C-H stretch near 2720 cm^-1. Which functional group is most consistent with the 1715 cm^-1 absorption?","opts":["O-H of a carboxylic acid","C identical C of an alkyne","C=O of a saturated ketone","N-H of a primary amide","C-O of an aliphatic ether"],"correct":2,"why":"Saturated ketones absorb near 1715 cm^-1 (sharp, strong). The lack of broad O-H or aldehyde C-H stretches eliminates the carboxylic acid and aldehyde alternatives.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_alk078","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":4,"q":"Cyclohexene is exposed to O3 in CH2Cl2 at -78 degrees C and then worked up with Zn/AcOH (reductive conditions). Predict the major product and contrast it with what would form under oxidative workup.","opts":["Cyclohexanone, since the ring is preserved","Hexanedioic acid (adipic acid), the oxidative-workup product","Hexanedial (open-chain dialdehyde) from reductive workup of the ozonide","Cyclohexanol from reduction of the alkene","1,2-Cyclohexanediol from syn dihydroxylation"],"correct":2,"q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_smiles":["O=C1CCCCC1",null,null,"OC1CCCCC1"],"why":"Ozone cleaves the cyclic C=C and the ozonide is reduced by Zn/AcOH to two aldehydes, opening the ring into hexanedial. Oxidative workup (H2O2) would push the aldehydes to carboxylic acids (adipic acid).","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec013","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":2,"q":"(S)-2-bromooctane reacts with NaN3 in DMSO. Doubling either the alkyl halide concentration or the azide concentration doubles the rate. Which step is rate-limiting?","opts":["Two-step ionization to an octyl carbocation","A single concerted bimolecular step where Nu attacks as LG departs","Loss of a beta-H to give an alkene first","Pre-equilibrium ion-pair formation","Radical abstraction of bromine before substitution"],"correct":1,"why":"When the rate depends on both [substrate] and [nucleophile], the mechanism is bimolecular and concerted. SN2 has a single transition state in which bond-making and bond-breaking happen simultaneously.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_aro105","section":"OCHEM","topic":"arom-eas","difficulty":1,"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with Br2 and a catalytic amount of FeBr3 at 80 degC. The -NO2 group is strongly deactivating and a meta director. What is the major monobromination product?","opts":["No reaction occurs at any temperature","p-bromonitrobenzene","o-bromonitrobenzene","m-bromonitrobenzene","2,4-dibromonitrobenzene"],"correct":3,"q_smiles":"[O-][N+](=O)c1ccccc1","why":"Drawing the resonance forms of the sigma complex shows that ortho and para attack place positive charge directly on the carbon bearing -NO2, which is severely destabilized by the electron-poor nitrogen. Meta attack avoids that conflict, so meta is the least bad option for a strong deactivator. Heat is required because the ring is deactivated, but meta is still the major regiochemistry, giving m-bromonitrobenzene.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_alk065","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":2,"q":"Propene is treated with dilute aqueous H2SO4 at 25 degrees C without peroxides. Which alcohol is the Markovnikov product, and what cation intermediate explains the regioselectivity?","opts":["Propanal via oxidation","Propane after H2 release","1-Propanol from primary cation","2-Propanol via the more stable secondary carbocation","1,2-Propanediol via dihydroxylation"],"correct":3,"q_smiles":"CC=C","opts_smiles":["CCC=O","CCC","CCCO","CC(O)C"],"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration protonates the alkene to give the more stable carbocation; the secondary cation at C2 is favored over the primary at C1. Water traps it and deprotonation gives 2-propanol.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"}]}},{"id":"MOCK_2","name":"Practice Test 2","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"BIO_cm107","section":"BIO","topic":"translation","difficulty":2,"q":"Crick's wobble hypothesis explains how 61 sense codons can be read by fewer than 61 different tRNA species. The first two codon positions pair stringently (Watson-Crick), while the third position allows non-standard pairing — for example, inosine in tRNA can pair with U, C, or A. Which codon position shows wobble?","opts":["1st codon position (5' end), which is fully redundant","2nd codon position, where U can pair with G","3rd codon position (3' end of the codon, 5' end of the anticodon), where non-standard pairing allows one tRNA to read multiple codons","Anticodon center (middle of the tRNA), which has no role in pairing","Both 1st and 2nd, but never the 3rd"],"correct":2,"why":"Wobble occurs at the 3rd codon position, which pairs with the 5' (first) position of the anticodon. Modified bases like inosine here can pair with multiple bases (U, C, A), so one tRNA can recognize several codons coding for the same amino acid. This explains the observed redundancy of the genetic code.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm069","section":"BIO","topic":"enzymes","difficulty":2,"q":"In bacterial isoleucine biosynthesis, isoleucine itself binds and inhibits the very first enzyme of the pathway (threonine deaminase). When isoleucine levels are high, the entire pathway shuts down; when isoleucine drops, biosynthesis resumes. Which regulatory mechanism is this?","opts":["Feedforward activation, where the substrate of the first step accelerates downstream enzymes","Feedback inhibition, where the end product of a pathway inhibits the first committed enzyme — preventing wasteful overproduction","Substrate-level competition between two pathways","Hormonal regulation by an external signaling molecule","Random mutation triggered by overproduction"],"correct":1,"why":"Feedback inhibition is a classic regulatory motif: the end product of a pathway allosterically inhibits the FIRST committed enzyme. This conserves resources by shutting off the entire pathway when product is sufficient. The Ile-deaminase example is the textbook case (Umbarger, 1956).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm124","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"Water can cross lipid bilayers slowly by simple diffusion, but in tissues with high water flux — kidney collecting ducts during ADH-induced water reabsorption, for example — the rate is dramatically increased by integral membrane proteins that form selective channels. What are these proteins called?","opts":["Active transport pumps that use ATP to move water across the membrane","Aquaporins — selective channels that allow water (and some small uncharged molecules like glycerol) to flow through at high rates without permitting ion passage","Receptor kinases that signal membrane changes","Cell-surface glycoproteins involved in cell adhesion","Ribosome-associated translocons"],"correct":1,"why":"Aquaporins are integral membrane channels that allow water to cross the bilayer rapidly while excluding ions (their selectivity filter rejects charged species). In the kidney, ADH triggers insertion of AQP2 into collecting duct cells, dramatically increasing water reabsorption. The 2003 Nobel Prize in Chemistry honored aquaporin discovery.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm039","section":"BIO","topic":"replication","difficulty":2,"q":"DNA polymerase III in E. coli adds new nucleotides to a growing strand by attacking the 3' OH of the strand's terminal nucleotide and forming a phosphodiester bond with the next dNTP. As a result, the new strand grows in only one direction. In which direction does DNA polymerase synthesize?","opts":["5' → 3' (the only direction DNA polymerase can synthesize)","3' → 5'","Both 5' → 3' and 3' → 5' equally well","Direction depends on whether the strand is leading or lagging","Direction depends on the temperature of the reaction"],"correct":0,"why":"All known DNA polymerases synthesize exclusively 5' → 3' — they catalyze nucleophilic attack of the 3' OH on the α-phosphate of the incoming dNTP. This forces antiparallel synthesis: one strand is made continuously (leading), the other discontinuously as Okazaki fragments (lagging).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm001","section":"BIO","topic":"water","difficulty":2,"q":"A pond freezes from the surface downward each winter, and aquatic life survives beneath the ice layer. Which physical property of water — uniquely tied to its molecular geometry — best explains why solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water?","opts":["High specific heat capacity that buffers temperature changes","Strong cohesive forces and surface tension between water molecules","Hydrogen bonds in ice form an open, hexagonal lattice that holds molecules farther apart than in the liquid","Water's universal solvent property, which prevents close packing of molecules","The polar O-H bonds, which orient toward charged ions in solution"],"correct":2,"why":"Water molecules form four hydrogen bonds in ice, locking into a tetrahedral hexagonal lattice with empty space between molecules. In liquid water, hydrogen bonds break and reform, allowing closer packing — so liquid water is denser than ice. This unusual property allows ice to float and insulate aquatic ecosystems.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm012","section":"BIO","topic":"nucleic","difficulty":2,"q":"An mRNA strand contains the sequence 5'-AUGCAU-3'. During reverse transcription using the same mRNA as template, the resulting DNA strand is synthesized as the complement. Which nitrogenous base in the RNA template will pair with adenine on the new DNA strand?","opts":["Thymine (T) — the standard DNA-DNA pairing partner of adenine","Uracil (U) — used in RNA in place of thymine","Guanine (G) — pairs with cytosine via three H-bonds","Cytosine (C) — pairs with guanine, never with adenine","Inosine (I) — a modified base in tRNA wobble pairing"],"correct":1,"why":"RNA uses uracil instead of thymine. So when adenine on a new DNA strand pairs with the RNA template, the template base is uracil. (DNA-DNA pairing always uses thymine; RNA introduces uracil.)","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm116","section":"BIO","topic":"eukaryotic-reg","difficulty":2,"q":"Histone tail acetylation by histone acetyltransferases (HATs) such as p300/CBP transfers acetyl groups to lysine residues. The chemical effect is to neutralize the positive charge on lysine, weakening the histone-DNA interaction. What is the typical effect of histone acetylation on gene expression?","opts":["Silences transcription by tightly compacting chromatin","Activates transcription by neutralizing histone positive charges, loosening DNA-histone contacts (open chromatin), and recruiting bromodomain-containing transcriptional activators","Has no effect on chromatin or transcription","Compresses chromatin into heterochromatin","Triggers DNA replication directly"],"correct":1,"why":"Histone acetylation neutralizes lysine positive charges, weakening histone-DNA interactions and opening chromatin (euchromatin). Acetylated histones are also bound by bromodomain proteins (e.g., BRD4) that recruit transcriptional machinery. Histone deacetylases (HDACs) reverse this and silence genes; HDAC inhibitors are used as anticancer drugs.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm033","section":"BIO","topic":"photo","difficulty":2,"q":"An isotopic labeling experiment uses water containing the heavy oxygen isotope (H2-18O) instead of normal water. Plants are grown in this labeled water and the gas they release is collected and analyzed. The released O2 contains the heavy isotope, demonstrating which step of photosynthesis?","opts":["O2 originates from the carbon dioxide (CO2) consumed by the plant","O2 originates from water, which is split (photolyzed) by Photosystem II to generate electrons, protons, and O2","O2 originates from glucose breakdown during respiration","O2 originates from NADPH oxidation in the Calvin cycle","O2 is a byproduct of cytochrome b6f activity in the thylakoid membrane"],"correct":1,"why":"The classic experiments of Ruben and Kamen showed that the O2 released by photosynthesis comes from water, not CO2. Photosystem II uses light energy to oxidize water (photolysis), producing electrons, protons, and O2 as a waste product. Carbon from CO2 ends up in glucose, not in O2.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm037","section":"BIO","topic":"mitosis","difficulty":2,"q":"A geneticist labels newly synthesized DNA with a thymidine analog (BrdU) in cycling cells. Cells exposed to BrdU only during a brief 1-hour window incorporate the label only if they happen to be in a specific phase of the cell cycle during that window. In which phase does DNA replication take place?","opts":["G1 phase, when cells grow and prepare for DNA synthesis","S phase, when DNA is replicated and chromatids become two-stranded","G2 phase, when cells perform a final DNA-damage check before mitosis","M phase, during mitotic chromosome condensation","G0, the quiescent state outside the cycle"],"correct":1,"why":"DNA replication occurs only in S phase ('synthesis'). Each chromosome becomes two identical sister chromatids joined at the centromere. G1 is preparation, G2 is final checks, M is the actual division. BrdU is incorporated only during S, making it a classic S-phase marker.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm008","section":"BIO","topic":"lipids","difficulty":2,"q":"Steroid hormones such as testosterone, cortisol, and estradiol all share a four-fused-ring carbon skeleton. They are synthesized in cells from a common precursor lipid that is also a critical component of animal cell membranes. Which lipid is this precursor?","opts":["Triglyceride (e.g., tristearin)","Phospholipid (e.g., phosphatidylcholine)","Cholesterol","Wax (e.g., beeswax)","Sphingomyelin"],"correct":2,"why":"Cholesterol is the four-ring sterol that serves as the precursor to all steroid hormones (cortisol, aldosterone, testosterone, estradiol, progesterone) and bile acids. It is also a key component of animal cell membranes, where it modulates fluidity.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm046","section":"BIO","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"In agarose gel electrophoresis of DNA fragments, an electric field is applied across the gel and DNA migrates through the porous matrix. Because DNA's phosphate backbone is uniformly negatively charged at physiological pH, all fragments migrate in the same direction toward one electrode. Toward which electrode does DNA migrate?","opts":["Toward the negatively charged cathode, since DNA has positive sugar groups","Toward the positively charged anode, since the DNA phosphate backbone is negatively charged","DNA does not migrate in agarose gels — only proteins do","Direction is randomized by molecular tumbling","Toward whichever electrode is at the lower temperature"],"correct":1,"why":"DNA's phosphate groups give it a uniform negative charge per unit length. In an electric field, negatively charged molecules migrate toward the positive electrode (anode). Smaller fragments encounter less resistance in the gel matrix and travel farther per unit time, separating fragments by size.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm083","section":"BIO","topic":"signaling","difficulty":2,"q":"Aldosterone is a steroid hormone secreted by the adrenal cortex. After diffusing across the plasma membrane of kidney cells, aldosterone binds its receptor; the hormone-receptor complex dimerizes and translocates to the nucleus, where it acts as a transcription factor. Where is this receptor located?","opts":["Cell surface, as a G-protein-coupled receptor","Cell surface, as a receptor tyrosine kinase","Intracellular (cytoplasm or nucleus) — steroid hormone receptors are nuclear/intracellular receptors that act as ligand-activated transcription factors","Inside an ion channel as a regulatory subunit","Anchored to the mitochondrial outer membrane"],"correct":2,"why":"Steroid hormones (aldosterone, cortisol, estrogen, testosterone) are lipophilic and pass through the plasma membrane. Their receptors are intracellular (cytoplasmic in some cases, nuclear in others). The hormone-receptor complex binds DNA response elements and modulates gene transcription directly — slow but durable effects on cell behavior.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm042","section":"BIO","topic":"transcription","difficulty":2,"q":"Eukaryotic mRNA is transcribed and processed in one cellular compartment, then exported across the nuclear envelope to be translated into protein in another compartment. In contrast to prokaryotes, this physical separation prevents simultaneous transcription and translation. Where in a eukaryotic cell does TRANSCRIPTION occur?","opts":["In the cytoplasm, where ribosomes are also located","In the nucleus — RNA polymerase II reads DNA and produces pre-mRNA there","On free ribosomes throughout the cytoplasm","On the rough endoplasmic reticulum, near membrane-bound ribosomes","In the mitochondrion only, since DNA is genomic"],"correct":1,"why":"Transcription occurs in the nucleus in eukaryotes. RNA polymerase II reads the DNA template and produces pre-mRNA, which is then capped, spliced, and polyadenylated before export to the cytoplasm for translation. Prokaryotes lack a nucleus, so transcription and translation can be coupled.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm061","section":"BIO","topic":"proteins","difficulty":2,"q":"When a globular protein folds in aqueous solution, the polypeptide adopts the conformation that minimizes free energy. The hydrophobic effect — driven primarily by the entropy of surrounding water molecules — is one of the largest energetic contributions to folding. As a result, hydrophobic R-groups in a folded protein tend to:","opts":["Face outward toward water, where they form van der Waals contacts with surrounding solvent","Cluster in the protein's interior, away from water — the hydrophobic core","Form hydrogen bonds with water and remain solvent-exposed","Become charged (deprotonated) to gain solubility in water","Distribute randomly throughout the protein with no preference"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydrophobic R-groups (Val, Leu, Ile, Phe, Met) cluster in the protein's interior. Burying them releases ordered water molecules that had been forming clathrate-like structures around them — a large entropy gain. Polar/charged R-groups remain on the surface to interact with water.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm129","section":"BIO","topic":"krebs","difficulty":2,"q":"After full aerobic catabolism of one glucose molecule (glycolysis + pyruvate decarboxylation + Krebs cycle), the bulk of the ATP is generated in mitochondria as NADH and FADH₂ feed electrons into the ETC, driving chemiosmosis through ATP synthase. Approximately how many ATP per glucose come from this oxidative phosphorylation alone?","opts":["~4 ATP, comparable to total glycolysis","~14 ATP, since only NADH contributes","~26-28 ATP from oxidative phosphorylation alone (out of ~30-32 total per glucose)","More than 40 ATP per glucose","0 ATP, since oxidative phosphorylation does not generate ATP directly"],"correct":2,"why":"Per glucose, oxidative phosphorylation processes: 10 NADH (~2.5 ATP each = ~25) + 2 FADH₂ (~1.5 ATP each = 3) ≈ 26-28 ATP. Substrate-level phosphorylation contributes another ~4 (2 from glycolysis + 2 from Krebs). Total ~30-32 ATP per glucose, with the lion's share from oxidative phosphorylation.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm071","section":"BIO","topic":"cell-types","difficulty":2,"q":"A small, circular, double-stranded DNA molecule that exists separately from the main chromosome and replicates autonomously is found in a microbial genome. The molecule frequently carries antibiotic-resistance genes and can transfer between cells via conjugation. Which kind of organism most commonly carries such DNA elements?","opts":["Eukaryotes, where plasmids are commonly found in the nucleus","Bacteria, in which plasmids are widespread and often carry resistance, virulence, or metabolic genes","Archaea only, never found in bacteria","Mitochondria, which carry plasmids instead of a circular chromosome","Viruses, where plasmids serve as the genome"],"correct":1,"why":"Plasmids are extrachromosomal, circular DNA molecules characteristic of bacteria. They replicate autonomously and often carry antibiotic resistance, virulence, or metabolic genes. While yeast (eukaryotes) have plasmids, the textbook association — and the basis for cloning vectors — is bacterial.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm029","section":"BIO","topic":"glycolysis","difficulty":2,"q":"Glycolysis is regulated at three irreversible steps. The most heavily regulated of these is allosterically inhibited by high ATP and citrate, and activated by AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate. Which enzyme catalyzes this rate-limiting step?","opts":["Hexokinase, which phosphorylates glucose at the entry of glycolysis","Phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1), which converts fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate","Pyruvate kinase, which generates ATP at the final step","Aldolase, which cleaves fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into two trioses","Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, which produces NADH"],"correct":1,"why":"PFK-1 is the rate-limiting (committed) step of glycolysis. It is allosterically inhibited by ATP and citrate (signs of high cellular energy) and activated by AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate (signs of low energy or high glucose). Hexokinase is regulated more weakly (by product inhibition); pyruvate kinase is the third regulated step.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm111","section":"BIO","topic":"mutation","difficulty":2,"q":"DNA point mutations are categorized into two types based on how the substitution affects the chemical class of the base. One type substitutes within the same chemical family (e.g., A↔G), while the other type swaps between families. Which definition correctly distinguishes these two types?","opts":["Transition and transversion are interchangeable terms with no real distinction","Transition = purine ↔ purine (A↔G) or pyrimidine ↔ pyrimidine (C↔T); transversion = purine ↔ pyrimidine (e.g., A↔C). Transitions are more common since they are easier chemically","Transversion = always silent; transition = always missense","Both create stop codons by definition","Transition affects only RNA; transversion affects only DNA"],"correct":1,"why":"Transition is a purine→purine or pyrimidine→pyrimidine substitution (chemically similar). Transversion is purine→pyrimidine (or vice versa) — a bigger structural change. Transitions outnumber transversions about 2:1 because they are easier to make (similar ring structure makes mispairing easier during replication).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm055","section":"BIO","topic":"carbs","difficulty":2,"q":"Mammals store glucose primarily as glycogen in the liver and skeletal muscle. Glycogen is a highly branched polymer with α-1,4 linkages along chains and α-1,6 linkages at branch points (every 8-10 residues). The branched structure is functionally important because it maximizes:","opts":["Maximum carbon storage density per unit volume","Number of non-reducing ends, which allows glycogen phosphorylase to release glucose-1-phosphate from many sites simultaneously for fast mobilization","Insolubility, so glycogen does not osmotically disrupt the cell","Sweetness, since branched glucose tastes more sugary","Resistance to amylase, preventing digestion in the gut"],"correct":1,"why":"Branching multiplies the number of non-reducing ends — the substrate sites for glycogen phosphorylase. Many phosphorylase molecules can act simultaneously, mobilizing glucose-1-phosphate rapidly during fight-or-flight or fasting. Linear starch (less branched) mobilizes more slowly.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm121","section":"BIO","topic":"organelles","difficulty":2,"q":"Eukaryotic cilia and flagella have a strikingly conserved internal structure. A cross-section under TEM reveals a characteristic arrangement of microtubule doublets surrounding a central pair, anchored to a basal body. Which microtubule arrangement defines this axoneme?","opts":["9+0 — nine outer doublets, no central pair (found in primary, non-motile cilia)","9+2 — nine outer microtubule DOUBLETS arranged around 2 central singlets (motile cilia and flagella)","9+1 — nine outer doublets, one central singlet","8+2 — eight outer doublets and two central singlets","Random arrangement, varying by species"],"correct":1,"why":"Motile cilia and flagella have a 9+2 axoneme: nine outer microtubule doublets around two central singlets. Dynein motor arms on the doublets generate movement by sliding adjacent doublets relative to one another. Primary (non-motile) cilia have a 9+0 arrangement and serve sensory functions (e.g., Hedgehog signaling).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm113","section":"BIO","topic":"repair","difficulty":2,"q":"Patients with xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) develop severe sun-induced skin damage and dramatically elevated skin-cancer risk. Their cells are unable to repair UV-induced thymine dimers, which then cause replication errors and mutations. XP is caused by a defect in which DNA repair pathway?","opts":["Base excision repair (BER), which fixes oxidized or alkylated bases","Nucleotide excision repair (NER), which removes bulky lesions like UV-induced thymine dimers by excising a short oligonucleotide","Telomerase, which extends chromosome ends","DNA polymerase III, which synthesizes new DNA strands","Mismatch repair, which fixes post-replication errors"],"correct":1,"why":"Xeroderma pigmentosum is caused by mutations in NER genes (XPA-XPG). NER recognizes bulky helix-distorting lesions like UV-induced thymine dimers (cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers), excises a ~25-30 nt oligonucleotide containing the lesion, and resynthesizes. Without NER, dimers persist and cause mutations during replication.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm105","section":"BIO","topic":"translation","difficulty":2,"q":"When a small ribosomal subunit scans an mRNA from the 5' cap looking for the start of translation, it identifies a specific codon to initiate protein synthesis. This codon also codes for the first amino acid of every protein (later cleaved in many cases). Which mRNA codon is the universal start codon?","opts":["AUG, which codes for methionine (formyl-methionine in prokaryotes) and is the universal start codon","GUG, used as an alternative start in some bacterial genes (rare)","UAA, one of the three stop codons","AAA, which codes for lysine","UUU, which codes for phenylalanine"],"correct":0,"why":"AUG is the universal start codon. It pairs with the special initiator tRNA carrying methionine (in eukaryotes) or N-formyl-methionine (in prokaryotes). Some bacteria use GUG as an alternate start, but AUG is dominant. Internal AUGs encode methionine in the elongation phase.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm070","section":"BIO","topic":"enzymes","difficulty":2,"q":"Hemoglobin's oxygen-binding curve is sigmoidal rather than the hyperbolic shape predicted by Michaelis-Menten kinetics. The same shape is also typical of allosteric enzymes such as aspartate transcarbamoylase. Which property explains the sigmoidal curve?","opts":["Hyperbolic Michaelis-Menten kinetics with no special features","Sigmoidal kinetics arising from cooperativity — binding of substrate (or O₂) at one subunit increases the affinity of the remaining subunits","Strictly linear (zero-order) kinetics independent of substrate concentration","No saturation, since allosteric enzymes have unlimited active sites","The reaction proceeds in reverse at high [substrate]"],"correct":1,"why":"Allosteric enzymes (and hemoglobin) have multiple subunits that show cooperativity: binding the first substrate increases affinity at neighboring subunits. The result is a sigmoidal (S-shaped) saturation curve. This makes the protein highly responsive in a narrow concentration range, ideal for switch-like regulation.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm078","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"A laboratory technician accidentally places red blood cells into pure distilled water rather than 0.9% saline. Within seconds, the cells visibly swell and many burst, releasing hemoglobin into the surrounding solution. Which osmotic environment caused this and what was the outcome?","opts":["Hypertonic environment; cells shrunk and crenated","Hypotonic environment; water flowed INTO cells via osmosis until they swelled and lysed (hemolysis)","Isotonic environment; cells remained morphologically unchanged","Hypertonic environment; cells actively pumped water in to equalize","Cells underwent apoptosis with characteristic membrane blebbing"],"correct":1,"why":"Distilled water has zero solute (hypotonic relative to RBC cytoplasm). Water flows into the cell down its concentration gradient via osmosis, the cell swells, and the membrane ruptures (hemolysis). Animal cells lack a rigid cell wall, so they cannot resist this swelling — unlike plants or bacteria.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm040","section":"BIO","topic":"replication","difficulty":2,"q":"Before DNA polymerase can begin synthesizing a new strand, the parental double helix must be opened to expose single-stranded templates. Which enzyme uses ATP to break the hydrogen bonds between the two strands and unwind the helix at the replication fork?","opts":["Helicase — uses ATP to break hydrogen bonds and unwind the parental DNA","Primase — synthesizes RNA primers for DNA polymerase to extend","Ligase — seals nicks between Okazaki fragments","Topoisomerase — relieves supercoiling ahead of the fork by transient cuts","Single-strand binding (SSB) protein — stabilizes unwound strands"],"correct":0,"why":"Helicase is the unwinding enzyme — it uses ATP hydrolysis to break the H-bonds between base pairs and separate the two strands. Topoisomerase relieves supercoiling ahead of the fork (a different problem), primase makes RNA primers, ligase seals nicks, and SSB proteins stabilize the exposed single strands.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm051","section":"BIO","topic":"water","difficulty":2,"q":"Coastal regions experience smaller daily temperature swings than inland areas at the same latitude. Marine biologists attribute this thermal stability partly to a unique physical property of liquid water that resists temperature change relative to most other substances. Which property is responsible?","opts":["Cohesion (water-water attraction) that resists evaporation","High specific heat capacity (~4.18 J/g·°C) that absorbs/releases large amounts of heat per °C change","Density anomaly that causes ice to float and insulate liquid below","Polarity that makes water a universal solvent","Surface tension caused by hydrogen bonding at the air-water interface"],"correct":1,"why":"Water has an unusually high specific heat capacity (~4.18 J/g·°C) — much higher than most substances — because energy must first break extensive hydrogen-bonding networks before raising kinetic energy. Large bodies of water buffer temperature swings, moderating climate near coasts.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm065","section":"BIO","topic":"nucleic","difficulty":2,"q":"When DNA is heated, the two strands separate (denature) at a temperature called the melting temperature (Tm). DNA samples enriched in G-C base pairs have noticeably higher Tm values than samples enriched in A-T pairs. What is the structural reason?","opts":["G-C base pairs are joined by 3 hydrogen bonds, while A-T pairs use only 2; more H-bonds require more energy to break","G-C pairs are physically shorter and thus harder to separate by stretching","G-C pairs are typically located at chromosome ends, where they are protected","G-C pairs are heavily methylated and the methyl groups stabilize them","G-C pairs use covalent bonds, while A-T pairs are non-covalent"],"correct":0,"why":"G pairs with C using three hydrogen bonds (N-H...O, N-H...N, N-H...O), while A pairs with T using only two (N-H...O, N...H-N). More hydrogen bonds = more energy required to break = higher Tm. GC-rich genomes (e.g., thermophilic bacteria) are correspondingly more thermostable.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm117","section":"BIO","topic":"eukaryotic-reg","difficulty":2,"q":"DNA methylation in mammals primarily targets cytosines within CpG dinucleotides, especially those clustered in 'CpG islands' near gene promoters. Methyl-CpG-binding proteins (e.g., MeCP2) and chromatin remodelers then assemble repressive chromatin structures. What is the typical effect of CpG-island methylation at a gene promoter?","opts":["Activates transcription by recruiting RNA polymerase","Silences transcription — methylated promoters are bound by repressive factors and assemble closed chromatin","Causes mutation by directly damaging the DNA backbone","Promotes splicing by marking exon-intron boundaries","Extends telomeres by recruiting telomerase"],"correct":1,"why":"CpG-island methylation (5-methylcytosine in promoter regions) silences gene expression. Methyl-CpG-binding proteins recruit histone deacetylases and other repressors, compacting chromatin. Aberrant promoter hypermethylation silences tumor suppressors in cancer; X-inactivation and genomic imprinting also use this mechanism.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm091","section":"BIO","topic":"photo","difficulty":2,"q":"The Calvin cycle in chloroplasts fixes CO₂ into a 5-carbon sugar (ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate, RuBP), forming an unstable 6-carbon intermediate that immediately splits into two 3-carbon molecules. The enzyme catalyzing this carboxylation is the most abundant protein on Earth. Which enzyme is it?","opts":["Hexokinase, the first enzyme of glycolysis","RuBisCO (ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase) — the most abundant protein on Earth, fixing CO₂ at the entry of the Calvin cycle","Phosphofructokinase-1, the regulatory step of glycolysis","ATP synthase, used in both chloroplasts and mitochondria","Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxylase, used in C4 photosynthesis"],"correct":1,"why":"RuBisCO catalyzes CO₂ fixation onto RuBP — the entry step of the Calvin cycle. It accounts for roughly half of all soluble protein in plant leaves and is considered the most abundant protein on Earth. It also has a side activity (oxygenase, leading to photorespiration), which is why C4 and CAM plants have evolved to minimize this.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm036","section":"BIO","topic":"mitosis","difficulty":2,"q":"During mitosis, sister chromatids — the two identical copies of each replicated chromosome held together at the centromere — are pulled apart and travel to opposite poles of the cell. Which phase is defined by this separation event?","opts":["Prophase, when chromosomes condense and become visible","Metaphase, when chromosomes align at the metaphase plate","Anaphase, when sister chromatids are separated and pulled to opposite poles by the shortening of kinetochore microtubules","Telophase, when nuclear envelopes reform around the daughter nuclei","Cytokinesis, when the cytoplasm physically divides"],"correct":2,"why":"Anaphase is defined by the separation of sister chromatids. Cohesin proteins are cleaved by separase (activated when the spindle assembly checkpoint is satisfied), allowing kinetochore microtubules to pull each chromatid to opposite poles. Metaphase is alignment; telophase is nucleus reformation.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm057","section":"BIO","topic":"lipids","difficulty":2,"q":"Trans fats are produced industrially by partial hydrogenation of vegetable oils. Despite containing C=C double bonds (as do cis-unsaturated fats), trans fats behave more like saturated fats: they are solid at room temperature and raise LDL cholesterol. Which structural feature explains this similarity?","opts":["Trans fats have no C=C double bonds remaining after partial hydrogenation","The trans configuration places the carbons across the double bond on opposite sides, allowing the chain to remain straight and pack tightly — like a saturated fat","Trans bonds are stabilized by extensive hydrogen bonding to neighboring chains","Trans fats are more highly branched than cis fats","Trans fats contain charged groups that cause aggregation"],"correct":1,"why":"Cis double bonds bend the chain by ~30°, preventing tight packing — these fats stay liquid (oils). Trans double bonds keep the chain nearly straight (like a saturated bond), so the molecules pack like saturated fats: solid at room temperature, harder to digest, and associated with elevated LDL.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm049","section":"BIO","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"A molecular biologist wants to confirm that a particular mRNA species is being expressed at high levels in liver but not in muscle. They size-fractionate total RNA from each tissue on a gel, transfer it to a membrane, and probe with a labeled cDNA complementary to their mRNA of interest. Which type of blot is this?","opts":["Southern blot — detects DNA sequences","Northern blot — detects RNA sequences using a labeled DNA or RNA probe","Western blot — detects proteins using antibodies","Eastern blot — detects post-translationally modified proteins","Far-Western blot — detects protein-protein interactions"],"correct":1,"why":"Northern blot detects RNA: gel separation of RNA, transfer to a membrane, and hybridization with a labeled complementary nucleic-acid probe. Southern blot is the same protocol but for DNA; Western blot uses antibodies to detect proteins. Mnemonic: Southern (DNA) → Northern (RNA) → Western (protein).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm081","section":"BIO","topic":"signaling","difficulty":2,"q":"Hormone binding to one G-protein-coupled receptor (GPCR) on a cell can ultimately activate thousands of downstream protein-kinase A molecules within seconds. The signaling pathway gains enormous amplification at each step. Which mechanism best explains this signal amplification?","opts":["The original receptor binds the hormone with very high affinity","Each activated enzyme in the cascade catalytically generates many copies of the next signaling molecule (e.g., one adenylyl cyclase makes hundreds of cAMP, each cAMP activates one PKA, etc.)","Hormone diffuses out of the cell and activates more receptors","Second messengers are permanent and accumulate forever","The hormone covalently bonds to all downstream targets simultaneously"],"correct":1,"why":"Signaling cascades amplify because each enzyme step is catalytic. One activated GPCR can activate ~100 G proteins, each Gαs activates ~10 adenylyl cyclases, each cyclase makes ~1000 cAMP, etc. Multiplicative amplification across stages turns a single hormone-receptor binding event into a massive intracellular response.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm043","section":"BIO","topic":"transcription","difficulty":2,"q":"After RNA polymerase II finishes transcribing a eukaryotic gene, the resulting pre-mRNA undergoes three major processing steps before nuclear export: addition of a modified guanine residue at the front, addition of a long string of adenines at the back, and removal of intronic sequences. Which of these is added at the 5' end of the pre-mRNA?","opts":["A 7-methylguanosine cap, which protects the mRNA from exonucleases and recruits ribosomes","Histone proteins, which package the mRNA into chromatin","tRNA, the adapter molecule that brings amino acids to ribosomes","A complete ribosome attached at the start codon","An additional pre-spliced intron sequence"],"correct":0,"why":"The 5' cap is a 7-methylguanosine added co-transcriptionally to the first nucleotide of the pre-mRNA via a 5'-5' triphosphate bridge. The cap protects mRNA from 5' exonucleases and is recognized by the ribosomal initiation complex. The poly(A) tail goes on the 3' end; introns are spliced out internally.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm106","section":"BIO","topic":"translation","difficulty":2,"q":"The ribosome catalyzes peptide bond formation between the carboxyl end of a growing polypeptide (in the P site) and the amino group of an incoming aminoacyl-tRNA (in the A site). The catalytic activity is NOT performed by a protein but by an RNA molecule. Where in the ribosome does peptide bond formation occur?","opts":["By a protein enzyme called peptidyl transferase","By the rRNA in the large ribosomal subunit (the peptidyl transferase center) — the ribosome is a ribozyme; the rRNA does the chemistry","By tRNA's anticodon loop","By mRNA in the small subunit","By the ribosomal exit tunnel (E site)"],"correct":1,"why":"The ribosome is a ribozyme. The peptidyl transferase active site in the large subunit is composed entirely of rRNA (no protein side chains involved in catalysis). This was a major piece of evidence for the RNA World hypothesis — that ancient catalysis was RNA-based before proteins took over most enzymatic functions.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm015","section":"BIO","topic":"enzymes","difficulty":2,"q":"Two glucose-binding enzymes are characterized: hexokinase has a Km of 0.1 mM for glucose, while glucokinase (in liver) has a Km of 10 mM. What does the markedly lower Km of hexokinase indicate about its interaction with glucose?","opts":["Hexokinase is a slower enzyme, taking longer per catalytic cycle","Hexokinase has lower affinity for glucose, requiring more substrate to reach half-Vmax","Hexokinase has higher affinity for glucose — it reaches half-Vmax at a much lower substrate concentration","Hexokinase has a higher Vmax than glucokinase under all conditions","Km tells you nothing about substrate-enzyme affinity, only the enzyme's stability"],"correct":2,"why":"Km is the substrate concentration at which the reaction rate is half-Vmax. A LOW Km means the enzyme reaches that rate at low [S] — high affinity for substrate. Hexokinase's Km of 0.1 mM means it works efficiently at low blood glucose; glucokinase only kicks in at high glucose levels (post-meal).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm079","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"Macrophages engulf invading bacteria by extending pseudopodia around them, sealing them inside a membrane vesicle, and merging that vesicle with a lysosome for digestion. The initial engulfment is one form of bulk transport that brings large solid particles INTO the cell. What is this process called?","opts":["Active transport — uses ATP-driven transmembrane pumps","Phagocytosis — a type of endocytosis specialized for engulfment of large solid particles ('cell eating')","Exocytosis — vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane, releasing contents outward","Simple diffusion — passive movement across the bilayer","Pinocytosis — endocytosis of dissolved small solutes ('cell drinking')"],"correct":1,"why":"Phagocytosis is a specialized form of endocytosis used by macrophages, neutrophils, and amoeba to engulf large particles (bacteria, dead cells, debris). The pseudopodia surround the target, the membrane closes to form a phagosome, and lysosomes fuse to deliver hydrolytic enzymes for digestion. Pinocytosis handles dissolved solutes.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm100","section":"BIO","topic":"replication","difficulty":2,"q":"DNA replication is bidirectional: at each replication fork, one strand is synthesized continuously (leading strand) while the other is synthesized as short fragments (Okazaki fragments) that are later joined. The reason for this asymmetry is intrinsic to the directionality of DNA polymerase. Why does the lagging strand REQUIRE Okazaki fragments?","opts":["A different polymerase synthesizes the lagging strand","DNA polymerase can only synthesize 5' → 3', but the parental DNA strands are antiparallel — so the template for the lagging strand runs the wrong way for continuous synthesis. Short fragments are made backwards from the fork.","The lagging strand uses a slower polymerase","There is no primer available for continuous lagging-strand synthesis","The lagging strand is composed of RNA, not DNA"],"correct":1,"why":"DNA polymerase only synthesizes 5' → 3'. At a replication fork, one parental strand has its 3' end pointing toward the fork (leading template, continuous synthesis); the other has its 3' end pointing AWAY from the fork (lagging template). Synthesis of the lagging strand must proceed away from the fork in short bursts, each starting from a primer — the Okazaki fragments — which are later joined by ligase.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm133","section":"BIO","topic":"translation","difficulty":2,"q":"When a single mRNA is translated by many ribosomes simultaneously — each ribosome at a different stage along the mRNA — the structure looks like beads on a string under EM. This arrangement amplifies protein output per mRNA molecule and is especially prominent in tissues with high biosynthetic demand. What is this structure called?","opts":["Polysome (polyribosome) — multiple ribosomes simultaneously translating one mRNA","Polyadenylated structures, with many adenines on one ribosome","Damaged or stalled ribosomes that cannot finish translation","Ribosomal storage particles for unused subunits","An RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC) bound to the mRNA"],"correct":0,"why":"Polysomes are mRNAs simultaneously bound by multiple ribosomes (typically 1 ribosome per ~80 nt of mRNA). They greatly amplify protein output per mRNA. Active translation polysomes appear by EM as beads-on-a-string. Cells under stress disassemble polysomes (e.g., during the integrated stress response or amino acid deprivation).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm014","section":"BIO","topic":"enzymes","difficulty":2,"q":"A pharmacology student measures the kinetics of an enzyme in the presence of inhibitor X. Adding more substrate fully restores the original Vmax, but the apparent Km has doubled. The Lineweaver-Burk plots of inhibited and uninhibited reactions intersect on the y-axis. What kind of inhibitor is X?","opts":["Competitive inhibitor — increases apparent Km, leaves Vmax unchanged","Non-competitive inhibitor — decreases Vmax, Km unchanged","Uncompetitive inhibitor — decreases both Vmax and Km equally","Mixed inhibitor — increases both Km and Vmax","Allosteric activator — decreases Km, increases Vmax"],"correct":0,"why":"Competitive inhibitors compete with substrate for the active site. Adding more substrate outcompetes the inhibitor, so Vmax is unchanged. But the substrate must work harder to reach half-Vmax, so the apparent Km increases. Lineweaver-Burk plots intersect on the y-axis (1/Vmax).","_rewritten":true}],"gc":[{"id":"GC_gl-q3","section":"GC","topic":"gas-laws","difficulty":2,"q":"A balloon of helium initially has V1 = 2.0 L at P1 = 1.0 atm and T1 = 300 K. It is moved to a new condition where P2 = 2.0 atm and T2 = 600 K (no gas added or lost). Apply the combined gas law P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2 and solve for V2.","opts":["1.0 L","2.0 L","4.0 L","8.0 L","0.50 L"],"correct":1,"why":"V2 = V1 (P1/P2)(T2/T1) = 2.0 x (1.0/2.0) x (600/300) = 2.0 x 0.5 x 2 = 2.0 L. Doubling P and doubling T cancel out exactly.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ic-e1","section":"GC","topic":"eq-ice","difficulty":1,"q":"A student sets up an ICE table for the reaction A(g) ⇌ B(g) at 298 K, with rows labeled I, C, and E representing the three stages tracked during equilibrium calculations. Considering the standard ICE-table convention used in equilibrium problems, what does the C row represent?","opts":["Calculated equilibrium concentrations","Change in concentration (±x)","Constant concentration of solids","Concentration of catalyst","Common-ion concentration"],"correct":1,"why":"ICE = Initial, Change, Equilibrium. The C row tracks the change in each species' concentration as the reaction proceeds (e.g., −x for a reactant, +x for a product), based on stoichiometry.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ph3","section":"GC","topic":"ph-basics","difficulty":2,"q":"Acetic acid has Ka = 1.8×10⁻⁵ at 25°C. The conjugate base, acetate ion (CH₃COO⁻), has Kb such that Ka × Kb = Kw = 1.0×10⁻¹⁴. What is Kb for acetate?","opts":["5.6×10⁻¹⁰","1.8×10⁻⁵","1.0×10⁻¹⁴","5.6×10⁻⁹","1.8×10⁻⁹"],"correct":0,"why":"Kb = Kw / Ka = (1.0×10⁻¹⁴) / (1.8×10⁻⁵) = 5.56×10⁻¹⁰. Forgetting Kw and just inverting Ka gives 5.6×10⁴ (wrong magnitude); confusing exponents gives 5.6×10⁻⁹.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_v-e7","section":"GC","topic":"vsepr","difficulty":3,"q":"Three sp³ molecules — CH₄ (109.5°, 0 LP), NH₃ (~107°, 1 LP), H₂O (~104.5°, 2 LP) — share the same parent tetrahedral electron geometry but differ by lone-pair count. Lone pairs exert stronger repulsion than bonding pairs, compressing the bond angle. Rank the bond angles from smallest to largest.","opts":["CH₄ < NH₃ < H₂O","H₂O < NH₃ < CH₄","All three are equal at 109.5°","NH₃ < CH₄ < H₂O","H₂O < CH₄ < NH₃"],"correct":1,"why":"Each lone pair compresses the bond angle. CH₄ (0 LP, 109.5°), NH₃ (1 LP, 107°), H₂O (2 LP, 104.5°). The order from smallest to largest is H₂O < NH₃ < CH₄, i.e., more lone pairs → more compression → smaller angle.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_if2","section":"GC","topic":"imf-rank","difficulty":1,"q":"When comparing argon (Ar), hydrogen chloride (HCl), and water (H₂O) in the gas phase at 25°C and 1 atm, only one type of intermolecular force is shared by all three substances despite their differing polarities. Which force is universally present in every molecule and atom?","opts":["Permanent dipole-dipole","Hydrogen bonding","London dispersion (induced dipole)","Ion-dipole","Ionic attraction"],"correct":2,"why":"London dispersion forces arise from instantaneous, transient dipoles created by electron-cloud fluctuations and exist in every species. Polar molecules also have dipole-dipole forces, and only N/O/F-bound hydrogens give H-bonds, but dispersion is the universal floor.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ti1","section":"GC","topic":"titration","difficulty":1,"q":"25.0 mL of 0.100 M acetic acid (weak acid, Ka = 1.8×10⁻⁵) is titrated with 0.100 M NaOH (strong base) to the equivalence point at 25°C. At equivalence the only species present is the conjugate base CH₃COO⁻, which hydrolyzes to give a basic solution. What is the pH at equivalence?","opts":["pH < 7","pH = 7","pH > 7","Depends only on volumes","pH = 4.74"],"correct":2,"why":"At equivalence, all HA has been converted to A⁻. A⁻ is a weak base that hydrolyzes water: A⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HA + OH⁻, so the solution is basic (pH > 7). For weak acid + strong base, equivalence pH is always > 7.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_sf3","section":"GC","topic":"sig-figs","difficulty":2,"q":"A student adds two volumes of acid measured with different glassware, recording 12.34 mL (buret) and 5.6 mL (graduated cylinder), and writes 12.34 + 5.6 = 17.94 mL on her datasheet. Applying the addition/subtraction rule that the answer keeps the fewest decimal places, what is the correctly rounded result?","opts":["17.94","17.9","18","17.940","18.0"],"correct":1,"why":"For addition, the result has the smallest number of decimal places among the inputs. 12.34 has 2 decimals and 5.6 has 1 decimal, so the sum keeps 1 decimal place. 17.94 rounds to 17.9.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ek-e3","section":"GC","topic":"eq-kc","difficulty":2,"q":"For the reaction 2 A(g) ⇌ B(g) at 298 K and 1 atm, the reaction quotient is Q = [B]/[A]^2. At a given instant the concentrations are measured as [A] = 1.0 M and [B] = 4.0 M, before equilibrium is reached. What is the value of Q at this instant?","opts":["0.50","1.0","2.0","4.0","8.0"],"correct":3,"why":"Q = [B]/[A]^2 = 4.0 / (1.0)^2 = 4.0. Forgetting to square [A] gives 4.0 (correct here only by coincidence with [A]=1); using [A] = 0.5 would expose the squaring error.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_en-e3","section":"GC","topic":"enthalpy","difficulty":2,"q":"A 50.0 g sample of an unknown solid with specific heat c = 2.0 J/(g·°C) is heated from 20.0 °C to 30.0 °C inside a well-insulated calorimeter. Assuming all heat is absorbed by the solid and using q = m·c·ΔT, how much heat (q) was supplied to the sample?","opts":["100 J","500 J","1000 J","2000 J","5000 J"],"correct":2,"why":"q = m·c·ΔT = 50.0 g x 2.0 J/(g·°C) x 10.0 °C = 1000 J. Forgetting to multiply by mass gives 20 J, while doubling ΔT gives 2000 J.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_b4","section":"GC","topic":"bonding","difficulty":2,"q":"In hydrogen cyanide (H-C≡N), the central carbon forms one single bond to H and a triple bond to N. Counting one electron domain for the H-C bond and one for the C≡N triple bond (a triple bond counts as one domain), what is the hybridization of carbon in HCN?","opts":["sp","sp²","sp³","sp³d","sp³d²"],"correct":0,"why":"Hybridization follows the number of electron domains: 2 = sp, 3 = sp², 4 = sp³. Carbon in HCN has two domains (H and N), giving sp hybridization with two unhybridized p-orbitals forming the two π-bonds of the triple bond.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_bf4","section":"GC","topic":"buffers","difficulty":2,"q":"You need to prepare a buffer holding pH ≈ 4.7 for an enzyme assay at 25°C. Optimal buffer choice has pKa within ±1 unit of the target pH. Choose from acetate (pKa 4.74), phosphate (pKa 7.20), bicarbonate (pKa 6.35), and ammonia (pKa 9.25). Which is best?","opts":["Acetate (pKa 4.74)","Phosphate (pKa 7.20)","Bicarbonate (pKa 6.35)","Ammonia (pKa 9.25)","Tris (pKa 8.06)"],"correct":0,"why":"Best buffer has pKa closest to the desired pH. Acetate's pKa = 4.74 is within 0.04 units of pH 4.7 — essentially perfect. The other choices (pKa 6.35, 7.20, 9.25) are too high and would not buffer effectively at pH 4.7.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_p-e5","section":"GC","topic":"periodic","difficulty":2,"q":"Electron affinity (EA) measures the energy released when a gas-phase atom gains an electron — the more negative EA, the more favorable the addition. Among the halogens F (-328 kJ/mol), Cl (-349), Br (-325), and I (-295), which has the MOST negative electron affinity?","opts":["F","Cl","Br","I","All halogens have equal EA"],"correct":1,"why":"Despite F being smaller and seeming to attract electrons more strongly, its 2p subshell is so compact that adding an electron causes significant electron-electron repulsion, slightly destabilizing F⁻. Cl⁻ (3p subshell) avoids this crowding, giving Cl the most negative EA among the halogens.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_hb3","section":"GC","topic":"hybridization","difficulty":2,"q":"Carbon dioxide (O=C=O) is a linear molecule (180°) found in the atmosphere at ~420 ppm. The central carbon participates in two double bonds with no lone pairs of its own. What hybridization is required at carbon to accommodate two σ bonds and two π bonds in this geometry?","opts":["sp","sp²","sp³","dsp²","sp³d"],"correct":0,"why":"Steric number at C = 2 σ bonds + 0 lone pairs = 2, giving sp hybridization. The two sp orbitals form σ bonds at 180° to the oxygens, while the two unhybridized 2p orbitals form the two π bonds (one with each oxygen).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lr5","section":"GC","topic":"lim-react","difficulty":3,"q":"In the same Haber problem (1.00 mol N₂ limiting; 4.5 mol H₂ initially), N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ uses 3 mol H₂ per 1 mol N₂. After complete reaction, how many moles of H₂ remain UNREACTED?","opts":["1.5 mol","3.0 mol","0.5 mol","4.5 mol","0 mol"],"correct":0,"why":"H₂ consumed = 3 × 1.00 = 3.0 mol. H₂ remaining = 4.5 − 3.0 = 1.5 mol. Forgetting the 3:1 ratio gives 3.5 mol; subtracting the wrong way gives a negative or 0.5 result.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_nu-h1","section":"GC","topic":"nuclear","difficulty":3,"q":"A 100.0 mg sample of a radioactive isotope is monitored over time and the remaining mass is measured as 12.5 mg. Using the relation m_t = m_0·(1/2)^n where n is the number of half-lives elapsed, determine n.","opts":["2","3","4","5","6"],"correct":1,"why":"Set m_t/m_0 = 12.5/100 = 0.125 = (1/2)^n. Recognize 0.125 = 1/8 = (1/2)³, so n = 3 half-lives. Equivalently, 100 → 50 (1) → 25 (2) → 12.5 (3).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lw-q3","section":"GC","topic":"lewis","difficulty":3,"q":"Sulfur tetrafluoride (SF4) has a see-saw geometry with 4 S-F bonds and 1 lone pair on the central sulfur (10 electrons around S, expanded octet). Using formal charge = (valence e-) - (non-bonded e-) - (1/2)(bonded e-), what is the formal charge on the central S atom?","opts":["-2","0","+2","+4","-1"],"correct":1,"why":"S has 6 valence electrons. In SF4: non-bonded e- = 2 (one LP); bonded e- = 8 (four bonds). FC = 6 - 2 - (8/2) = 6 - 2 - 4 = 0. Sulfur's expanded octet is allowed because of accessible 3d orbitals in some treatments.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_en5","section":"GC","topic":"entropy","difficulty":3,"q":"Iron rusts in air according to the equation 4 Fe(s) + 3 O2(g) -> 2 Fe2O3(s) at 25°C. Three moles of gaseous O2 disappear and only solid product remains. Using the rule that gases dominate entropy and Δn_gas controls ΔS for reactions with mixed phases, predict the sign and approximate magnitude of ΔS_rxn.","opts":["Strongly positive","Slightly positive","Strongly negative","Approximately zero","Cannot be determined"],"correct":2,"why":"Δn_gas = 0 - 3 = -3, so 3 mol of gas disappears into a more ordered solid lattice. This collapse in microstates makes ΔS strongly negative (~-550 J/K typical for this reaction). Gases overwhelmingly dominate the entropy change.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_mc-e17","section":"GC","topic":"mole-conv","difficulty":3,"q":"A 22.2 g sample of CaCl₂ (MW = 111.0 g/mol; Ca = 40.0, Cl = 35.5 each) is dissolved in water. Each formula unit contains 2 Cl atoms. Use mass% × total mass, OR n_compound × 2 × MW_Cl, to find the total mass of chlorine.","opts":["7.1 g","14.2 g","22.2 g","35.5 g","71.0 g"],"correct":1,"why":"Mass fraction of Cl in CaCl₂ = (2 × 35.5) / 111.0 = 71/111 = 0.640. Mass of Cl in 22.2 g CaCl₂ = 0.640 × 22.2 = 14.2 g. Equivalently: n(CaCl₂) = 0.200 mol, so n(Cl) = 0.400 mol → 0.400 × 35.5 = 14.2 g.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ar-e5","section":"GC","topic":"arrhenius","difficulty":3,"q":"An empirical kinetics rule of thumb for reactions with typical activation energies (~50 kJ/mol) near room temperature states that the rate constant k approximately doubles per a fixed temperature rise. Based on Arrhenius behavior at ~300 K with E_a ≈ 50 kJ/mol, what is the approximate temperature rise (in K) required to double k?","opts":["1 K","5 K","10 K","25 K","100 K"],"correct":2,"why":"For E_a ~ 50 kJ/mol near 300 K, k roughly doubles for every ~10 K rise (the classic 'Q10 ≈ 2' biochemical rule). This stems from exp(E_a·ΔT/(R·T^2)) ≈ 2 when ΔT ≈ 10 K.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_sw6","section":"GC","topic":"strong-weak","difficulty":3,"q":"Four monoprotic acids are dissolved separately in water at 0.10 M and 25°C: HCl, HF (Ka = 6.6 x 10^-4), HClO4, and HBr. Three are strong (essentially fully dissociated), one is weak. Recall the inverse rule: weaker acid has a STRONGER conjugate base. Which acid has the STRONGEST conjugate base?","opts":["HCl (conjugate Cl-)","HF (conjugate F-)","HClO4 (conjugate ClO4-)","HBr (conjugate Br-)","All equally strong conjugate bases"],"correct":1,"why":"Strong acids (HCl, HBr, HClO4) produce essentially non-basic conjugate bases. HF is weak (Ka = 6.6 x 10^-4), so F- is the only conjugate base in this list with measurable basicity (Kb ~ 1.5 x 10^-11). Stronger acid -> weaker conjugate base.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ca-e2","section":"GC","topic":"calorimetry","difficulty":3,"q":"Inside a well-insulated coffee-cup calorimeter, 50.0 g of hot water at 90.0 °C is mixed with 50.0 g of cold water at 10.0 °C, and the mixture is allowed to reach thermal equilibrium. Assuming no heat loss and equal specific heats, what is the final equilibrium temperature?","opts":["20 °C","30 °C","40 °C","50 °C","60 °C"],"correct":3,"why":"Energy balance: m·c·(Tf − 90) + m·c·(Tf − 10) = 0. With equal masses, Tf is the simple average: (90 + 10)/2 = 50 °C. Unequal masses would weight the average.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_xp007","section":"GC","topic":"solutions","difficulty":3,"q":"A 0.200 m aqueous CaCl₂ solution shows a freezing-point depression of 1.05 °C. The Kf of water is 1.86 °C/m. What is the apparent van't Hoff factor (i), and what does it suggest about ion association in solution?","opts":["i ≈ 2.82; the solution shows partial ion-pairing (Ca²⁺ and Cl⁻ associate slightly), reducing i below the ideal 3","i = 3.00 exactly; CaCl₂ fully dissociates","i = 1.00; CaCl₂ does not dissociate","i = 2.00; only 1 Cl⁻ is dissociated","i ≈ 4.0; some clustering occurs"],"correct":0,"why":"i = ΔTf / (Kf · m) = 1.05 / (1.86 × 0.200) = 2.82. Ideal i for CaCl₂ is 3 (1 Ca²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻). i < 3 indicates ion pairing reduces effective particle count."},{"id":"GC_el5","section":"GC","topic":"electrolysis","difficulty":3,"q":"Two electrolytic cells are wired in SERIES so the same current passes through both: cell 1 deposits Cu (Cu^2+ + 2e- -> Cu, M = 63.5), cell 2 deposits Ag (Ag+ + e- -> Ag, M = 108). For every 1.00 g of Cu plated in cell 1, what mass of Ag is plated in cell 2?","opts":["0.85 g","1.7 g","3.4 g","108 g","0.59 g"],"correct":2,"why":"Same charge, so moles e- equal: moles Cu x 2 = moles Ag x 1. Moles Cu = 1.00/63.5 = 0.01575 mol, moles Ag = 0.0315 mol, mass Ag = 0.0315 x 108 = 3.40 g. Forgetting the n-factor mismatch yields the trap 1.7 g.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_nr-e6","section":"GC","topic":"nernst","difficulty":3,"q":"A hydrogen electrode operating at 25°C (298 K) is immersed in a solution buffered to pH 4.00 with H2 gas bubbling at 1.00 atm above the platinum surface. Given the half-reaction 2H+ + 2e- -> H2 with E° = 0.000 V and n = 2, what is the electrode potential E predicted by the Nernst equation?","opts":["+0.24 V","-0.24 V","0.00 V","-0.12 V","+0.12 V"],"correct":1,"why":"Apply E = E° - (0.0592/n)log Q with Q = P(H2)/[H+]^2 = 1/(10^-4)^2 = 10^8. So E = 0 - (0.0592/2)(8) = -0.2368 V, which rounds to -0.24 V. The negative sign reflects that low [H+] makes reduction less favorable.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ks2","section":"GC","topic":"ksp","difficulty":3,"q":"AgCl is dissolved in 0.10 M NaCl at 25°C; Ksp(AgCl) = 1.8×10⁻¹⁰. By the common-ion effect, [Cl⁻] ≈ 0.10 M is fixed by NaCl. Setting Ksp = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] and solving for [Ag⁺] = molar solubility s, what is the solubility of AgCl in this solution?","opts":["1.34×10⁻⁵ M","1.8×10⁻⁹ M","1.8×10⁻¹⁰ M","5.4×10⁻⁵ M","1.0×10⁻⁵ M"],"correct":1,"why":"s = Ksp / [Cl⁻] = (1.8×10⁻¹⁰) / 0.10 = 1.8×10⁻⁹ M. Solubility in pure water would be √Ksp ≈ 1.34×10⁻⁵; the common-ion 0.10 M Cl⁻ suppresses dissolution by ~10⁴×.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_g5","section":"GC","topic":"gas-laws","difficulty":2,"q":"Two ideal gases He (MW = 4.0 g/mol) and O₂ (MW = 32.0 g/mol) effuse from identical pinholes at the same temperature. Graham's law gives rate(A)/rate(B) = √(MW_B / MW_A). What is the ratio of effusion rates rate(He) : rate(O₂)?","opts":["1 : 1","2 : 1","2.83 : 1","8 : 1","32 : 1"],"correct":2,"why":"rate(He)/rate(O₂) = √(32/4) = √8 ≈ 2.83. Forgetting the square root (using 32/4 = 8) gives 8:1; using the mass ratio without inversion gives 1:8. Lighter gases effuse faster.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ic2","section":"GC","topic":"eq-ice","difficulty":2,"q":"In a weak-acid ICE table at 298 K, we often use the simplification 'C − x ≈ C', where C is the initial concentration and x is the amount dissociated. The 5% rule states this approximation introduces acceptable error only when x is below a critical fraction of C. Under what condition is the 5% rule valid?","opts":["When x > 5% of C","When x < 5% of C","Always valid","Never valid","Only when Ka > 1"],"correct":1,"why":"If x is less than 5% of initial C, then C − x ≈ C introduces less than 5% error in [HA] and the simplification is acceptable. For x ≥ 5% of C (typical when Ka is 'large' or C is 'small'), solve the quadratic instead.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ph-e7","section":"GC","topic":"ph-basics","difficulty":2,"q":"Consider the conjugate acid-base pair HA / A- in water at 25 °C, with the acid having Ka and the conjugate base having Kb. Using the equations HA ⇌ H+ + A- and A- + H2O ⇌ HA + OH-, the relation between Ka and Kb at this temperature is determined by water's self-ionization. What does Ka × Kb equal?","opts":["1","Kw","14","pKa + pKb","[H+][OH-]/[A-]"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiplying the two equilibrium expressions gives Ka·Kb = [H+][OH-] = Kw = 1.0x10^(-14) at 25 °C. Equivalently, pKa + pKb = pKw = 14.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_v-q1","section":"GC","topic":"vsepr","difficulty":3,"q":"The triiodide-like dichloroiodate ICl2- has 22 valence electrons around the central iodine: 2 bonding pairs to Cl and 3 equatorial lone pairs (AX2E3 by VSEPR). With the lone pairs occupying the equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid, what is the Cl-I-Cl bond angle?","opts":["90°","109.5°","120°","180°","104.5°"],"correct":3,"why":"AX2E3 places three lone pairs in the equatorial plane (minimizing LP-LP repulsion at 120° apart), and the two Cl ligands occupy the two axial positions opposite each other. The axial-axial Cl-I-Cl angle is therefore 180° (linear).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_gl-e13","section":"GC","topic":"gas-laws","difficulty":3,"q":"An ideal gas of CO2 (MW = 44.0 g/mol) is held at standard temperature and pressure (273 K, 1.00 atm), where the molar volume of an ideal gas is 22.4 L/mol. Using density = MW/V_molar, what is the density of CO2 in g/L under these conditions?","opts":["0.50 g/L","1.10 g/L","1.96 g/L","2.20 g/L","22.4 g/L"],"correct":2,"why":"Density = MW / V_molar = 44.0 g/mol / 22.4 L/mol = 1.96 g/L. Inverting (22.4/44 = 0.51) is a frequent slip; reporting V_molar itself (22.4) confuses units.","_rewritten":true}],"qr":[{"id":"QR_pem1293","section":"QR","topic":"pemdas","difficulty":1,"q":"Evaluate: 5 + 7 × 2","opts":["37","19","9","24"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_pct1139","section":"QR","topic":"pct-chg","difficulty":1,"q":"A $80 item is decreased by 50%. New price?","opts":["30","120","130","40"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_dis1271","section":"QR","topic":"distrib","difficulty":2,"q":"Solve: 3(x - 1) = 0","opts":["2","0","1","-1"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_rm4","section":"QR","topic":"ratios","difficulty":2,"q":"If 3 lbs of beef costs $24, what does 8 lbs cost?","opts":["$64","$72","$32","$48"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_ds1689","section":"QR","topic":"ds","difficulty":2,"q":"Slope from (0,-3) to (-2,-9)?","opts":["-3","-8","0.33","3"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_vol1672","section":"QR","topic":"vol-sa","difficulty":2,"q":"Box: 4×3×8. Volume?","opts":["30","96","100","15"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_wor1491","section":"QR","topic":"word-decode","difficulty":2,"q":"Five less than 6 times a number is 25. The number?","opts":["6","5","4","11"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_mix1411","section":"QR","topic":"mixture","difficulty":2,"q":"Mix 150mL of 10% with 50mL of 20%. Final concentration?","opts":["25","12.5","15","30"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_abs1732","section":"QR","topic":"absval","difficulty":2,"q":"|-6| = ?","opts":["-6","-12","6","-5"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_dis1594","section":"QR","topic":"discr","difficulty":3,"q":"For -1x² + 0x + 4 = 0, how many real roots?","opts":["no real roots","two real roots","one real root","two imaginary roots"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_pct1188","section":"QR","topic":"pct-seq","difficulty":4,"q":"$480 → -30% → +20%. Final?","opts":["432","403.2","336","576"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_coo1442","section":"QR","topic":"coord","difficulty":1,"q":"Distance from (-3, -2) to (5, 3)?","opts":["13","3.61","9.43","14"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_fd4","section":"QR","topic":"frac-dec","difficulty":1,"q":"1/4 = ?","opts":["0.25","0.4","0.14","0.5"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_log1747","section":"QR","topic":"logs","difficulty":2,"q":"log(1) = ?","opts":["0","0.1","1","-1"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_wor1389","section":"QR","topic":"work-rate","difficulty":2,"q":"A finishes a job in 4 hr, B in 8 hr. Together?","opts":["4","2.67","6","12"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_pfl1510","section":"QR","topic":"p-flip","difficulty":2,"q":"5 fair coin flips. P(heads on every flip)?","opts":["0.03","0.06","0.02","0.97"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_bnm2","section":"QR","topic":"binomials","difficulty":2,"q":"Factor 49 − x².","opts":["(7 + x)(7 − x)","(7 − x)²","(x − 7)²","(7 + x)²"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_pct1132","section":"QR","topic":"pct-of","difficulty":3,"q":"55% of 225 = ?","opts":["12375","12.38","280","123.75"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_pan1535","section":"QR","topic":"p-andor","difficulty":3,"q":"events: P(A) = 0.3, P(B) = 0.2, P(A and B) = 0.01. P(A and B)?","opts":["0.1","0.5","0.01","0.06"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_pco1554","section":"QR","topic":"p-cond","difficulty":3,"q":"Disease prevalence 2%, test sensitivity 99%, specificity 90%. P(disease | positive test)?","opts":["0.99","0.9","0.02","0.17"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_in3","section":"QR","topic":"interest","difficulty":3,"q":"$2000 at 8% compounded quarterly for 1 year. Final ≈?","opts":["$2165","$2160","$2080","$2160.50"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_har1618","section":"QR","topic":"harm","difficulty":4,"q":"Driving 60 mi at 20 mph then 60 mi at 60 mph. Average speed?","opts":["30","1200","40","80"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_neg1331","section":"QR","topic":"neg-ineq","difficulty":4,"q":"Solve: -4x < 6","opts":["x = -1.5","x > -1.5","x > 1.5","x < -1.5"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_pem1296","section":"QR","topic":"pemdas","difficulty":2,"q":"Evaluate: 8 + 3 × 4","opts":["44","28","4","20"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_pct1157","section":"QR","topic":"pct-chg","difficulty":3,"q":"A $89 item is decreased by 45%. New price?","opts":["44","48.95","134","40.05"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_dis1275","section":"QR","topic":"distrib","difficulty":2,"q":"Solve: 3(x - 2) = -3","opts":["0","2","-1","1"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_r4","section":"QR","topic":"ratios","difficulty":2,"q":"A map's scale is 1 inch = 50 miles. Two cities are 4.5 inches apart. Real distance?","opts":["225 mi","45 mi","11 mi","450 mi"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_ds1693","section":"QR","topic":"ds","difficulty":3,"q":"Slope from (4,7) to (-1,4)?","opts":["0.6","-8","1.67","-0.6"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_vol1679","section":"QR","topic":"vol-sa","difficulty":4,"q":"Cube with side 10. Find surface area.","opts":["600","1006","100","400"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_wor1498","section":"QR","topic":"word-decode","difficulty":3,"q":"The sum of a number and 5 times the number is 60. Find the number.","opts":["10","15","9","11"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_mix1412","section":"QR","topic":"mixture","difficulty":3,"q":"Mix 200mL of 40% with 150mL of 15%. Final concentration?","opts":["27.5","102.5","29.29","96.25"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_abs1733","section":"QR","topic":"absval","difficulty":3,"q":"Solve: |x − 5| = 3","opts":["8 or 2","8","5 ± 3","-2 or 2"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_dis1597","section":"QR","topic":"discr","difficulty":3,"q":"For 1x² + 5x + 0 = 0, how many real roots?","opts":["two real roots","one real root","no real roots","two imaginary roots"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_pct1186","section":"QR","topic":"pct-seq","difficulty":4,"q":"$80 → -40% → -10%. Final?","opts":["48","43.2","40","72"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_pem1307","section":"QR","topic":"pemdas","difficulty":3,"q":"Evaluate: 6 × 6 − 3 × 5","opts":["21","165","4","90"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_pct1155","section":"QR","topic":"pct-chg","difficulty":3,"q":"A $318 item is decreased by 33%. New price?","opts":["351","285","104.94","213.06"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_dst4","section":"QR","topic":"distrib","difficulty":3,"q":"4(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) = ?","opts":["x + 11","x + 5","7x + 11","x + 7"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_rat1348","section":"QR","topic":"ratios","difficulty":3,"q":"In a 3:6 ratio totaling 18, what's the smaller part?","opts":["7","12","13","6"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_ds1699","section":"QR","topic":"ds","difficulty":4,"q":"Slope from (-2,12) to (5,6)?","opts":["1","-1.17","0.86","-0.86"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_pem1304","section":"QR","topic":"pemdas","difficulty":3,"q":"Evaluate: 7 + 4 × 5","opts":["33","13","55","27"],"correct":3}],"ochem":[{"id":"OC_alc142","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alc-eth","difficulty":3,"q":"A target ether is to be made by Williamson ether synthesis. SN2 backside attack on the alkyl halide by the alkoxide must outpace E2 elimination, so steric crowding on the electrophile must be minimized. Which combination is preferred?","opts":["Primary alkyl halide + alkoxide nucleophile","Secondary alkyl halide + carboxylic acid","Tertiary alkyl halide + alcohol","Aldehyde + alcohol","Aryl chloride + sodium methoxide"],"correct":0,"why":"The competition between SN2 (gives ether) and E2 (gives alkene) is decided by sterics on the electrophile: bulkier halides shift the balance toward elimination. A primary alkyl halide minimizes that crowding, letting SN2 win cleanly. Aryl halides cannot undergo SN2 because the sp2 carbon and ring geometry block backside attack.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec024","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":3,"q":"Solvolysis of 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane in 80% aqueous acetone follows rate = k[RX] with rearranged product detected. Which step controls the rate of this SN1 process?","opts":["Backside attack of water on the alkyl halide","Heterolysis of the C-Cl bond producing the tertiary carbocation","Concerted loss of HCl","Capture of the cation by water","Migration of a methyl group across the C-C bond"],"correct":1,"why":"Independence of rate from nucleophile concentration, plus carbocation rearrangement, points to ionization as the slow step. Once the cation forms, rearrangement and trapping happen quickly in non-rate-determining steps.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_sp016","section":"OCHEM","topic":"spec-ir-nmr","difficulty":3,"q":"A 13C NMR spectrum has a peak at 205 ppm with no peaks between 165-180 ppm. Which functional group accounts for the 205 ppm signal?","opts":["Ester carbonyl (~165-175 ppm)","Aromatic ring carbon (~125-150 ppm)","Alkene carbon (~110-150 ppm)","Aldehyde or ketone C=O (~190-210 ppm)","Carboxylic acid C=O (~170-180 ppm)"],"correct":3,"why":"Aldehyde and ketone carbonyls appear furthest downfield in 13C, around 190-210 ppm. Esters, amides, and acids fall around 165-180 ppm because the heteroatom donates electron density to the carbonyl carbon, reducing deshielding.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_ab005","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":3,"q":"A terminal alkyne C-H has pKa ~25, while a vinyl alkene C-H has pKa ~44. Both involve C-H deprotonation to leave a carbanion on the same atom (carbon). The 19-unit gap is best assigned to which CARDIO factor?","opts":["Resonance through the pi system","Charge — different formal charges on the conjugate bases","Orbital hybridization: sp (50% s) holds electrons closer to the nucleus than sp2 (33% s), stabilizing the carbanion","Different atom (atom is the same)","Induction from extra hydrogens on the vinyl carbon"],"correct":2,"why":"Same atom, no resonance involved on these C-H bonds, so the deciding factor is hybridization. Higher s-character means electrons are held closer to the nucleus, stabilizing the lone pair: alkyne (sp) is more acidic than alkene (sp2).","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_ste036","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":1,"q":"At a stereocenter, the lowest-priority substituent points away from the viewer. The 1->2->3 priority arc, viewed from the opposite side, sweeps clockwise. What is the configuration?","opts":["Meso (no configuration can be assigned)","Achiral; not a true stereocenter","S","R","E (alkene geometry)"],"correct":3,"why":"When the lowest-priority group is oriented away, the visible 1->2->3 rotation is read directly. Clockwise corresponds to R; counterclockwise would be S.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_aro110","section":"OCHEM","topic":"arom-eas","difficulty":2,"q":"Benzene is treated with one equivalent of Br2 in the presence of a catalytic amount of FeBr3 at 30 degC. Which product is consistent with electrophilic aromatic substitution under these polar conditions?","opts":["Benzyl bromide (from radical bromination)","Bromobenzene","1,2-dibromobenzene","1,2-dibromocyclohexane (from electrophilic addition)","p-bromophenol"],"correct":1,"q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts_smiles":["BrCc1ccccc1","Brc1ccccc1",null,null],"why":"Br2 alone is too weakly electrophilic for benzene, but FeBr3 forms a Br-Br-FeBr3 complex with much greater Br+ character. Benzene attacks, the sigma complex loses H+, and aromaticity returns, giving bromobenzene. Polar/ionic conditions favor EAS over radical (no light/peroxides), and aromaticity prevents addition products like dibromocyclohexane.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec025","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":3,"q":"2-Iodopentane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide in t-BuOH at 65 degrees C. The major alkene is 1-pentene rather than 2-pentene. Which mechanism best explains this regiochemical outcome?","opts":["SN2 substitution with retention","E2 favoring the Hofmann (less substituted) product due to the bulky base","E1 favoring the Zaitsev product through a secondary carbocation","SN1 ionization with later proton loss to the more substituted face","Radical chain elimination"],"correct":1,"why":"Bulky strong bases force the elimination toward the less hindered beta-hydrogen, giving the less substituted Hofmann alkene. The reaction is concerted (E2) because the base is strong and the substrate is secondary.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_lb032","section":"OCHEM","topic":"lab","difficulty":4,"q":"1-butanol is independently treated with (a) PCC in dry CH2Cl2 and (b) Jones reagent (CrO3/H2SO4 aq). Which reagent stops cleanly at the aldehyde, and why?","opts":["Both stop at the aldehyde","Jones — aqueous conditions favor aldehyde","PCC — anhydrous, mild Cr(VI) reagent that cannot form the aldehyde hydrate, so no over-oxidation to carboxylic acid","Neither — both go to the acid","Only KMnO4 stops at aldehyde"],"correct":2,"why":"Over-oxidation of an aldehyde requires hydration of the C=O to a gem-diol, which Cr(VI) then re-oxidizes to a carboxylic acid. PCC is run in anhydrous CH2Cl2, so the aldehyde never hydrates and the reaction halts cleanly. Jones is aqueous and proceeds to the acid.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_ab003","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":4,"q":"Phenol (PhOH, pKa ~10) is roughly 6 pKa units more acidic than cyclohexanol (pKa ~16), even though both bear an O-H on an sp3-hybridized neighbor. The 6-unit gap is best ascribed to:","opts":["The more electronegative atom in phenol","Different formal charges on the conjugate bases","Resonance: phenoxide delocalizes negative charge into the aromatic ring across ortho/para positions","Inductive donation from the methylene groups in cyclohexanol","Solvent effects in water only"],"correct":2,"q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","why":"Phenoxide's negative charge is delocalized through the aromatic pi system to the ortho and para carbons by resonance, dramatically stabilizing it. Cyclohexanolate has no such delocalization, so its O-H is much less acidic.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_ste042","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":2,"q":"(2R,3S)-Tartaric acid contains two stereocenters but possesses an internal mirror plane between C2 and C3. Pure samples do not rotate polarized light. Which stereochemical classification applies?","opts":["An enantiomer of itself","A meso compound","A diastereomer of (2R,3S) without any symmetry","A racemic mixture by default","A constitutional isomer"],"correct":1,"why":"An internal mirror plane between the two stereocenters makes the molecule superimposable on its mirror image. It is achiral despite containing chirality centers, which is the definition of a meso compound.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_cb048","section":"OCHEM","topic":"carbonyl","difficulty":3,"q":"Methylenetriphenylphosphorane (Ph3P=CH2, an unstabilized Wittig ylide) is added to cyclohexanone in THF at room temperature. Identify the products.","opts":["An alkene (methylenecyclohexane) plus triphenylphosphine oxide (Ph3P=O); Wittig replaces C=O with C=C","An ester via Baeyer-Villiger","An acetal","A tertiary alcohol","A carboxylic acid"],"correct":0,"why":"The Wittig reaction joins the carbonyl carbon to the ylide carbon and ejects the oxygen as Ph3P=O via a four-membered oxaphosphetane, replacing C=O with C=C. Unstabilized ylides typically give the Z-alkene; on a ketone like cyclohexanone, geometry doesn't apply.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec023","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":3,"q":"(S)-2-Bromopentane is heated with potassium tert-butoxide in tert-butanol at 60 degrees C. The major product is the less substituted alkene 1-pentene rather than 2-pentene. Which mechanism is responsible?","opts":["SN2 displacement by tert-butoxide","SN1 with later isomerization","E2 favoring the Hofmann (less substituted) alkene because the bulky base targets the most accessible beta-H","E1 favoring the Zaitsev alkene through cation intermediacy","Radical elimination via tert-butoxyl radical"],"correct":2,"why":"Bulky bases like t-BuOK can't fit at the more crowded internal beta-H, so they remove the more accessible terminal beta-H. The result is concerted bimolecular E2 that disobeys Zaitsev and gives the Hofmann alkene.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_lab248","section":"OCHEM","topic":"lab","difficulty":1,"q":"An ether solution contains a neutral hydrocarbon and a small amount of a carboxylic acid impurity (pKa approximately 4-5). To selectively remove the acid into the aqueous layer without protonating any neutral organics, which aqueous wash should be used?","opts":["Wash with aqueous NaHCO3, which deprotonates only the acid; the carboxylate moves to the aqueous layer","Wash with 1 M HCl, which protonates any basic functional groups","Wash with saturated brine (NaCl) and discard the organic layer","Distill the entire mixture and collect the lower-boiling fraction","Wash with 1 M NaOH, which would also deprotonate phenols and any other weak acids present"],"correct":0,"opts_smiles":["Cl[H]",null,null,null],"why":"Sodium bicarbonate (pKa of H2CO3 approximately 6.4) is basic enough to deprotonate carboxylic acids but not phenols (pKa approximately 10). Selective deprotonation converts only the acid to its water-soluble carboxylate, leaving the neutral hydrocarbon in the organic layer.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_abo269","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":1,"q":"In the gas phase (with solvation effects removed), arrange the following from STRONGEST to WEAKEST base toward a proton: an alkyl anion (R3C-), hydroxide (OH-), fluoride (F-), and ammonia (NH3).","opts":["OH- > F- > NH3 > alkyl","F- > OH- > NH3 > alkyl","Alkyl > NH3 > OH- > F- (gas phase)","NH3 > alkyl > OH- > F-","All four are equivalent bases"],"correct":2,"why":"Across a period, electronegativity rises (C < N < O < F), so the negative charge is least tolerated on carbon and most tolerated on fluorine. The carbanion is therefore the strongest base, and fluoride the weakest.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_ste046","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":3,"q":"(2R,3S)-2,3-dichlorobutane contains two stereocenters but is internally symmetric: a mirror plane runs perpendicular to the C2-C3 bond. The neat liquid has zero optical rotation. Which stereochemical descriptor fits?","opts":["Diastereomer with no symmetry","Racemic mixture","Enantiomer of itself","Meso compound","Atropisomer"],"correct":3,"why":"An internal plane of symmetry makes the molecule superimposable on its mirror image; the optical rotations from each chiral half cancel. Such a single stereoisomer with no net chirality is a meso compound.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_cb047","section":"OCHEM","topic":"carbonyl","difficulty":4,"q":"Phenylmagnesium bromide (PhMgBr) is added to acetonitrile (CH3CN) in dry ether at 0 degC, then the reaction mixture is treated with aqueous H3O+. The final product is:","opts":["A primary amine (PhCH2NH2)","An aryl alkyl ketone (PhCOCH3, acetophenone) — addition gives an N-magnesiomide that hydrolyzes to a ketone on aqueous workup","A tertiary alcohol","A carboxylic acid","An amide"],"correct":1,"why":"Grignards add once to nitriles, giving an N-Mg ketimine intermediate. Aqueous acidic workup hydrolyzes the imine to a ketone. The carbon from the nitrile becomes the carbonyl carbon, and the alkyl/aryl from the nitrile becomes one substituent.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec021","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":3,"q":"An SN1 reaction of 2-bromo-2-methylbutane in 80% aqueous ethanol shows first-order kinetics in the substrate and zero-order dependence on water. A 1,2-hydride shift is observed in part of the product. Which step is rate-limiting?","opts":["Attack of water on the carbocation","Heterolysis of the C-Br bond producing a tertiary carbocation","A single concerted bimolecular displacement","Loss of a beta-H from the alkyl halide","Methyl migration to form a more stable cation"],"correct":1,"why":"First-order kinetics with no nucleophile dependence and observable rearrangement both diagnose carbocation formation as the slow step. After ionization, rearrangement and trapping by water occur in fast subsequent steps.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_lb027","section":"OCHEM","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"You need to separate two miscible organic liquids by simple distillation at atmospheric pressure. The technique works most cleanly when:","opts":["Boiling points are within 5 degC of each other","Boiling points differ by more than ~25 degC, allowing the lower-boiling component to vaporize selectively","Both components have identical boiling points (azeotrope)","Neither liquid boils below its decomposition temperature","One is solid at room temperature"],"correct":1,"why":"Simple distillation relies on a meaningful vapor-pressure difference. If the boiling points differ by less than ~25 degC, the vapor over the boiling mixture is too rich in both components and a fractional column is required for clean separation.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_abo276","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":2,"q":"In the gas phase (intrinsic basicity, no solvent), rank an alkyl anion, NH3, OH-, and F-. Use periodic-trend reasoning across a row of the periodic table.","opts":["OH- > F- > NH3 > alkyl","Alkyl > NH3 > OH- > F- (less electronegative atom = stronger base)","All equally basic","F- > OH- > NH3 > alkyl","NH3 > F- > OH- > alkyl"],"correct":1,"why":"Across a row, electronegativity climbs (C < N < O < F), and a less electronegative atom destabilizes negative charge. The carbanion is therefore the strongest base; fluoride is the weakest.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_xp005","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":4,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation","q":"A molecule has three stereocenters and ONE internal mirror plane that bisects the central C and exchanges the other two stereocenters. How many distinct stereoisomers does the molecule have, and how many of them are optically active?","opts":["8 total; all 8 optically active","4 total (one of which is meso); 2 optically active","6 total; 4 optically active","2 total; both optically active","4 total; all optically active"],"correct":1,"why":"Internal mirror plane reduces 2³ = 8 to 4 unique configurations. Pairs related by the mirror plane collapse: (RRR ↔ SSS pair active enantiomers), (RSR = meso, achiral), (SRS = meso, achiral). So 4 distinct stereoisomers, 2 optically active."},{"id":"OC_alk064","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":2,"q":"Propene is treated with BH3 in THF at 0 degrees C, then with H2O2 and aqueous NaOH. Predict the major product, including its regiochemistry relative to the original double bond.","opts":["2-Propanol via Markovnikov hydration","1-Propanol; anti-Markovnikov, syn addition of H and OH","Propane via exhaustive reduction","Propanal via oxidative cleavage","1,2-Propanediol via syn dihydroxylation"],"correct":1,"q_smiles":"CC=C","opts_smiles":["CC(O)C","CCCO","CCC","CCC=O"],"why":"Hydroboration places boron on the less hindered (terminal) carbon with syn delivery of B and H. Oxidative workup converts B to OH with retention of configuration, giving anti-Markovnikov 1-propanol.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec015","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":2,"q":"A kinetic study of dehydrohalogenation of 2-bromobutane with NaOMe in MeOH reveals rate = k[2-bromobutane][NaOMe]. Which elimination mechanism includes base in its rate-determining step?","opts":["Neither E1 nor E2 has base in the rate law","E1, because base attacks the carbocation","E2, because base removes the beta-H in the slow concerted step","Both have base in the rate law","Only E1cb mechanisms show this dependence"],"correct":2,"why":"Bimolecular kinetics imply a concerted single-step transition state involving both substrate and base. That is E2: removal of the beta-H, formation of the pi bond, and loss of the leaving group all occur together.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_lb024","section":"OCHEM","topic":"lab","difficulty":3,"q":"The iodoform test (I2 + aqueous NaOH) gives a yellow CHI3 precipitate. A positive result indicates the substrate contains either a CH3-C(=O)- group or a CH3-CH(OH)- group. Which class of compounds gives a positive iodoform test?","opts":["Any ketone, regardless of substitution","Methyl ketones (and acetaldehyde) plus methyl carbinols (CH3-CH(OH)-R) that are oxidized in situ","All carbonyl compounds, including esters and amides","Carboxylic acids only","Terminal alkynes"],"correct":1,"why":"The iodoform reaction tri-iodinates the alpha-methyl of a methyl ketone (or methyl carbinol oxidized first). NaOH then cleaves the C-CI3 bond, releasing yellow CHI3 (iodoform). Substrates without a -COCH3 (or -CH(OH)CH3) cannot form CHI3.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_ak036","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":3,"q":"Cold, dilute aqueous KMnO4 (basic conditions, ~0 degC) is added to cyclohexene. The major organic product is:","opts":["Two separate carbonyl compounds (oxidative cleavage)","cis-cyclohexane-1,2-diol (a syn diol from concerted [3+2] addition of MnO4-)","trans-cyclohexane-1,2-diol (anti from epoxide opening)","Cyclohexene oxide (epoxide)","Cyclohexanone"],"correct":1,"opts_smiles":[null,null,null,"C1CO1"],"why":"Cold, dilute KMnO4 forms a cyclic manganate ester across one face of the alkene, then hydrolyzes to deliver both OH groups to the same face — a syn-diol. Hot/concentrated KMnO4 instead cleaves to carbonyls.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_arr008","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-arrows","difficulty":3,"q":"Benzene + Br2 / FeBr3. The mechanism shows electrophile generation followed by aromatic π-attack. Which set of arrows correctly describes BOTH steps?","q_svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 160\" width=\"360\" height=\"160\"><style>\n  .bond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .dbond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .atom { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; fill: #1a1d20; text-anchor: middle; }\n  .lp   { fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .chg  { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .arrow { stroke: #cf3b3b; stroke-width: 1.8; fill: none; }\n  .arrowhead { fill: #cf3b3b; }\n  .lbl { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #6a737a; }\n</style><defs>\n  <marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" class=\"arrowhead\"/>\n  </marker>\n  <marker id=\"ah-half\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5\" class=\"arrowhead\" stroke=\"#cf3b3b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  </marker>\n</defs>\n    <!-- benzene -->\n    <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"80\" r=\"22\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1a1d20\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"80,58 100,68 100,92 80,102 60,92 60,68\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1a1d20\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <!-- Br-Br --- FeBr3 -->\n    <text x=\"160\" y=\"80\" class=\"atom\">Br</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"172\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <text x=\"208\" y=\"80\" class=\"atom\">Br</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"80\" class=\"atom\">FeBr3</text>\n    <!-- arrow from Br-Br to FeBr3 -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 200 70 Q 220 50 250 70\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <!-- arrow from benzene pi to Br+ -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 90 60 Q 130 30 165 65\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"140\" class=\"lbl\">Step 1: Br2 + FeBr3 → Br+ · Step 2: arene π attacks Br+ (loses aromaticity)</text>\n  </svg>","opts":["2 arrows: Br–Br to FeBr3 generating Br+, then arene π to Br+ forming sigma complex","1 arrow: arene π attacks Br2 directly","3 arrows: arene π to Br, Br–Br to FeBr3, FeBr3 to Br","No arrows — radical mechanism"],"correct":0,"why":"EAS = combined mechanism. Step 1 generates Br+ via Lewis-acid activation. Step 2 arene π attacks the electrophile, breaking aromaticity at the sigma-complex.","_ada_category":"Mechanisms","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1"},{"id":"OC_sp021","section":"OCHEM","topic":"spec-ir-nmr","difficulty":4,"q":"An IR spectrum of an aromatic unknown shows two strong peaks at ~1530 cm-1 and ~1350 cm-1 (sometimes called the ‘vampire-tooth’ pair). Which substituent is most consistent with this pattern?","opts":["Nitro group (NO2): asymmetric stretch ~1500-1570 + symmetric stretch ~1300-1380","Carbonyl C=O (~1700)","O-H (broad, 3200-3600)","C=C alkene (~1640)","C≡C (~2100-2200)"],"correct":0,"why":"Nitro groups have characteristic asymmetric and symmetric N-O stretches that appear as two strong bands near 1530 and 1350 cm-1. No other common group produces this distinctive ‘two-tooth’ pattern.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_cb042","section":"OCHEM","topic":"carbonyl","difficulty":4,"q":"An aldehyde is stirred with two equivalents of methanol in the presence of a catalytic acid (p-TsOH) under Dean-Stark conditions to remove water. Which functional group is the final isolated product?","opts":["Hemi-acetal (only one MeOH added)","Acetal (R-CH(OMe)2, sp3 C bonded to two -OMe groups)","Enol ether","Imine","Methyl ester via Tishchenko-like rearrangement"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed addition of one equivalent of ROH gives the hemiacetal; loss of water (driven by the Dean-Stark trap) and a second ROH addition installs the second alkoxy group, yielding an acetal. Acetals are stable under base and serve as carbonyl protecting groups.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_arr002","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-arrows","difficulty":2,"q":"2-Bromobutane + KOEt (concentrated, hot). Arrows show concerted E2. What is the major Zaitsev product?","q_svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 160\" width=\"360\" height=\"160\"><style>\n  .bond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .dbond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .atom { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; fill: #1a1d20; text-anchor: middle; }\n  .lp   { fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .chg  { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .arrow { stroke: #cf3b3b; stroke-width: 1.8; fill: none; }\n  .arrowhead { fill: #cf3b3b; }\n  .lbl { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #6a737a; }\n</style><defs>\n  <marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" class=\"arrowhead\"/>\n  </marker>\n  <marker id=\"ah-half\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5\" class=\"arrowhead\" stroke=\"#cf3b3b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  </marker>\n</defs>\n    <!-- 2-bromobutane skeletal -->\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"40\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"80\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"100\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"120\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"120\" class=\"atom\">Br</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"120\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"115\"/>\n    <!-- base -->\n    <text x=\"220\" y=\"60\" class=\"atom\">EtO</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"54\" class=\"chg\">⊖</text>\n    <!-- arrow from EtO to H on adjacent C -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 225 70 Q 180 60 110 76\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <!-- arrow from C-H to C-C bond (forming pi) -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 105 90 Q 100 95 95 90\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <!-- arrow from C-Br to Br -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 130 100 Q 145 115 130 125\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"140\" class=\"lbl\">E2: anti-periplanar, single concerted step</text>\n  </svg>","opts":["1-butene (Hofmann)","cis-2-butene","2-butene (Zaitsev, mainly trans)","butane"],"opts_smiles":["CCC=C","C/C=C\\C","C/C=C/C","CCCC"],"correct":2,"why":"Strong base + concentrated conditions favor E2 with Zaitsev orientation. Anti-periplanar geometry favors trans.","_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_aci174","section":"OCHEM","topic":"acids","difficulty":1,"q":"Propanoic acid is heated with excess ethanol and a catalytic amount of concentrated H2SO4 at reflux for several hours. What is the name of this reaction and what are the products?","opts":["Saponification, giving a carboxylate salt and an alcohol","Fischer esterification, giving an ester (ethyl propanoate) + H2O","Acidic hydrolysis, giving a carboxylic acid and an alcohol","Aldol addition, giving a beta-hydroxy ester","Claisen condensation, giving a beta-keto ester"],"correct":1,"opts_smiles":[null,"O",null,null],"why":"Carboxylic acid + alcohol with H+ catalyst at reflux is the classic Fischer esterification: H+ activates the carbonyl, the alcohol attacks, and a tetrahedral intermediate collapses with loss of water. Excess alcohol pushes the reversible equilibrium forward (Le Chatelier). The products are the corresponding ester (ethyl propanoate) and water.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_ak039","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":2,"q":"1-methylcyclohexene is subjected to hydroboration-oxidation: BH3·THF at 0 degC, then aqueous NaOH/H2O2. What is the major alcohol, including stereochemistry?","opts":["1-methylcyclohexanol (Markovnikov OH at the more substituted C)","trans-2-methylcyclohexanol (anti-Markovnikov OH on the less hindered C, syn addition of B and H places OH and CH3 trans)","cis-2-methylcyclohexanol","3-methylcyclohexanol","trans-1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration is anti-Markovnikov (boron goes to the less substituted C) and syn (B and H on the same face). Oxidative workup retains stereochemistry, replacing B with OH. On 1-methylcyclohexene this places OH on C2 trans to the C1 methyl.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"}]}},{"id":"MOCK_3","name":"Practice Test 3","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"BIO_cm050","section":"BIO","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"Restriction enzymes such as EcoRI, BamHI, and HindIII are used in molecular cloning to cut DNA at predictable, sequence-specific locations. EcoRI, for example, cuts at the sequence 5'-GAATTC-3'/3'-CTTAAG-5'. What general type of recognition sequence do most restriction enzymes recognize?","opts":["Random sites with no sequence specificity","Palindromic sequences (sequences that read the same 5' → 3' on both strands)","Methylated bases that mark host DNA from foreign DNA","Only GC-rich repeat regions","Telomeric (TTAGGG) repeats at chromosome ends"],"correct":1,"why":"Most type II restriction enzymes recognize palindromic sequences (e.g., GAATTC on one strand, CTTAAG on the other — same 5' → 3' on both). This palindromic geometry lets the enzyme bind and cut both strands symmetrically. Bacteria use methylation of their own DNA as the strategy to protect themselves from their own restriction enzymes.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm120","section":"BIO","topic":"cell-types","difficulty":2,"q":"Some bacteria (e.g., Bacillus, Clostridium) survive harsh environmental conditions by forming highly resistant dormant structures. These structures contain dehydrated cytoplasm, a thick protein coat, and DPA-Ca²⁺ in their core, allowing them to withstand boiling, desiccation, and UV radiation for years. What are these structures called?","opts":["Spore-forming protists, with motile flagella","Bacterial endospores — dormant, highly resistant survival forms produced inside the parent cell during nutrient deprivation","Eukaryotic seeds, which are multicellular","Viral particles released during lytic infection","Capsule polysaccharide layers around growing cells"],"correct":1,"why":"Endospores are formed within the parent cell in response to starvation. The DPA-Ca²⁺ core, multiple protein coats, and dehydrated cytoplasm make endospores extraordinarily resistant — Clostridium botulinum spores survive boiling. Pressure-cooking (autoclaving at 121°C) is required to kill them. Anthrax (Bacillus anthracis) spreads via these durable spores.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm080","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"Epithelial cells lining the intestine are joined by specialized intercellular junctions that prevent water and small solutes from leaking between adjacent cells. As a result, all material crossing the epithelium must pass through the cells (transcellular route), allowing tightly regulated absorption. Which junction creates this barrier?","opts":["Gap junction — allows direct passage of small molecules between cells","Tight junction — claudins/occludins form a band that seals adjacent cells, preventing paracellular leakage","Desmosome — anchors cells via intermediate filaments for mechanical stability","Adherens junction — links actin filaments via cadherins","Plasmodesmata — found in plant cell walls, not animals"],"correct":1,"why":"Tight junctions (zonula occludens) are belts of claudin and occludin proteins encircling each cell. They form an impermeable seal between adjacent cells, blocking the paracellular pathway. This forces solutes to cross transcellularly, allowing regulated, polarized absorption (e.g., glucose via apical SGLT, basolateral GLUT).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm035","section":"BIO","topic":"photo","difficulty":2,"q":"The light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis use absorbed light energy to drive water splitting and electron transport, which generates a proton gradient and reduces a coenzyme. The two products that are passed to the Calvin cycle to power CO2 fixation are:","opts":["Glucose and oxygen, the final outputs of photosynthesis","ATP and NADPH","NADH and FADH2, the same as the mitochondrial inputs to the ETC","CO2 and H2O, supplied by atmospheric exchange","Pyruvate and acetyl-CoA, intermediates of carbon fixation"],"correct":1,"why":"Light reactions output ATP (made by chloroplast ATP synthase using the proton gradient) and NADPH (formed when NADP+ is reduced at PSI). Both fuel the Calvin cycle: ATP provides phosphorylation energy, NADPH provides reducing power for CO2 fixation. NADH and FADH2 are mitochondrial coenzymes, not chloroplast products.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm060","section":"BIO","topic":"proteins","difficulty":2,"q":"β-pleated sheets, the second major secondary-structure element after the α-helix, consist of two or more strands of polypeptide running side by side (parallel or antiparallel). The strands are held together by regular non-covalent interactions between backbone atoms. Which interaction stabilizes the β-sheet?","opts":["Disulfide bridges between cysteines on adjacent strands","Backbone hydrogen bonds between the carbonyl C=O of one strand and the amide N-H of the adjacent strand","R-group ionic bonds between charged side chains","Hydrophobic clustering of nonpolar side chains across strands","Aromatic π-stacking between phenylalanine R-groups"],"correct":1,"why":"β-sheets are stabilized by backbone hydrogen bonds running across strands — the carbonyl C=O of one polypeptide's backbone H-bonds with the amide N-H of the adjacent strand. R-groups can fine-tune stability but the structural framework is backbone H-bonding (analogous to α-helices but laid out side-by-side rather than coiled).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm126","section":"BIO","topic":"glycolysis","difficulty":2,"q":"Glycolysis produces a small amount of ATP directly via substrate-level phosphorylation, where a high-energy phosphorylated intermediate transfers its phosphate directly to ADP. Two specific glycolytic enzymes carry out this direct ATP-generating step. Which enzymes catalyze substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis?","opts":["Hexokinase, which phosphorylates glucose using ATP (consumes, doesn't produce)","Phosphofructokinase-1, which converts fructose-6-P to fructose-1,6-bisP using ATP (consumes)","Phosphoglycerate kinase (PGK) and pyruvate kinase — both transfer phosphate from a high-energy intermediate to ADP, generating ATP","Aldolase, which splits fructose-1,6-bisP into two trioses (no ATP)","Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, which produces NADH (not ATP)"],"correct":2,"why":"Substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis happens at two steps: PGK transfers phosphate from 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to ADP, making ATP and 3-phosphoglycerate; pyruvate kinase transfers phosphate from phosphoenolpyruvate to ADP. Each step happens twice per glucose (once per triose), giving 4 ATP gross output, minus 2 ATP invested earlier = 2 ATP net.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm139","section":"BIO","topic":"organelles","difficulty":2,"q":"Skeletal muscle cells contain a specialized form of smooth endoplasmic reticulum that stores calcium ions at high concentration. When an action potential reaches the muscle fiber, calcium is rapidly released into the cytoplasm to trigger contraction. What is this specialized organelle called?","opts":["Mitochondrial reticulum, used for ATP storage in muscle","Sarcoplasmic reticulum — the muscle-specific smooth ER, which stores Ca²⁺ and releases it through ryanodine receptors during excitation-contraction coupling","Cisternae, a generic term for ER stacks","T-tubules, which carry the action potential into the muscle fiber (a separate plasma-membrane structure)","Golgi apparatus modified for muscle"],"correct":1,"why":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) is a muscle-specific smooth ER that stores Ca²⁺ at ~1 mM (cytoplasmic resting [Ca²⁺] is ~100 nM). T-tubules invaginate from the plasma membrane to deliver action potentials deep into the fiber; voltage-sensors there trigger ryanodine receptors on the SR to release Ca²⁺, initiating contraction (excitation-contraction coupling).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm099","section":"BIO","topic":"replication","difficulty":2,"q":"DNA polymerase III in E. coli has very high fidelity (~1 error per 10⁷ bases) thanks to base-selectivity at the active site PLUS a built-in proofreading function. If a wrong nucleotide is incorporated, the polymerase backs up, removes the misincorporated base, and re-polymerizes. Which catalytic activity drives this proofreading?","opts":["5' → 3' exonuclease activity, also used to remove RNA primers","3' → 5' exonuclease activity, an intrinsic proofreading function that excises mispaired bases at the 3' end of the growing strand","Helicase activity, used to separate strands","Endonuclease activity, used to introduce double-strand breaks","Reverse transcriptase activity, found only in retroviruses"],"correct":1,"why":"DNA polymerase III has 3' → 5' exonuclease activity in a separate domain. When a wrong base is incorporated, the resulting mispairing distorts the active site; the polymerase moves the 3' end into the exonuclease site, removes the wrong nucleotide, and resumes synthesis. This proofreading lowers the error rate ~100-fold beyond base selectivity alone.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm128","section":"BIO","topic":"krebs","difficulty":2,"q":"The citric acid cycle generates ATP (or GTP) directly at one specific step via substrate-level phosphorylation. The high-energy thioester bond in succinyl-CoA is broken, and the released energy drives phosphorylation of GDP (or ADP). Which Krebs enzyme catalyzes this step?","opts":["Citrate synthase, which condenses acetyl-CoA with oxaloacetate (no ATP made)","α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, which produces NADH and CO₂","Succinyl-CoA synthetase (succinyl-CoA → succinate + GTP/ATP), the substrate-level phosphorylation step of the cycle","Malate dehydrogenase, which oxidizes malate → oxaloacetate (NADH made, no ATP)","Isocitrate dehydrogenase, the rate-limiting step (NADH and CO₂ made, no ATP)"],"correct":2,"why":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase couples thioester hydrolysis (succinyl-CoA → succinate + CoA) with phosphorylation of GDP → GTP (or ADP → ATP, in some isoforms). This is the only substrate-level phosphorylation in the Krebs cycle. The other ATP from oxidation comes through the ETC after NADH/FADH₂ are reoxidized.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm114","section":"BIO","topic":"operon","difficulty":2,"q":"The lac operon in E. coli contains three structural genes (lacZ, lacY, lacA) for lactose metabolism, plus a regulatory gene encoding a tetrameric protein that binds the operator and blocks transcription in the absence of lactose. Which gene encodes the lac repressor?","opts":["lacZ — encodes β-galactosidase, the enzyme that cleaves lactose","lacI — the regulatory gene encoding the tetrameric Lac repressor protein, located upstream of the operon","lacY — encodes lactose permease, which imports lactose into the cell","lacA — encodes thiogalactoside transacetylase, of secondary importance","The CAP-binding site, which is a regulatory DNA sequence (not a gene)"],"correct":1,"why":"lacI is the regulatory gene encoding the Lac repressor (a tetrameric protein). When lactose is absent, the repressor binds the operator and blocks transcription. When allolactose (an isomer of lactose) is present, it binds the repressor and removes it from DNA, allowing transcription. lacZ, lacY, lacA are the structural genes.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm138","section":"BIO","topic":"biomolecules","difficulty":2,"q":"Cholesterol biosynthesis takes place primarily in the liver. The pathway condenses two-carbon units from acetyl-CoA in a series of reactions, eventually forming the four-fused-ring sterol skeleton through the key intermediate squalene. What is the starting material for cholesterol synthesis?","opts":["Glucose, directly converted to the cholesterol ring","Amino acids, especially leucine","Acetyl-CoA — three molecules condense to form HMG-CoA, the precursor to mevalonate (the rate-limiting step) and ultimately squalene → cholesterol","Glycerol, derived from triglyceride breakdown","Inorganic carbonate"],"correct":2,"why":"Cholesterol is built from acetyl-CoA. Three acetyl-CoA → HMG-CoA → mevalonate (catalyzed by HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting step). The mevalonate pathway eventually produces squalene, which cyclizes to lanosterol and then cholesterol. Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase to lower blood cholesterol — a $30B/year drug class.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm056","section":"BIO","topic":"carbs","difficulty":2,"q":"An adult who experiences abdominal cramps, bloating, and diarrhea after consuming dairy products produces normal levels of pancreatic enzymes but lacks one specific brush-border enzyme. As a result, lactose passes undigested into the colon, where bacterial fermentation produces gas. Which enzyme is deficient?","opts":["Sucrase, which hydrolyzes sucrose into glucose + fructose","Maltase, which hydrolyzes maltose into two glucose molecules","Lactase, which hydrolyzes lactose into glucose + galactose","α-amylase, which cleaves starch into maltose and dextrins","Trypsin, a pancreatic protease unrelated to carbohydrates"],"correct":2,"why":"Lactase (β-galactosidase) cleaves the β-1,4 glycosidic bond between galactose and glucose in lactose. In lactose intolerance, lactase production declines after weaning (most adults worldwide are lactase-deficient). Undigested lactose passes to colonic bacteria, which ferment it producing the symptoms.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm045","section":"BIO","topic":"translation","difficulty":2,"q":"During translational elongation, an aminoacyl-tRNA delivers its amino acid to the ribosome. The tRNA enters the ribosome at one specific binding site, then moves through two more sites before being ejected. At which site does an incoming aminoacyl-tRNA FIRST bind?","opts":["A site (Aminoacyl) — the entry point for incoming aminoacyl-tRNAs paired with the next codon","P site (Peptidyl) — already holds the growing polypeptide chain","E site (Exit) — releases empty (deacylated) tRNAs from the ribosome","M site, an additional regulatory site","I site (Initiator) — used only by the initial methionyl-tRNA"],"correct":0,"why":"Incoming aminoacyl-tRNAs first bind the A site, where they pair with the next mRNA codon. Peptide-bond formation transfers the polypeptide to the new amino acid, and translocation moves the tRNA from A → P, while the previous P-site tRNA shifts to E for ejection.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm118","section":"BIO","topic":"eukaryotic-reg","difficulty":2,"q":"MicroRNAs (miRNAs) are short ~22-nucleotide non-coding RNAs that, after processing, are loaded into the RISC complex and guide the complex to complementary sites typically in the 3' UTR of target mRNAs. By what mechanism do miRNAs regulate gene expression?","opts":["By methylating cytosines in DNA (epigenetic silencing)","By base-pairing with target mRNAs and either accelerating their decay (perfect pairing) or blocking translation (imperfect pairing) via the RISC complex","By acetylating histones at target gene promoters","By splicing pre-mRNA into mature mRNA","By directly binding RNA polymerase II and inhibiting transcription"],"correct":1,"why":"miRNAs base-pair with sites in the 3' UTR (or other regions) of target mRNAs. The miRNA-RISC complex either cleaves the target mRNA (if pairing is near-perfect, plant-like) or represses translation and accelerates deadenylation (mammalian, imperfect pairing). One miRNA can target hundreds of mRNAs, so they sit at major regulatory hubs (e.g., let-7, miR-21).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm134","section":"BIO","topic":"enzymes","difficulty":2,"q":"In Michaelis-Menten kinetics, Vmax represents the maximum rate of a reaction when the enzyme is fully saturated with substrate. Doubling the substrate concentration above this point cannot increase the rate. What primarily determines Vmax in a given experiment?","opts":["Substrate concentration, since Vmax is reached at infinite [S]","Total enzyme concentration in the reaction, plus the enzyme's intrinsic kcat (turnover number); Vmax = kcat × [E]_total","Temperature only, with no enzyme dependence","Km (Michaelis constant), which is unrelated to Vmax","pH alone, regardless of enzyme amount"],"correct":1,"why":"Vmax = kcat × [E]_total. It depends on total enzyme concentration (more enzyme = more Vmax) and the enzyme's intrinsic turnover number (kcat). It does NOT depend on substrate concentration (as long as Vmax is approached). Increasing temperature can increase kcat to a point but eventually denatures the enzyme; pH affects kcat indirectly through residue protonation.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm150","section":"BIO","topic":"water","difficulty":2,"q":"Water (H₂O, MW 18) has a boiling point of 100 °C, while methane (CH₄, MW 16, similar size) boils at −161 °C. Even hydrogen sulfide (H₂S, MW 34, larger) boils at only −60 °C. Why does water have such an unusually high boiling point compared to molecules of similar size?","opts":["Larger molecular mass — water is heavier than methane (only ~2 g/mol heavier; doesn't explain a 261 °C difference)","Extensive hydrogen bonding between water molecules (H attached to highly electronegative O), which requires significant energy to break before water can vaporize","Ionic bonds between water molecules, like in NaCl","Branched molecular structure that resists evaporation","Water is denser than other liquids of similar size"],"correct":1,"why":"Water's high boiling point reflects its extensive hydrogen-bond network. Each water molecule can form up to four H-bonds with neighbors. Vaporizing water requires breaking enough of these bonds to release individual molecules to the gas phase — far more energy than dispersing methane (no H-bonds) or H₂S (much weaker H-bonds; sulfur is less electronegative).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm007","section":"BIO","topic":"lipids","difficulty":2,"q":"Two fatty acids of identical carbon-chain length are isolated from different food sources. Sample A is solid at 25°C (e.g., butter), while sample B is liquid at the same temperature (e.g., olive oil). Which structural difference best explains this physical-state difference?","opts":["Sample A is fully saturated (no C=C double bonds), allowing tight packing and a higher melting point","Sample A contains many cis double bonds, which kink the chain and reduce packing","Sample A is polyunsaturated with at least three C=C bonds","Sample A is phosphorylated at one of its carboxyl carbons","Sample A is composed of D-isomers, while sample B is composed of L-isomers"],"correct":0,"why":"Saturated fatty acids have no C=C double bonds, allowing straight chains to pack tightly via van der Waals interactions, which raises melting point — they are solid at room temperature. Unsaturated fats have cis double bonds that introduce kinks, preventing tight packing, so they remain liquid.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm103","section":"BIO","topic":"transcription","difficulty":2,"q":"After RNA polymerase II transcribes a eukaryotic gene, the pre-mRNA contains both protein-coding sequences and intervening non-coding sequences. A large ribonucleoprotein machine recognizes specific sequences at intron-exon boundaries (5' GU and 3' AG splice sites) and removes the non-coding regions, ligating the protein-coding pieces. What does the spliceosome remove?","opts":["Exons (protein-coding sequences) that interrupt regulatory regions","Introns — non-coding intervening sequences — leaving the exons joined","The poly-A tail at the 3' end of the mRNA","The 5' methylguanosine cap added during transcription","Both exons and introns indiscriminately"],"correct":1,"why":"The spliceosome (a complex of snRNPs U1, U2, U4, U5, U6) excises introns and joins exons. Splice sites are marked by GU at the 5' splice site and AG at the 3' splice site. Alternative splicing (using different exon combinations) lets one gene produce multiple protein isoforms — vastly expanding proteome diversity.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm131","section":"BIO","topic":"mitosis","difficulty":2,"q":"The spindle assembly checkpoint (SAC) prevents anaphase from beginning until every chromosome is properly attached to spindle fibers from opposite poles. Until that condition is satisfied, the SAC inhibits a key cell-cycle E3 ubiquitin ligase that would otherwise trigger anaphase by ubiquitinating securin and cyclin B. Which complex does the SAC inhibit?","opts":["Cyclin D-CDK4, which drives G1 progression","The anaphase-promoting complex/cyclosome (APC/C), the E3 ligase that, once active, ubiquitinates securin (releasing separase to cleave cohesin) and cyclin B (mitotic exit)","p53, the genome guardian","The retinoblastoma protein (Rb), which blocks G1/S progression","ATP synthase, the mitochondrial F0F1 enzyme"],"correct":1,"why":"The SAC inhibits APC/C^Cdc20 until all kinetochores are attached. Once attachment is complete, SAC release allows APC/C to ubiquitinate securin (whose destruction releases active separase, which cleaves cohesin) and cyclin B (mitotic exit). Loss of SAC function (e.g., Mad2 mutations) causes chromosomal instability, a hallmark of cancer.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm066","section":"BIO","topic":"nucleic","difficulty":2,"q":"RNA differs from DNA in three key ways: it is typically single-stranded, it uses uracil instead of thymine, and its sugar (ribose) carries an extra 2'-hydroxyl group. The 2'-OH has direct chemical consequences for RNA's stability and reactivity. Which is the most important consequence?","opts":["RNA is more chemically stable than DNA, allowing for long-term storage","RNA is less stable and more reactive — the 2'-OH can attack the adjacent phosphodiester bond, accelerating hydrolysis","The 2'-OH gives RNA a permanent positive charge","RNA molecules are larger than DNA molecules of the same length","The 2'-OH allows RNA to bind directly to membrane lipids"],"correct":1,"why":"The 2'-OH on ribose can act as an intramolecular nucleophile, attacking the adjacent 3'-phosphate and cleaving the phosphodiester backbone — especially at alkaline pH or with metal ions. This is why RNA is hydrolytically less stable than DNA, supporting RNA's role as a transient information molecule rather than a permanent genetic store.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm047","section":"BIO","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"PCR amplifies a specific DNA segment by cycling a reaction mixture (template, primers, dNTPs, Taq polymerase, buffer) through three temperatures. Each step has a specific role: separating the strands, annealing primers, and extending new DNA. Which sequence of approximate temperatures correctly describes one PCR cycle?","opts":["Denaturation 95°C → primer annealing 50-60°C → primer extension 72°C","Denaturation 60°C → annealing 95°C → extension 72°C","Extension 72°C → denaturation 95°C → annealing 55°C","37°C → 60°C → 90°C, optimized for non-thermophilic polymerases","All three steps performed at 72°C with varying durations"],"correct":0,"why":"PCR cycles: ~95°C separates the DNA strands (denaturation), ~50-60°C lets primers bind their target sequences (annealing), and ~72°C is the optimal temperature for Taq DNA polymerase to extend the primers (extension). The use of a thermostable polymerase (Taq, from Thermus aquaticus) is what makes repeated heating possible.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm019","section":"BIO","topic":"cell-types","difficulty":2,"q":"Mitochondria contain their own circular DNA and 70S ribosomes that are similar to those of bacteria. Mitochondria also divide independently of the cell by binary fission, and their double membrane is consistent with engulfment by an ancestral eukaryotic cell. These observations together provide the strongest evidence for which evolutionary theory?","opts":["The fluid-mosaic model of membrane structure","The endosymbiotic theory — mitochondria descended from an aerobic prokaryote engulfed by an ancestral eukaryote","Mendel's laws of segregation and independent assortment","The cell theory (all life is composed of cells)","The chemiosmotic hypothesis of ATP synthesis"],"correct":1,"why":"The endosymbiotic theory proposes that mitochondria (and chloroplasts) originated when an ancestral eukaryote engulfed a free-living aerobic bacterium. Their bacterial-like (70S) ribosomes, circular DNA, double membrane, and binary fission all support this origin. Cell theory and Mendel's laws address other levels of biology.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm147","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"Many cell-surface proteins are decorated with covalently attached oligosaccharide chains, forming glycoproteins. These sugars project into the extracellular space, where they participate in cell-cell interactions, antigen recognition, and protection from proteases. What is the primary functional role of these glycoproteins on cell surfaces?","opts":["Direct ATP synthesis at the plasma membrane","Cell-cell recognition (e.g., immune cell receptors, blood-group antigens) and signaling, plus protection of the protein backbone from proteolysis","DNA replication and chromosomal segregation","Direct ion transport across the bilayer (which requires channels or pumps, not glycoproteins per se)","Photosynthetic light absorption"],"correct":1,"why":"Surface glycoproteins (and glycolipids) form the glycocalyx and serve as cell-cell recognition tags. ABO blood groups are oligosaccharides on RBC glycoproteins. Selectins on leukocytes recognize sugars on endothelial cells. The sugars also protect the protein backbone from proteases and contribute to the immune system's distinction of self from non-self.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm034","section":"BIO","topic":"photo","difficulty":2,"q":"The light-independent reactions of photosynthesis (the Calvin cycle) fix CO2 into a 3-carbon sugar precursor (G3P) using ATP and NADPH that were generated by the light-dependent reactions. Where in the chloroplast does the Calvin cycle take place?","opts":["Thylakoid membrane, where pigment-protein complexes capture light","Thylakoid lumen, where the proton gradient accumulates","Stroma, the soluble fluid surrounding the thylakoids","Outer chloroplast membrane, in contact with the cytosol","Inner chloroplast membrane, the boundary of the stroma"],"correct":2,"why":"The Calvin cycle takes place in the stroma — the soluble compartment outside the thylakoids. RuBisCO and the other Calvin enzymes are dissolved here. The light reactions occur in the thylakoid membrane (electron transport) and lumen (proton accumulation), analogous to the mitochondrial cristae and intermembrane space.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm009","section":"BIO","topic":"proteins","difficulty":2,"q":"An enzyme is incubated at 95°C for 5 minutes. The protein loses all catalytic activity but, when re-analyzed by mass spectrometry, retains its original molecular mass and amino-acid sequence. Which level of protein structure has been preserved despite this denaturation?","opts":["Primary structure — the covalent peptide-bond sequence is unaffected by heat","Secondary structure — α-helices and β-sheets are stabilized by covalent bonds","Tertiary structure — the 3D fold is held only by covalent disulfide bonds","Quaternary structure — subunit interactions resist heat better than smaller folds","Both tertiary and quaternary, since only secondary structure is heat-sensitive"],"correct":0,"why":"Primary structure is the linear sequence of amino acids linked by covalent peptide bonds, which require strong proteolytic conditions (not heat) to break. Higher-order structures depend on weaker non-covalent interactions (H-bonds, van der Waals, ionic) that are disrupted by heat — so denaturation destroys 2°/3°/4° but preserves 1°.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm084","section":"BIO","topic":"glycolysis","difficulty":2,"q":"Hexokinase catalyzes the very first step of glycolysis: glucose + ATP → glucose-6-phosphate + ADP. This step is highly exergonic (ΔG ≈ −16.7 kJ/mol) and is considered functionally irreversible under cellular conditions. Why is this step physiologically important?","opts":["Because the cell lacks any enzyme that can reverse it","Because the strongly negative ΔG ensures unidirectional flow and traps glucose inside the cell as glucose-6-phosphate (which cannot leave via GLUT transporters), committing it to glycolysis","Because it requires two ATPs rather than one","Because it produces ATP rather than consuming it","Because hexokinase has no allosteric regulation"],"correct":1,"why":"Hexokinase is functionally irreversible because the ΔG is large and negative. Phosphorylation also traps glucose: G6P is charged and cannot diffuse out via GLUT transporters, committing the molecule to glycolysis. Cells reverse the step (during gluconeogenesis) using a different enzyme, glucose-6-phosphatase, present mainly in liver and kidney.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm073","section":"BIO","topic":"organelles","difficulty":2,"q":"Peroxisomes are small, single-membrane organelles that participate in fatty-acid β-oxidation (especially of very-long-chain fatty acids) and other metabolic reactions. A characteristic byproduct of these reactions is broken down within the peroxisome by an enzyme that gives the organelle its name. What dangerous byproduct is detoxified by peroxisomal catalase?","opts":["Ammonia (NH₃), which would be detoxified to urea instead","Hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂), broken down by catalase to water and oxygen","Lactic acid, which is metabolized in liver","Free radicals such as superoxide, broken down by superoxide dismutase","Hydrochloric acid, neutralized by buffering"],"correct":1,"why":"Peroxisomal oxidations (β-oxidation, amino-acid oxidation) produce hydrogen peroxide as a byproduct. Catalase converts 2 H₂O₂ → 2 H₂O + O₂, preventing oxidative damage. The organelle's name comes from this reaction. Superoxide is handled by separate SOD enzymes.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm041","section":"BIO","topic":"replication","difficulty":2,"q":"Because DNA polymerase synthesizes only 5' → 3' but the two strands of DNA are antiparallel, replication of the lagging strand proceeds discontinuously. Short DNA segments are synthesized away from the fork, each starting from an RNA primer. These segments are later joined into a continuous lagging strand. What are these segments called?","opts":["Continuous segments of leading-strand synthesis","Okazaki fragments — short pieces of newly synthesized lagging-strand DNA","RNA primers, which remain in the final product","Replication errors caused by polymerase slippage","Telomeric repeats added by telomerase"],"correct":1,"why":"Okazaki fragments are the short DNA segments (1-2 kb in eukaryotes) synthesized on the lagging strand. Each starts from an RNA primer made by primase, is extended by DNA polymerase, then has its primer removed and is ligated to the next fragment. The leading strand is synthesized continuously.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm086","section":"BIO","topic":"krebs","difficulty":2,"q":"One molecule of glucose enters glycolysis and is converted to 2 molecules of pyruvate. Each pyruvate enters the mitochondrial matrix, is decarboxylated by pyruvate dehydrogenase to acetyl-CoA, and the acetyl group enters the citric acid cycle. How many TIMES does the Krebs cycle run per glucose molecule?","opts":["Once — one glucose generates one acetyl-CoA","Twice — one glucose produces two pyruvate, each becoming one acetyl-CoA, each running the cycle once","Four times, since each pyruvate runs the cycle twice","Depends on oxygen availability — the cycle runs more times in low oxygen","Six times, once per carbon in glucose"],"correct":1,"why":"Glucose (6 carbons) → 2 pyruvate (3 carbons each) → 2 acetyl-CoA (2 carbons each, releasing 2 CO₂). Each acetyl-CoA enters the cycle once, so the cycle runs TWICE per glucose. Total per glucose: 6 NADH + 2 FADH₂ + 2 ATP/GTP + 4 CO₂ from the Krebs steps alone.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm115","section":"BIO","topic":"operon","difficulty":2,"q":"The lac operon is positively regulated by catabolite activator protein (CAP, also called CRP), which binds DNA only when complexed with cyclic AMP (cAMP). Glucose levels modulate cAMP via inverse regulation of adenylate cyclase. When does CAP-cAMP bind the lac promoter and activate transcription?","opts":["When glucose is HIGH (lots of preferred energy source available)","When glucose is LOW — low glucose causes cAMP to rise (no glucose-inhibition of adenylate cyclase), and CAP-cAMP then activates the lac promoter, allowing lactose use as a backup energy source","Glucose levels do not affect CAP function","Only when glucose and lactose are both equal in concentration","Only at very high temperature"],"correct":1,"why":"Glucose suppresses cAMP via catabolite repression. When glucose is LOW, cAMP rises, binds CAP, and CAP-cAMP binds the lac promoter to recruit RNA polymerase. This means the lac operon is expressed only when (1) lactose is present (repressor off) AND (2) glucose is low (CAP-cAMP on) — the cell uses glucose first.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm137","section":"BIO","topic":"biomolecules","difficulty":2,"q":"Fatty-acid catabolism oxidatively trims two-carbon acetyl-CoA units from the carboxyl end of an activated fatty acyl-CoA molecule. Each cycle generates one FADH₂, one NADH, and one acetyl-CoA. The pathway also requires functional mitochondria. Where does β-oxidation take place?","opts":["Cytoplasm, alongside glycolysis","Mitochondrial matrix (after fatty acids are imported via the carnitine shuttle), where β-oxidation enzymes reside","Endoplasmic reticulum, where fatty-acid synthesis occurs","Lysosome, the site of acid hydrolysis","Smooth ER, where lipid synthesis takes place"],"correct":1,"why":"β-oxidation occurs in the mitochondrial matrix (and to a smaller extent in peroxisomes, especially for very-long-chain fatty acids). Long-chain fatty acids must enter via the carnitine shuttle (carnitine palmitoyltransferase I/II), since CoA cannot cross the inner membrane. β-oxidation generates the acetyl-CoA that feeds the Krebs cycle, plus NADH/FADH₂ for the ETC.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm004","section":"BIO","topic":"carbs","difficulty":2,"q":"Starch and cellulose are both polymers of glucose, yet humans can digest starch but not cellulose. Cattle and termites overcome this limitation only by harboring symbiotic microbes that produce specific cellulases. What molecular feature accounts for this difference in human digestibility?","opts":["Cellulose contains a different monomer than starch","Starch uses α-1,4 glycosidic bonds, which human amylase can hydrolyze, while cellulose uses β-1,4 bonds that human enzymes cannot cleave","Cellulose lacks any glycosidic linkages between glucose units","Starch is hydrophobic, while cellulose is highly hydrophilic","Cellulose monomers are L-glucose, while starch is built from D-glucose"],"correct":1,"why":"Both polymers are made of D-glucose, but starch uses α-1,4 glycosidic bonds (and α-1,6 branches in amylopectin), which mammalian amylase can hydrolyze. Cellulose uses β-1,4 linkages — the inverted geometry forms tightly hydrogen-bonded fibers that human enzymes cannot cleave.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm108","section":"BIO","topic":"translation","difficulty":2,"q":"When a protein destined for secretion (e.g., insulin, antibody) is being translated, a short hydrophobic sequence near the N-terminus is recognized by the signal recognition particle (SRP). SRP halts translation and brings the ribosome to a specific organelle, where translation resumes and the polypeptide is threaded into the lumen. Where is the ribosome directed?","opts":["Mitochondrion, where signal sequences direct matrix proteins","Rough endoplasmic reticulum, where the signal peptide is recognized by the Sec61 translocon and the polypeptide enters the ER lumen","Nucleus, via nuclear localization signals","Cytoplasm, where free ribosomes translate cytosolic proteins","Peroxisome, via peroxisomal targeting signals"],"correct":1,"why":"Signal peptides direct ribosomes to the rough ER. SRP binds the signal sequence as it emerges from the ribosome and brings the complex to the SRP receptor on the ER membrane. The Sec61 translocon then channels the polypeptide co-translationally into the ER lumen, where signal peptidase cleaves the signal peptide. Mitochondrial, nuclear, and peroxisomal proteins use post-translational targeting via different signals.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm145","section":"BIO","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"Sanger DNA sequencing — the dominant method until next-generation sequencing — uses chain-terminating nucleotide analogs that are incorporated into a growing DNA strand but lack the 3'-OH needed for further extension. The resulting fragments are separated by size to read the sequence. What are these terminator nucleotides called?","opts":["Restriction enzymes, which cut at palindromic sequences","Dideoxynucleotides (ddNTPs) — they lack the 3'-OH, so DNA polymerase cannot extend the strand once incorporated, causing chain termination","Standard dNTPs, which always extend the chain normally","Reverse transcriptase substrates, used only in retroviral biology","Methylated nucleotides, which only modify base pairing"],"correct":1,"why":"Sanger sequencing uses ddNTPs (dideoxynucleotides). They are missing the 3'-OH, so once incorporated by DNA polymerase, no further nucleotide can be added — the chain terminates. Each ddNTP carries a different fluorescent label, and capillary electrophoresis separates fragments by size, reading the sequence base-by-base.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm149","section":"BIO","topic":"cell-types","difficulty":2,"q":"When a bacteriophage infects one bacterium and accidentally packages a piece of bacterial DNA inside a phage particle, that DNA can be delivered to a new bacterial host upon subsequent infection. The new host integrates the foreign DNA into its genome and acquires new traits. What is this mechanism called?","opts":["Conjugation, mediated by a sex pilus","Transduction — DNA transferred between bacteria by a bacteriophage vector (generalized transduction packages random host DNA, specialized transduction packages specific genes flanking the prophage site)","Direct cell-cell contact like animal cell fusion","Free DNA uptake (transformation)","Spore formation"],"correct":1,"why":"Transduction is DNA transfer between bacteria via bacteriophage. Generalized transduction: phage accidentally packages random bacterial DNA. Specialized transduction: prophage excises imprecisely and carries adjacent host genes. Transduction is one of three major bacterial gene-transfer mechanisms (transformation, transduction, conjugation).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm123","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"Cholesterol intercalates between phospholipids in animal cell membranes and modulates membrane fluidity in a temperature-dependent manner. At high temperatures it restrains phospholipid movement; at low temperatures it disrupts ordered packing. Which statement best summarizes cholesterol's effect?","opts":["Cholesterol always increases fluidity, regardless of temperature","Cholesterol always decreases fluidity, regardless of temperature","Cholesterol BUFFERS fluidity — it increases fluidity at low temperatures (preventing rigid crystallization) and decreases fluidity at high temperatures (preventing excessive disorder)","Cholesterol has no measurable effect on membrane fluidity","Cholesterol only affects fluidity in plant cell membranes, not animal"],"correct":2,"why":"Cholesterol is a fluidity buffer. At low temperatures, it prevents tight crystalline packing of phospholipids (maintains some fluidity). At high temperatures, it restrains excessive lateral movement (prevents over-fluidization). The net effect: animal membranes maintain similar fluidity over a wider temperature range.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm093","section":"BIO","topic":"photo","difficulty":2,"q":"CAM (crassulacean acid metabolism) plants — typified by cacti and succulents — open their stomata only at night to conserve water in arid environments. They fix CO₂ at night using PEP carboxylase, store the carbon as malate in the vacuole, and release CO₂ into the Calvin cycle during the day. What is the primary advantage of this strategy?","opts":["Fixes CO₂ at night and stores it; daytime stomatal closure prevents water loss while CO₂ is released to the Calvin cycle from stored malate","Fixes O₂ instead of CO₂","Stores additional water in the leaf overnight","Performs anaerobic photosynthesis without ETC","Doubles ATP yield compared to C3 plants"],"correct":0,"why":"CAM plants temporally separate CO₂ uptake (night, stomata open) from carbon fixation (day, stomata closed). Nocturnal CO₂ is fixed by PEP carboxylase to oxaloacetate → malate, stored in vacuoles. During day, malate releases CO₂ to RuBisCO. Closing stomata during hot days dramatically reduces water loss — critical in arid habitats.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm146","section":"BIO","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"A cell biologist wants to separate cellular organelles or molecular complexes that have similar mass but different densities. They layer the lysate on top of a tube containing a continuous gradient of sucrose (from low concentration at top to high at bottom) and centrifuge. Each component migrates to the layer matching its own density. Which centrifugation method is this?","opts":["Differential centrifugation, which separates only by mass at fixed density","Sucrose-gradient (density-gradient) centrifugation, which separates particles by density (each migrates to its iso-density layer)","Microcentrifuge spin, used for small-volume sample concentration","Tabletop centrifuge, used for plate-format separations","Rate-zonal centrifugation, which separates by sedimentation rate"],"correct":1,"why":"Density-gradient (isopycnic) centrifugation uses a sucrose, CsCl, or Percoll gradient to separate particles by density. Each particle moves until it reaches the gradient point matching its own density and stops (zero net buoyant force). Differential centrifugation separates by mass (sequential pelleting at increasing speeds), which is a different principle.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm148","section":"BIO","topic":"cell-types","difficulty":2,"q":"In Griffith's classic 1928 experiment, harmless R-strain pneumococci became virulent when mixed with heat-killed S-strain bacteria. Avery, MacLeod, and McCarty later showed that DNA was the transforming substance. The bacteria had taken up free DNA from their environment and integrated it into their genome. What is this form of horizontal gene transfer called?","opts":["Transformation — uptake of free (naked) DNA from the environment, integrated into the recipient genome","Transduction, where DNA is delivered by a bacteriophage","Conjugation, which requires direct cell-to-cell contact via a sex pilus","Translation, an unrelated cellular process","Transposition, where mobile DNA jumps within a single genome"],"correct":0,"why":"Transformation is uptake of free DNA from the environment by 'competent' bacterial cells. Some species (e.g., Streptococcus, Bacillus) are naturally competent; others (e.g., E. coli) need artificial induction. Transformation was the basis of Avery-MacLeod-McCarty's identification of DNA as the genetic material in 1944.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"BIO_cm130","section":"BIO","topic":"membrane","difficulty":2,"q":"Intestinal epithelial cells absorb dietary glucose against its concentration gradient — moving glucose from low-concentration lumen into high-concentration cell interior. They achieve this without directly hydrolyzing ATP at the transporter; instead they exploit the steep Na+ gradient maintained elsewhere by Na+/K+-ATPase. What kind of transport is this?","opts":["Primary active transport, since ATP is being used at the same membrane","Secondary active transport — SGLT couples 'downhill' Na+ entry with 'uphill' glucose entry, indirectly powered by the Na+ gradient that the Na+/K+-ATPase maintains","Facilitated diffusion through GLUT, no energy required","Simple diffusion across the lipid bilayer","Endocytosis, with glucose engulfed in vesicles"],"correct":1,"why":"SGLT (sodium-glucose cotransporter) uses secondary active transport. It moves glucose against its gradient by coupling movement to the favorable Na+ gradient — Na+ flows down its gradient and 'drags' glucose with it. The Na+ gradient itself is built by primary active transport (Na+/K+-ATPase using ATP) elsewhere on the cell. SGLT inhibitors are antidiabetic drugs (e.g., empagliflozin).","_rewritten":true}],"gc":[{"id":"GC_mc-q1","section":"GC","topic":"mole-conv","difficulty":2,"q":"A baker measures out 36.0 g of pure glucose (C6H12O6, MW 180.0 g/mol) for a fermentation experiment. Each glucose molecule contains 6 oxygen atoms. Using N_A = 6.022 x 10^23, how many oxygen atoms are present in this 36.0 g sample?","opts":["1.2 x 10^23","7.2 x 10^23","1.2 x 10^24","4.3 x 10^24","3.6 x 10^23"],"correct":1,"why":"Moles glucose = 36.0/180 = 0.200 mol; molecules = 0.200 x 6.022e23 = 1.20e23. Each has 6 O atoms, so N(O) = 1.20e23 x 6 = 7.2 x 10^23 oxygen atoms.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ph-e1","section":"GC","topic":"ph-basics","difficulty":1,"q":"A student dissolves enough HCl(g) in water at 25 °C to make a 0.010 M HCl solution, which dissociates completely into H+ and Cl- because HCl is a strong acid. Using pH = -log[H+] for this strong-acid solution, what is the pH of the resulting solution?","opts":["1.0","2.0","3.0","10","12"],"correct":1,"why":"[H+] = 0.010 M = 1.0x10^(-2) M because HCl fully dissociates. pH = -log(1.0x10^(-2)) = 2.0. Confusing the exponent magnitude (giving 1 or 3) is a common slip.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ic1","section":"GC","topic":"eq-ice","difficulty":2,"q":"A weak acid HA dissociates in water: HA(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + A⁻(aq). Initial [HA] = 0.10 M and Ka = 1.0×10⁻⁵ at 298 K. Setting up an ICE table with x = [H⁺] formed and using the small-x approximation Ka ≈ x²/0.10, what is the approximate [H⁺] at equilibrium?","opts":["1.0×10⁻¹ M","1.0×10⁻³ M","1.0×10⁻⁵ M","1.0×10⁻⁶ M","1.0×10⁻⁴ M"],"correct":1,"why":"x² = Ka × C = (1.0×10⁻⁵)(0.10) = 1.0×10⁻⁶. x = √(1.0×10⁻⁶) = 1.0×10⁻³ M = [H⁺]. Forgetting the square root gives 10⁻⁶; setting [H⁺] = Ka gives 10⁻⁵.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_sf7","section":"GC","topic":"sig-figs","difficulty":2,"q":"A trace-metal analysis report gives the lead concentration in a water sample as 0.00050300 g/L exactly as printed on the data sheet. Counting all non-leading-zero digits including trailing zeros after the last nonzero digit, how many significant figures does this value carry?","opts":["3","4","5","8","2"],"correct":2,"why":"Leading zeros (0.000) are placeholders. The digits 5, 0, 3, 0, 0 are significant: the first 5 starts the count, and trailing zeros after a decimal are significant. Total = 5 sig figs (or 5.0300 x 10^-4).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lr-e14","section":"GC","topic":"lim-react","difficulty":3,"q":"A student burns 6.0 g of methane in excess O2 at 298 K and 1 atm according to CH4 + 2O2 -> CO2 + 2H2O. The theoretical yield of CO2 is 22.0 g, but only 16.0 g is actually collected after drying. What is the percent yield of CO2 for this combustion?","opts":["68%","73%","78%","82%","88%"],"correct":1,"why":"Percent yield = (actual / theoretical) x 100%. Plugging in: (16.0 g / 22.0 g) x 100% = 72.7%, which rounds to 73%. Common errors include inverting the ratio or forgetting to multiply by 100.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_gl-e14","section":"GC","topic":"gas-laws","difficulty":3,"q":"A balloon contains 5.00 L of pure N2 (MW = 28.0 g/mol) at standard temperature and pressure (273 K, 1.00 atm), where the molar volume of an ideal gas is 22.4 L/mol. Using ideal-gas behavior, what mass of N2 is contained in the balloon?","opts":["3.10 g","5.00 g","6.25 g","12.5 g","25.0 g"],"correct":2,"why":"n = V/V_molar = 5.00 / 22.4 = 0.223 mol; mass = n x MW = 0.223 x 28.0 = 6.25 g. Forgetting to convert to moles (and using V x MW directly) gives 140 g.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_gb1","section":"GC","topic":"gibbs","difficulty":1,"q":"A reaction has ΔH = −50 kJ/mol (exothermic) and ΔS = +120 J/(K·mol) (entropy increases). Using ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, both terms favor spontaneity at any T > 0 K. At what temperatures is this reaction spontaneous?","opts":["Spontaneous at all temperatures","Never spontaneous","Spontaneous only at low T","Spontaneous only at high T","Spontaneous only at T = 298 K"],"correct":0,"why":"When ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0, ΔG = (negative) − T(positive) is negative for every T > 0 K. Both enthalpy and entropy drive the reaction forward, so it is spontaneous at all (positive) temperatures.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_v-e1","section":"GC","topic":"vsepr","difficulty":1,"q":"Carbon dioxide (O=C=O) has a central carbon with two double bonds to oxygen and zero lone pairs on C. VSEPR theory predicts geometry by counting electron domains around the central atom (each double bond counts as one domain). What is the molecular shape of CO₂?","opts":["Linear (180°)","Bent (~120°)","Trigonal planar (120°)","Tetrahedral (109.5°)","Octahedral (90°)"],"correct":0,"why":"Steric number on C = 2 bonding domains + 0 lone pairs = 2, which gives linear electron and molecular geometry with 180° bond angle. Despite each C=O being polar, the symmetric linear arrangement makes CO₂ a nonpolar molecule overall.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_qn-e2","section":"GC","topic":"quantum-numbers","difficulty":2,"q":"The 4d subshell has principal quantum number n = 4 and angular momentum quantum number l = 2. The number of orbitals in any subshell is (2l + 1), and each orbital holds at most 2 electrons by the Pauli exclusion principle. What is the maximum number of electrons that the 4d subshell can hold?","opts":["2","6","10","14","18"],"correct":2,"why":"Number of d-orbitals = 2l+1 = 2(2)+1 = 5. Each orbital holds 2 e⁻, so the 4d subshell holds 5 × 2 = 10 electrons maximum. This applies to ALL d-subshells (3d, 4d, 5d, ...).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_nr5","section":"GC","topic":"nernst","difficulty":2,"q":"A galvanic cell at 25°C is assembled with reactants and products at non-standard concentrations and the measured EMF is found to be LESS than its E° value. Using the Nernst equation E = E° - (0.0592/n)·log Q, what does this observation indicate about the reaction quotient Q?","opts":["Q > 1 (more products relative to reactants)","Q < 1 (more reactants relative to products)","Q = 0","Q = K exactly","Q is undefined"],"correct":0,"why":"If E < E°, then -(0.0592/n)·log Q is negative, requiring log Q > 0, i.e. Q > 1. The cell has accumulated products (or depleted reactants), so EMF has fallen toward zero as it approaches equilibrium.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_b-e4","section":"GC","topic":"bonding","difficulty":2,"q":"Carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄) contains four C-Cl polar bonds (ΔEN ≈ 0.5). The molecule is tetrahedral with bond angles of 109.5°. Considering the vector sum of the four bond dipoles arranged tetrahedrally, what is the net molecular polarity of CCl₄?","opts":["Polar (net dipole moment > 0)","Nonpolar (dipoles cancel by symmetry)","Ionic","Cannot be determined from geometry","Polar only at high temperature"],"correct":1,"why":"Although each C-Cl bond is polar, the four bond dipoles point symmetrically toward the corners of a tetrahedron and cancel exactly by vector addition. The net molecular dipole moment is zero, making CCl₄ nonpolar despite having polar bonds.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_at-e3","section":"GC","topic":"atomic","difficulty":2,"q":"Phosphorus has atomic number Z = 15 and is found in phosphate groups of DNA and ATP. Following the Aufbau principle (1s 2s 2p 3s 3p ...), Hund's rule, and the Pauli exclusion principle, what is the ground-state electron configuration of neutral P?","opts":["[Ne] 3s² 3p³","[Ne] 3s² 3p⁵","[Ar] 3p³","[Ne] 3s² 3p²","[Ne] 3s¹ 3p⁴"],"correct":0,"why":"Total of 15 electrons: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p³, abbreviated as [Ne] 3s² 3p³. The three 3p electrons each occupy a separate p orbital with parallel spins (Hund's rule), giving 3 unpaired electrons.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_e5","section":"GC","topic":"enthalpy","difficulty":2,"q":"Combustion of glucose: C₆H₁₂O₆(s) + 6O₂(g) → 6CO₂(g) + 6H₂O(l), ΔH_combustion = −2800 kJ/mol at 298 K. A bomb calorimeter burns 0.500 mol of glucose to completion. How much heat is released?","opts":["−700 kJ","−1400 kJ","−2800 kJ","+1400 kJ","−5600 kJ"],"correct":1,"why":"Heat released = n × ΔH = 0.500 × (−2800) = −1400 kJ (1400 kJ given off). Ignoring the 0.5 factor gives −2800; doubling instead of halving gives −5600; flipping sign gives +1400.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_nu-e3","section":"GC","topic":"nuclear","difficulty":2,"q":"Carbon-14 produced naturally in the upper atmosphere undergoes beta-minus (electron) emission with a half-life of 5730 years, the basis of radiocarbon dating. In the nuclear equation 14-6 C -> X + 0-1 e + antineutrino, what is the daughter nuclide X formed when a neutron in the C-14 nucleus converts into a proton?","opts":["14-7 N","10-5 B","14-5 B","10-6 C","13-6 C"],"correct":0,"why":"In beta-minus decay a neutron becomes a proton plus an emitted electron, so A is unchanged and Z increases by 1. From 14-6 C, the daughter has A = 14 and Z = 7, which is nitrogen-14. Mass and charge are conserved across the equation.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_pd4","section":"GC","topic":"phase-diag","difficulty":2,"q":"On a single-component P-T phase diagram, the three coexistence curves (sublimation, fusion, vaporization) intersect at one unique combination of pressure and temperature called the triple point. For water this point lies at 0.006 atm and 273.16 K. Which statement is TRUE at the triple point?","opts":["Only liquid is stable","Solid, liquid, and gas coexist in stable equilibrium","Liquid and gas become indistinguishable (critical condition)","No phase exists at all","Only solid is stable"],"correct":1,"why":"The triple point is the unique P,T at which the three two-phase coexistence lines meet, so all three phases (s, l, g) coexist simultaneously. Above the critical point, l and g merge; that is a different feature of the diagram.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_xp001","section":"GC","topic":"kinetics","difficulty":3,"q":"For a first-order reaction with k = 0.025 s⁻¹, what is the half-life, AND how many half-lives must pass for the reactant concentration to fall below 1% of its initial value?","opts":["t½ = 27.7 s; ~7 half-lives needed (≈194 s)","t½ = 40.0 s; ~5 half-lives needed (≈200 s)","t½ = 13.9 s; ~3 half-lives needed (≈42 s)","t½ = 0.025 s; ~50 half-lives needed","t½ = 0.5 s; ~100 half-lives needed"],"correct":0,"why":"t½ = ln(2)/k = 0.693/0.025 ≈ 27.7 s. After n half-lives, fraction remaining = (1/2)ⁿ. For < 1%: (1/2)ⁿ < 0.01 → n > log(100)/log(2) ≈ 6.64 → 7 half-lives."},{"id":"GC_bf3","section":"GC","topic":"buffers","difficulty":3,"q":"A buffer initially contains 0.10 mol/L HA and 0.10 mol/L A⁻ (pKa = 5.00, pH = 5.00). 0.010 mol/L of strong acid (H⁺) is added. The H⁺ converts A⁻ to HA: new [A⁻] = 0.090 M, new [HA] = 0.110 M. Using Henderson-Hasselbalch, what is the new pH?","opts":["5.00","4.91","5.09","4.50","4.00"],"correct":1,"why":"log(0.090/0.110) = log(0.818) = −0.087. pH = 5.00 + (−0.087) ≈ 4.91. The buffer absorbs H⁺ with only a small drop. Without buffer, adding 0.01 M H⁺ to neutral water would crash pH to 2.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lf003","section":"GC","topic":"acid-base","difficulty":3,"q":"A 25.0 mL sample of 0.100 M acetic acid (Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵) is titrated with 0.100 M NaOH. What is the pH at the half-equivalence point?","opts":["4.74 (pH = pKa)","7.00 (neutral)","8.72 (slightly basic)","2.87 (acidic)","11.3 (strongly basic)"],"correct":0,"why":"At half-equivalence, [HA] = [A⁻] in the buffer, so pH = pKa = −log(1.8×10⁻⁵) = 4.74. This is the classic Henderson-Hasselbalch result."},{"id":"GC_ar-e4","section":"GC","topic":"arrhenius","difficulty":3,"q":"A catalyst lowers the activation energy of a reaction from E_a = 75 kJ/mol to E_a' = 50 kJ/mol while leaving the Arrhenius pre-factor A unchanged at T = 300 K (R = 8.314 J/(mol·K)). Using k_new/k_old = exp((E_a − E_a')/RT), estimate the rate-enhancement factor.","opts":["~5","~50","~5x10^4","~10^6","~10^9"],"correct":2,"why":"ΔE_a = 25,000 J/mol; (E_a − E_a')/(RT) = 25000/(8.314 x 300) ≈ 10.0. exp(10.0) ≈ 2.2 x 10^4, near 5 x 10^4. Forgetting J vs kJ gives a hugely different (and wrong) answer.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lf002","section":"GC","topic":"equilibrium","difficulty":3,"q":"For the reaction N2(g) + 3 H2(g) ⇌ 2 NH3(g), ΔH = −92 kJ/mol. A reaction vessel at equilibrium contains all three gases. The temperature is then increased while the volume is kept constant. According to Le Chatelier's principle, which change correctly describes the new equilibrium position?","opts":["Shifts right; more NH3 is produced because the system absorbs heat","Shifts left; the equilibrium favors reactants because the forward reaction is exothermic","No shift; temperature does not affect equilibrium position","Shifts right; Kp increases with temperature for all reactions","Shifts left; the increase in pressure compensates for added heat"],"correct":1,"why":"Forward reaction is exothermic. Adding heat is like adding \"product heat\"; equilibrium shifts left (toward reactants) to consume the added heat."},{"id":"GC_s-e11","section":"GC","topic":"solutions","difficulty":3,"q":"A student prepares an aqueous mixture by dissolving 1.0 mol of NaCl in 9.0 mol of H2O at 25 °C and stirring until uniform. The solution is to be used to verify Raoult's law deviations. What is the mole fraction (X) of NaCl in this solution, treating NaCl as an undissociated molecular formula unit?","opts":["0.05","0.10","0.11","0.50","0.90"],"correct":1,"why":"X(NaCl) = mol NaCl / total mol = 1.0 / (1.0 + 9.0) = 0.10. A common error is dividing by the solvent only (1/9 = 0.11) instead of by total moles.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_rl-e5","section":"GC","topic":"rate-laws","difficulty":3,"q":"A zero-order reaction A -> products at 298 K has rate constant k = 0.050 M/s and initial concentration [A]_0 = 1.00 M. Using the zero-order half-life formula t_(1/2) = [A]_0 / (2k), how long does it take for [A] to fall to 0.50 M?","opts":["5.0 s","10. s","20. s","30. s","40. s"],"correct":1,"why":"t_(1/2) = [A]_0 / (2k) = 1.00 / (2 x 0.050) = 10. s. Note that for zero order, t_(1/2) depends on [A]_0; using [A]_0/k (without the 2) gives 20 s.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_el4","section":"GC","topic":"electrolysis","difficulty":3,"q":"In the Hall-Heroult process, molten Al2O3 is electrolyzed at ~960°C to deposit aluminum at the cathode via Al^3+ + 3e- -> Al(s). To produce exactly 1.00 mol of aluminum metal (27 g), how many moles of electrons must flow through the cell?","opts":["1","2","3","6","9"],"correct":2,"why":"Stoichiometry of the cathode half-reaction shows 3 mol of electrons reduce 1 mol of Al^3+ to Al. So 1 mol Al requires 3 mol e- = 3 x 96485 ~ 2.9 x 10^5 C. The factor n equals the change in oxidation state.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_mc-e12","section":"GC","topic":"mole-conv","difficulty":2,"q":"A sample of pure CO₂ is analyzed for elemental carbon content. Atomic mass of C = 12.0 g/mol; molar mass of CO₂ = 44.0 g/mol. The mass percent of an element in a compound is %X = (mass X / mass compound) × 100. Calculate the %w/w of carbon in CO₂.","opts":["18%","27%","36%","55%","73%"],"correct":1,"why":"%C = (12.0 / 44.0) × 100 = 27.3%. The remaining 72.7% is oxygen (32 g of O / 44 g total). Useful for back-calculating mass of C from a CO₂ measurement (e.g., 4.4 g CO₂ contains 1.2 g C).","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ph-e5","section":"GC","topic":"ph-basics","difficulty":2,"q":"A 0.0010 M aqueous NaOH solution is prepared at 25 °C, and the strong base fully dissociates to give [OH-] = 1.0x10^(-3) M. Using pOH = -log[OH-] and pH + pOH = 14, what is the pH of this solution?","opts":["3.0","7.0","10","11","13"],"correct":3,"why":"[OH-] = 1.0x10^(-3) M -> pOH = 3.0. Therefore pH = 14 - 3.0 = 11. Confusing pH with pOH gives 3; forgetting the conversion gives 13.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_ic3","section":"GC","topic":"eq-ice","difficulty":3,"q":"Acetic acid is dissolved in water at 25°C: CH₃COOH ⇌ H⁺ + CH₃COO⁻ with Ka = 1.8×10⁻⁵ and initial [CH₃COOH] = 0.10 M. Setting up the ICE table with the small-x approximation x²/0.10 ≈ 1.8×10⁻⁵, what is [H⁺] at equilibrium?","opts":["1.34×10⁻³ M","1.8×10⁻⁴ M","1.8×10⁻⁵ M","4.5×10⁻⁵ M","1.0×10⁻⁷ M"],"correct":0,"why":"x² = (1.8×10⁻⁵)(0.10) = 1.8×10⁻⁶. x = √(1.8×10⁻⁶) ≈ 1.34×10⁻³ M = [H⁺]. (5% check: 1.34×10⁻³ / 0.10 = 1.34%, well under 5%.) Forgetting the √ gives 1.8×10⁻⁶; using Ka itself gives 1.8×10⁻⁵.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_sf6","section":"GC","topic":"sig-figs","difficulty":3,"q":"A buret reading of 2.36 mL is added to a graduated-cylinder reading of 0.011 mL, then the sum is multiplied by a measured concentration factor of 1.20. Apply addition rules first (smallest decimal places), THEN multiplication rules (smallest sig figs) to determine the correct value of (2.36 + 0.011) x 1.20.","opts":["2.85","2.8","2.84","2.844","2.8442"],"correct":2,"why":"Add: 2.36 + 0.011 = 2.371; rounded to 2 decimal places (the addition rule) gives 2.37 (3 sig figs). Multiply: 2.37 x 1.20 = 2.844, with both factors having 3 sig figs, so report 2.84.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_lr-e10","section":"GC","topic":"lim-react","difficulty":3,"q":"The Haber process N₂(g) + 3 H₂(g) → 2 NH₃(g) is run with 10.0 g N₂ (MW = 28 → 0.357 mol) and 5.00 g H₂ (MW = 2 → 2.50 mol). Compute mole/coefficient ratios to identify the limiting reactant.","opts":["N₂ is limiting","H₂ is limiting","Both run out simultaneously","Cannot tell from given data","NH₃ is limiting"],"correct":0,"why":"Ratios: N₂ → 0.357/1 = 0.357; H₂ → 2.50/3 = 0.833. Smaller ratio = N₂ limits. With N₂ limiting, H₂ used = 0.357 × 3 = 1.07 mol, so 2.50 - 1.07 = 1.43 mol H₂ remains in excess.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_gl-e12","section":"GC","topic":"gas-laws","difficulty":3,"q":"A rigid steel cylinder at 298 K contains a binary mixture in which N2 contributes a partial pressure of 0.50 atm and O2 contributes 1.50 atm, with no other gases present. According to Dalton's law and the relation X_i = P_i/P_total, what is the mole fraction of N2?","opts":["0.20","0.25","0.33","0.50","0.75"],"correct":1,"why":"P_total = 0.50 + 1.50 = 2.00 atm. X_N2 = 0.50/2.00 = 0.25. Confusing X_N2 with the smaller-of-two ratio (0.50/1.50 = 0.33) is the most common error.","_rewritten":true},{"id":"GC_mc-e15","section":"GC","topic":"mole-conv","difficulty":3,"q":"A patient receives a 100.0 g infusion of normal saline that contains pure NaCl (MW = 58.5 g/mol; Na = 23, Cl = 35.5). Use n = m/MW to determine how many moles of NaCl are present.","opts":["1.7 mol","5.85 mol","58.5 mol","17.1 mol","0.585 mol"],"correct":0,"why":"n = 100.0 / 58.5 = 1.71 mol of NaCl. The other answers reverse the division (5.85, 17.1) or confuse mass with moles (58.5). Check: 1.71 × 58.5 ≈ 100 g ✓.","_rewritten":true}],"qr":[{"id":"QR_un1","section":"QR","topic":"units","difficulty":1,"q":"30 mph in feet per second (1 mile = 5280 ft)?","opts":["44 ft/s","30 ft/s","88 ft/s","22 ft/s"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_po4","section":"QR","topic":"pct-of","difficulty":1,"q":"10% of 60 = ?","opts":["6","10","60","0.6"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_vol1671","section":"QR","topic":"vol-sa","difficulty":2,"q":"Box: 3×6×8. Volume?","opts":["144","34","17","147"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_wor1387","section":"QR","topic":"work-rate","difficulty":2,"q":"A finishes a job in 5 hr, B in 3 hr. Together?","opts":["8","3","4","1.88"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_fra1047","section":"QR","topic":"frac-mul","difficulty":2,"q":"9/8 · 5/3 = ?","opts":["14/24","27/40","15/8","45/11"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_coo1440","section":"QR","topic":"coord","difficulty":1,"q":"Distance from (-3, -2) to (3, 3)?","opts":["3.32","11","7.81","12"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_fam1","section":"QR","topic":"frac-add","difficulty":2,"q":"5/12 + 1/4 = ?","opts":["8/12","6/16","5/16","2/3"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_est1196","section":"QR","topic":"estim","difficulty":2,"q":"Estimate 706 × 46.","opts":["36000","35000","34000","32476"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_rt2","section":"QR","topic":"rate-time","difficulty":2,"q":"A train covers 240 miles in 3 hours. What is its speed?","opts":["80 mph","60 mph","120 mph","720 mph"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_cp2","section":"QR","topic":"count-pc","difficulty":2,"q":"How many 4-letter \"words\" using A,B,C,D,E (no repeats)?","opts":["P(5,4) = 120","C(5,4) = 5","5⁴ = 625","4! = 24"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_rat1349","section":"QR","topic":"ratios","difficulty":3,"q":"In a 1:8 ratio totaling 45, what's the smaller part?","opts":["6","5","40","41"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_pem1302","section":"QR","topic":"pemdas","difficulty":3,"q":"Evaluate: 8 × 4 − 2 × 1","opts":["16","34","30","9"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_dis1582","section":"QR","topic":"discr","difficulty":1,"q":"For 3x² + 0x + 1 = 0, how many real roots?","opts":["two imaginary roots","two real roots","one real root","no real roots"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_g2dm2","section":"QR","topic":"geo-2d","difficulty":2,"q":"Circle of area 49π. Radius = ?","opts":["7","49","14","7π"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_lg3","section":"QR","topic":"logs","difficulty":2,"q":"log₂(1/8) = ?","opts":["−3","3","1/3","−1/3"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_pct1170","section":"QR","topic":"pct-seq","difficulty":2,"q":"$100 → +40% → -10%. Final?","opts":["90","126","140","130"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_tri1656","section":"QR","topic":"tri-inv","difficulty":3,"q":"Sides 4, 5, 10: valid triangle?","opts":["Cannot determine","Only if right triangle","Yes","No"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_ut5","section":"QR","topic":"untimed","difficulty":3,"q":"Why does untimed practice come BEFORE timed practice?","opts":["Speed amplifies whatever pattern you've drilled","It feels easier","To save the hard problems","To avoid wasting time"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_mix1416","section":"QR","topic":"mixture","difficulty":3,"q":"Mix 300mL of 30% with 150mL of 20%. Final concentration?","opts":["112.5","25","26.67","120"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_pan1537","section":"QR","topic":"p-andor","difficulty":3,"q":"events: P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.2, P(A and B) = 0.05. P(A or B)?","opts":["0.65","0.7","0.3","0.1"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_lin1264","section":"QR","topic":"lin-eq","difficulty":4,"q":"Solve: 4x + 8 = 1x - 3","opts":["-2.67","-3.67","3.67","-4.67"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_dis1287","section":"QR","topic":"distrib","difficulty":4,"q":"Expand and evaluate 3(1 + 0).","opts":["0","6","3","1"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_men1233","section":"QR","topic":"mental","difficulty":4,"q":"Mentally compute 4 × 15.","opts":["160","70","60","50"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_un2","section":"QR","topic":"units","difficulty":2,"q":"Convert 250 mL to liters.","opts":["0.25 L","25 L","2.5 L","2500 L"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_pct1128","section":"QR","topic":"pct-of","difficulty":3,"q":"12% of 156 = ?","opts":["18.72","1.87","168","1872"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_vol1675","section":"QR","topic":"vol-sa","difficulty":3,"q":"Cylinder r=5, h=3. Volume?","opts":["94.25","47.12","235.62","75"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_wr3","section":"QR","topic":"work-rate","difficulty":3,"q":"A inlet fills tank in 6 hr. Drain empties in 9 hr. With both open, time to fill?","opts":["18 hr","3 hr","15 hr","7.5 hr"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_fm4","section":"QR","topic":"frac-mul","difficulty":2,"q":"5/6 ÷ 2/3 = ?","opts":["5/4","10/18","5/9","3/12"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_coo1448","section":"QR","topic":"coord","difficulty":2,"q":"Distance from (5, 5) to (9, 9)?","opts":["5.66","8","2.83","9"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_fra1025","section":"QR","topic":"frac-add","difficulty":3,"q":"1/6 + 3/5 = ?","opts":["29/30","3/30","4/11","23/30"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_est1208","section":"QR","topic":"estim","difficulty":3,"q":"Estimate 2192 × 69.","opts":["154000","153000","155000","151248"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_rat1365","section":"QR","topic":"rate-time","difficulty":2,"q":"A car drives 60 mph for 1 hr. Distance?","opts":["61","0","60","120"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_cou1575","section":"QR","topic":"count-pc","difficulty":3,"q":"How many ways to arrange 3 of 7 items in order?","opts":["35","10","21","210"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_rat1350","section":"QR","topic":"ratios","difficulty":3,"q":"In a 3:7 ratio totaling 10, what's the smaller part?","opts":["7","3","4","8"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_pem1309","section":"QR","topic":"pemdas","difficulty":4,"q":"Evaluate: (9 + 8) × 2","opts":["74","25","34","144"],"correct":2},{"id":"QR_uni1475","section":"QR","topic":"units","difficulty":3,"q":"8.8 cm in mm?","opts":["88","8.8","0.88","18.8"],"correct":0},{"id":"QR_pct1134","section":"QR","topic":"pct-of","difficulty":4,"q":"12% of 225 = ?","opts":["2.7","27","2700","237"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_vol1677","section":"QR","topic":"vol-sa","difficulty":3,"q":"Box: 4×4×6. Volume?","opts":["100","14","28","96"],"correct":3},{"id":"QR_wor1395","section":"QR","topic":"work-rate","difficulty":3,"q":"A finishes a job in 3 hr, B in 4 hr. Together?","opts":["3","1.71","3.5","7"],"correct":1},{"id":"QR_fra1057","section":"QR","topic":"frac-mul","difficulty":4,"q":"1/16 · 4/7 = ?","opts":["7/64","1/28","4/23","5/112"],"correct":1}],"ochem":[{"id":"OC_alk071","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":3,"q":"Propene is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 in basic aqueous solution at 0 degrees C. Which product, with which stereochemistry, results from this Baeyer test?","opts":["Propanoic acid via cleavage","Propane via reduction","1,2-Propanediol via syn dihydroxylation through a cyclic manganate ester","Propanal via Wacker oxidation","Acetone via Markovnikov hydration"],"correct":2,"q_smiles":"CC=C","opts_smiles":["CCC(=O)O","CCC",null,"CCC=O"],"why":"Cold, dilute KMnO4 forms a cyclic Mn(V) ester across the alkene. Hydrolysis gives a syn diol because both OH groups end up on the same face. With propene, the product is 1,2-propanediol.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_arr003","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-arrows","difficulty":2,"q":"The carbonate ion has the Lewis structure shown with curved arrows indicating resonance. What is the correct major contributor?","q_svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 160\" width=\"360\" height=\"160\"><style>\n  .bond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .dbond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .atom { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; fill: #1a1d20; text-anchor: middle; }\n  .lp   { fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .chg  { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .arrow { stroke: #cf3b3b; stroke-width: 1.8; fill: none; }\n  .arrowhead { fill: #cf3b3b; }\n  .lbl { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #6a737a; }\n</style><defs>\n  <marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" class=\"arrowhead\"/>\n  </marker>\n  <marker id=\"ah-half\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5\" class=\"arrowhead\" stroke=\"#cf3b3b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  </marker>\n</defs>\n    <!-- carbonate central C with three O's -->\n    <text x=\"160\" y=\"84\" class=\"atom\">C</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"160\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"120\"/>\n    <text x=\"160\" y=\"135\" class=\"atom\">O</text>\n    <text x=\"172\" y=\"132\" class=\"chg\">⊖</text>\n    <line class=\"dbond\" x1=\"153\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"55\"/>\n    <line class=\"dbond\" x1=\"148\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"60\"/>\n    <text x=\"115\" y=\"50\" class=\"atom\">O</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"167\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"55\"/>\n    <text x=\"200\" y=\"50\" class=\"atom\">O</text>\n    <text x=\"212\" y=\"48\" class=\"chg\">⊖</text>\n    <!-- arrow from C=O pi to O making it lone pair -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 145 60 Q 130 30 115 35\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <!-- arrow from O- lone pair to form new pi to C -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 200 60 Q 185 35 170 60\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"140\" class=\"lbl\">Three equivalent resonance structures · charge delocalized over 3 O's</text>\n  </svg>","opts":["One C=O, two C-O⊖ — three equivalent resonance forms","Three C=O double bonds","One C=O, one C-O⊖, one C-OH","C with three single bonds and triple negative charge"],"opts_smiles":["[O-]C(=O)[O-]",null,null,null],"correct":0,"why":"Carbonate has three equivalent resonance structures with one C=O and two C-O⊖ each; arrows show electron-pair movement between equivalent contributors.","_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_lab259","section":"OCHEM","topic":"lab","difficulty":3,"q":"A dichloromethane solution contains a neutral aromatic ester and a small carboxylic acid impurity. Considering pKa values and ester sensitivity to base, which aqueous wash will selectively remove only the acid into the aqueous phase?","opts":["Wash with saturated aqueous NaHCO3, which deprotonates the carboxylic acid (pKa 4-5) but not phenols and does not saponify the ester","Wash with 1 M HCl, which protonates basic groups","Distill the entire mixture and discard the residue","Wash with brine (NaCl) and decant","Wash with 1 M NaOH (which would also hydrolyze the ester)"],"correct":0,"opts_smiles":[null,"Cl[H]",null,null],"why":"Bicarbonate (pKa of H2CO3 approximately 6.4) is basic enough to deprotonate carboxylic acids but too weak to deprotonate phenols (pKa approximately 10) or hydrolyze esters. The carboxylate ion enters the aqueous phase; the ester stays organic.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_abo279","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":3,"q":"1-pentyne (terminal alkyne) and 1-pentene are each treated with the same base. Why does NaNH2 cleanly deprotonate 1-pentyne but not the vinyl C-H of 1-pentene? Compare the relevant pKa values.","opts":["Vinyl C-H is more acidic (~25) due to better resonance","Terminal alkyne pKa ~25, vinyl C-H pKa ~44; alkyne wins because the sp orbital has 50% s-character","Both pKa ~50; reaction is kinetic","Both pKa ~25; depends on solvent","Alkyne has pKa ~10 due to triple bond strain"],"correct":1,"why":"Higher s-character in the C-H bond holds electrons closer to carbon, stabilizing the conjugate base. The sp-hybridized terminal alkyne carbon (50% s) gives pKa ~25, while sp2 (33% s) leaves the vinyl C-H at ~44.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_ste032","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":1,"q":"When assigning configuration at a stereocenter, the lowest-priority substituent is oriented toward the viewer (a 'wedge in') and the visible 1->2->3 priority arc traces clockwise. What is the true configuration?","opts":["S, because viewing from the wrong side requires inverting the apparent direction","R, take the apparent rotation as drawn","Achiral; no configuration can be assigned","Meso, because the lowest priority is in front","E or Z (alkene assignment)"],"correct":0,"why":"CIP rules require viewing the stereocenter with the lowest-priority group pointing away. When it points toward you, the apparent rotation is reversed. Apparent clockwise therefore corresponds to true counterclockwise (S).","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_aro116","section":"OCHEM","topic":"arom-eas","difficulty":3,"q":"Benzene is treated with a 1:1 mixture of concentrated HNO3 and H2SO4 at 50 degC. Sulfuric acid protonates HNO3, which loses water to give NO2+. After electrophilic substitution and rearomatization, which product is isolated?","opts":["Nitrocyclohexane (loss of aromaticity)","Benzoic acid (oxidation)","Aniline (reduction product)","Nitrobenzene","1,3-dinitrobenzene (over-nitration)"],"correct":3,"q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts_smiles":[null,"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","[O-][N+](=O)c1ccccc1"],"why":"H2SO4 generates the nitronium ion (NO2+) by protonating and dehydrating HNO3. The arene attacks NO2+ to form the sigma complex, and proton loss restores aromaticity to give nitrobenzene. The newly installed -NO2 is strongly deactivating, so a second nitration would require much harsher conditions and would go meta.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec018","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":2,"q":"2-Chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane is dissolved in 80% aqueous ethanol at 50 degrees C with no added base. A small amount of rearranged product (3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol) is observed in addition to the tert-butyl-style alcohol. Which mechanism best explains the observation?","opts":["SN1, because a 3 degrees cation can form and undergo a 1,2-hydride shift","SN2, with no rearrangement possible","E2 with the bulky internal proton","Concerted [1,2]-shift without ionization","Radical chain substitution"],"correct":0,"why":"Carbocation rearrangements (hydride or methyl shifts) are diagnostic of SN1/E1 chemistry. Tertiary substrates in polar protic solvent ionize first; the resulting cation can shift to a more stable cation before the nucleophile attacks.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_sp018","section":"OCHEM","topic":"spec-ir-nmr","difficulty":4,"q":"Calculate the degrees of unsaturation for C6H5N (note: nitrogen contributes +1 in the numerator) using DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2. What is the result, and what kind of structure could it represent?","opts":["3 (e.g., two rings + one double bond)","4 (e.g., one aromatic ring + one C=N or one extra ring)","5 (e.g., aromatic ring + nitrile, like cyanobenzene C6H5CN minus C; in C6H5N a pyridine-like fused system)","6","7"],"correct":2,"why":"DoU = (2(6) + 2 + 1 - 5)/2 = 10/2 = 5. Five degrees fit, for instance, an aromatic ring (4 DoU) plus an additional pi bond or ring. Always remember to add N in the numerator and subtract halogens.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_abo281","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":4,"q":"Benzoic acid is dissolved in diethyl ether and shaken with a saturated aqueous NaOH layer. Predict the chemistry and the location of the organic acid after partitioning, considering pKa values (PhCOOH ~4.2; H2O ~15.7).","opts":["No deprotonation; the acid stays in the ether layer","Brønsted neutralization: sodium benzoate forms and partitions into the aqueous layer; the equilibrium is driven by formation of the weaker acid/base pair (H2O + PhCOO-)","Fischer esterification gives ethyl benzoate in the ether layer","Benzoyl ether forms by SN2 displacement","The acid is reduced to benzaldehyde by NaOH"],"correct":1,"q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_smiles":[null,"O",null,null],"why":"Hydroxide (conjugate acid pKa 15.7) is strong enough to deprotonate benzoic acid (pKa 4.2). The ionic benzoate is highly water-soluble, so it migrates into the aqueous layer — a textbook acid-base extraction.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_ste040","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":2,"q":"While assigning a stereocenter on paper, a student notices that the lowest-priority group is drawn on a wedge coming out toward them. The visible 1->2->3 priority arc rotates clockwise. What is the actual configuration?","opts":["R, read directly without correction","S, because the apparent rotation must be flipped when the lowest priority points toward the viewer","Achiral; no configuration possible","Meso, by symmetry rule","Cannot be determined without a 3D model"],"correct":1,"why":"CIP requires viewing with the lowest priority pointing away. When it points toward you, you see the 'wrong' side, so apparent clockwise rotation is really counterclockwise. The configuration is therefore S.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_ami200","section":"OCHEM","topic":"amines","difficulty":3,"q":"Considering the competing effects of alkyl induction (favoring more substituted amines) and hydrogen-bond solvation of the conjugate acid (requiring N-H bonds), which sequence correctly orders the aqueous basicities of NH3 and the 1, 2, 3 deg aliphatic amines?","opts":["All approximately equal","3 deg > 2 deg > 1 deg > NH3 always","NH3 > 1 deg > 2 deg > 3 deg","2 deg > 1 deg approx 3 deg > NH3 in water; gas-phase 3 deg > 2 deg > 1 deg","1 deg > NH3 > 2 deg > 3 deg"],"correct":3,"opts_smiles":[null,null,null,"O"],"why":"Pure induction (gas phase) gives 3 deg > 2 deg > 1 deg > NH3, but hydrogen-bond stabilization of the protonated amine in water depends on N-H bonds, partially offsetting the induction trend. Net aqueous basicity is 2 deg > 1 deg approx 3 deg > NH3.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_arr004","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-arrows","difficulty":2,"q":"Propene + HBr. Arrows show electrophilic addition forming the more stable carbocation. Predict the major product.","q_svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 160\" width=\"360\" height=\"160\"><style>\n  .bond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .dbond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .atom { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; fill: #1a1d20; text-anchor: middle; }\n  .lp   { fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .chg  { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .arrow { stroke: #cf3b3b; stroke-width: 1.8; fill: none; }\n  .arrowhead { fill: #cf3b3b; }\n  .lbl { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #6a737a; }\n</style><defs>\n  <marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" class=\"arrowhead\"/>\n  </marker>\n  <marker id=\"ah-half\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5\" class=\"arrowhead\" stroke=\"#cf3b3b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  </marker>\n</defs>\n    <!-- propene CH3-CH=CH2 -->\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"40\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"70\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"80\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"90\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"84\" y1=\"74\" x2=\"116\" y2=\"92\"/>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"120\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"70\"/>\n    <!-- HBr -->\n    <text x=\"220\" y=\"74\" class=\"atom\">H</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"232\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"252\" y2=\"70\"/>\n    <text x=\"266\" y=\"74\" class=\"atom\">Br</text>\n    <!-- arrow from C=C pi to H -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 100 60 Q 160 40 220 60\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <!-- arrow from H-Br to Br -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 244 80 Q 270 100 280 84\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"140\" class=\"lbl\">Markovnikov: H adds to CH2 (more H's), Br to internal C</text>\n  </svg>","opts":["1-bromopropane (anti-Markovnikov)","2-bromopropane (Markovnikov)","1,2-dibromopropane","Propanol"],"opts_smiles":["CCCBr","CC(Br)C","BrCC(Br)C","CCCO"],"correct":1,"why":"Electrophilic addition: π electrons attack H, forming the more stable secondary carbocation. Br− then attacks the 2° carbon → 2-bromopropane.","_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_lab252","section":"OCHEM","topic":"lab","difficulty":1,"q":"A crude solid product contains a small amount of soluble impurity, and the desired compound is more soluble in hot solvent than cold. Which technique exploits this temperature-dependent solubility difference to purify the solid?","opts":["Recrystallization","Centrifugation","Simple distillation","Column chromatography","Liquid-liquid extraction"],"correct":0,"why":"Recrystallization dissolves the crude solid in a minimum volume of hot solvent and slowly cools to crystallize the major component, leaving impurities in solution. The choice of solvent must give large solubility differences between hot and cold to be effective.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_abo268","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":1,"q":"Among the four monohaloacetic acids X-CH2-COOH (X = F, Cl, Br) plus acetic acid (CH3-COOH), which is the strongest acid in water? Inductive (through-sigma) electron withdrawal stabilizes the carboxylate.","opts":["CH3-COOH (no halogen)","Br-CH2-COOH","Cl-CH2-COOH","F-CH2-COOH (most electronegative halogen)","All four have nearly identical pKa values"],"correct":3,"why":"Inductive withdrawal stabilizes the carboxylate conjugate base; the strongest -I effect at the alpha carbon comes from the most electronegative halogen. Fluorine is the most electronegative, so F-CH2-COOH (pKa ~2.6) is strongest, followed by Cl, Br, then unsubstituted acetic acid (pKa ~4.76).","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_st057","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":3,"q":"A molecule contains three stereogenic centers and no internal symmetry (no meso form). Using 2^n, what is the MAXIMUM possible number of distinct stereoisomers?","opts":["3 (one per center)","6 (n*2)","8 (2^3, all RRR/RRS/RSR/RSS/SRR/SRS/SSR/SSS)","9","16"],"correct":2,"why":"Each stereocenter contributes a factor of 2 (R or S), so the maximum count is 2^n. With n=3 centers and no symmetry to fold pairs into meso compounds, the molecule has 2^3 = 8 distinct stereoisomers (4 pairs of enantiomers).","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_alk074","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":3,"q":"Propene is treated with Br2 in CCl4 at 0 degrees C, kept in the dark to suppress radicals. What is the major product, and what stereochemistry does it inherit from the bromonium ion intermediate?","opts":["3-Bromopropene via radical allylic bromination","2,2-Dibromopropane via Markovnikov dihalide","1,1-Dibromopropane via geminal addition","1,2-Dibromopropane via anti addition","Propenyl bromide via SN1"],"correct":3,"q_smiles":"CC=C","why":"Br2 forms a bridged bromonium ion across the C=C; bromide opens it from the back face, so the two Br atoms add anti (trans). On propene this gives vicinal 1,2-dibromopropane.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec027","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":4,"q":"tert-Amyl chloride (2-chloro-2-methylbutane) is refluxed in ethanol for 12 hours with no added base. GC analysis shows mostly 2-methyl-2-butene plus minor 2-methyl-1-butene, along with some tert-amyl ethyl ether. Which mechanistic combination dominates?","opts":["SN2 on a tertiary carbon","E2 only via ethanol as a strong base","E1 (giving the alkene mixture) with competing SN1 (giving the ether)","Pure SN1 with no alkene formation","Radical decomposition to elemental carbon"],"correct":2,"q_smiles":"CCO","why":"Tertiary substrates in hot polar protic solvents ionize to a carbocation. From the cation, loss of a beta-H gives alkenes (E1, with Zaitsev preference) while solvent capture gives an ether (SN1). The product distribution is the classic E1/SN1 fingerprint.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_lb025","section":"OCHEM","topic":"lab","difficulty":2,"q":"Bromine in CCl4 (orange-red) is added dropwise to four separate samples in the dark. The orange color disappears within seconds for which substrate, indicating addition across the pi bond?","opts":["Cyclohexane (alkane)","Benzene (aromatic, requires Lewis acid catalyst for bromination)","1-hexene (alkene; Br2 adds to C=C, decolorizing)","Ethanol (no pi bond)","Diethyl ether"],"correct":2,"why":"Br2 in CCl4 undergoes electrophilic addition across alkene/alkyne pi bonds at room temperature without catalyst, consuming Br2 and decolorizing the solution. Alkanes need light/heat (radical), aromatics need Lewis acid, and alcohols don't react.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_abo270","section":"OCHEM","topic":"ab-ochem","difficulty":2,"q":"Sodium amide (NaNH2) deprotonates 1-hexyne (pKa ~25) cleanly but does not deprotonate vinyl C-H of 1-hexene (pKa ~44). The pKa gap is best explained by:","opts":["Both protons are ~25; the difference is steric","sp orbital character of the alkyne C (50% s) vs sp2 (33% s) for the alkene — alkyne is more acidic, ~25 vs ~44","Both protons are ~50; reactivity is kinetic","Alkene C-H is more acidic due to better resonance","Inductive effect of the second pi bond raises alkene acidity"],"correct":1,"why":"An sp-hybridized C-H bond holds bonding electrons closer to the carbon nucleus than sp2, stabilizing the conjugate base. Higher s-character means greater acidity, giving terminal alkyne pKa ~25 vs vinyl pKa ~44.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Acid-Base Chemistry"},{"id":"OC_ste051","section":"OCHEM","topic":"stereo-rs","difficulty":4,"q":"(2R,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane has each interior carbon bonded to H, CH3, Br, and a distinct remaining group. How many CIP stereocenters does this meso molecule formally contain?","opts":["4","0","2","1","3"],"correct":2,"why":"Both C2 and C3 bear four different substituents (H, CH3, Br, and a different remainder of the chain), so each is a stereocenter. Two stereocenters can still be 'meso' if there is an internal mirror plane.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Structural Evaluation"},{"id":"OC_alk075","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":3,"q":"Propene reacts with anhydrous HBr at 0 degrees C in the dark with no peroxides. Why does the Markovnikov product dominate, and what is it?","opts":["3-Bromopropane; primary radicals are favored","2-Bromopropane (Markovnikov); H+ adds to give the more stable secondary carbocation, which Br- traps","Propane; HBr is reduced to H2","1-Bromopropane; anti-Markovnikov from steric control","1,2-Dibromopropane via cyclic intermediate"],"correct":1,"q_smiles":"CC=C","opts_smiles":[null,null,"CCC",null],"why":"Without peroxides, addition is ionic. Protonation gives the more stable carbocation, and a secondary cation is favored over a primary one. Bromide then captures the secondary cation to form 2-bromopropane.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_arr001","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-arrows","difficulty":2,"q":"Given starting materials and curved arrows shown, predict the SN2 product.","q_svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 160\" width=\"320\" height=\"160\"><style>\n  .bond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .dbond { stroke: #1a1d20; stroke-width: 1.5; fill: none; }\n  .atom { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; fill: #1a1d20; text-anchor: middle; }\n  .lp   { fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .chg  { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #1a1d20; }\n  .arrow { stroke: #cf3b3b; stroke-width: 1.8; fill: none; }\n  .arrowhead { fill: #cf3b3b; }\n  .lbl { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11px; fill: #6a737a; }\n</style><defs>\n  <marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" class=\"arrowhead\"/>\n  </marker>\n  <marker id=\"ah-half\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\">\n    <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5\" class=\"arrowhead\" stroke=\"#cf3b3b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  </marker>\n</defs>\n    <!-- HO- attacking CH3-Br -->\n    <text x=\"35\" y=\"80\" class=\"atom\">HO</text>\n    <text x=\"55\" y=\"74\" class=\"chg\">⊖</text>\n    <!-- arrow from HO- to C -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 60 86 Q 85 110 110 86\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <!-- methyl bromide -->\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"80\" class=\"atom\">C</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"120\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"50\"/>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"46\" class=\"atom\">H</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"112\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"100\"/>\n    <text x=\"95\" y=\"113\" class=\"atom\">H</text>\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"128\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"100\"/>\n    <text x=\"145\" y=\"113\" class=\"atom\">H</text>\n    <!-- C-Br bond breaking -->\n    <line class=\"bond\" x1=\"132\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <!-- arrow from C-Br to Br -->\n    <path class=\"arrow\" d=\"M 150 70 Q 175 50 195 60\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"80\" class=\"atom\">Br</text>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"84\" class=\"atom\">→ ?</text>\n    <text x=\"180\" y=\"140\" class=\"lbl\">Single SN2 step · backside attack</text>\n  </svg>","opts":["CH3OH + Br−","CH3Br (no reaction)","CH3OBr","CH4 + HOBr"],"opts_smiles":["CO","CBr","COBr","C"],"correct":0,"why":"HO− attacks the carbon backside, displacing Br−. Product: methanol + bromide.","_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_spe219","section":"OCHEM","topic":"spec-ir-nmr","difficulty":3,"q":"A student is reviewing chemical-shift tables for 1H NMR. Aromatic ring protons are deshielded relative to typical sp3 C-H protons because of the ring current induced by the aromatic pi system. What chemical-shift range is most characteristic for aromatic protons?","opts":["3 - 4 ppm","9 - 10 ppm","6.5 - 8.5 ppm","0 - 1 ppm","11 - 13 ppm"],"correct":2,"why":"Aromatic ring currents produce a deshielding cone outside the ring plane, increasing the local field at attached protons. Typical aromatic 1H shifts thus fall in the 6.5 - 8.5 ppm window, downfield of vinyl and alkyl protons.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties"},{"id":"OC_alc144","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alc-eth","difficulty":3,"q":"When planning a Williamson ether synthesis, choose the alkyl halide and alkoxide so that SN2 occurs cleanly on a sterically uncluttered carbon. Which combination is preferred when forming an unsymmetrical ether?","opts":["Primary alkyl halide + alkoxide","Tertiary alkyl halide + alcohol","Aldehyde + alcohol with H+","Secondary alkyl halide + acid","Vinyl bromide + alkoxide"],"correct":0,"why":"Always assign the alkyl halide to the less hindered partner because SN2 needs backside access to the electrophilic carbon. Primary halides allow clean substitution; tertiary halides give E2 elimination; and vinyl/aryl halides simply don't undergo SN2 because their sp2 carbons can't accept backside attack.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec029","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":4,"q":"(R)-3-Bromo-3-methylhexane is dissolved in 90% aqueous acetone at 35 degrees C. The alcohol product shows no measurable optical rotation despite starting from a single enantiomer. What stereochemical outcome explains the result?","opts":["Pure retention of (R)","Pure inversion to (S) via SN2","Loss of the stereocenter through alkene formation","Diastereomeric meso product","Racemization through an achiral planar carbocation intermediate"],"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary substrates ionize to a carbocation in polar protic solvent. The cation is flat (sp2), so water attacks both faces about equally, giving a 1:1 mixture of R and S alcohol that is optically inactive (racemic SN1 product).","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_xp010","section":"OCHEM","topic":"spec-ir-nmr","difficulty":4,"_ada_category":"Chemical & Physical Properties","q":"Which compound shows the LARGEST chemical-shift downfield (highest ppm) for the indicated proton in ¹H NMR? Choose the proton that experiences the strongest deshielding from a combination of electronegativity, anisotropy, and resonance-induced positive character.","opts":["The CH₃ of acetone (δ ~2.1)","The aldehydic H of butanal (δ ~9.7)","The carboxylic-acid OH of acetic acid (δ ~12)","The vinyl CH of styrene (δ ~5.7)","The aromatic H of benzene (δ ~7.3)"],"correct":2,"why":"Carboxylic acid O–H typically appears as a very broad signal at 11–13 ppm — by far the most deshielded common proton due to strong H-bonding plus the electron-poor O. Aldehydic H is next (~9.7)."},{"id":"OC_aci185","section":"OCHEM","topic":"acids","difficulty":3,"q":"Benzoyl chloride is added dropwise to a stirred solution of diethylamine (2.2 equiv) in anhydrous tetrahydrofuran at 0 degrees C. After warming to room temperature, what are the principal organic and inorganic products?","opts":["Benzoic acid and diethylammonium chloride","Ethyl benzoate and ethylammonium chloride","Benzoic anhydride and diethylamine","N,N-diethylbenzamide and HCl (trapped as the ammonium salt)","Benzaldehyde and diethylformamide"],"correct":3,"opts_smiles":[null,null,null,"Cl[H]"],"why":"Acid chlorides react rapidly with secondary amines to give tertiary amides via nucleophilic acyl substitution. The first equivalent of amine forms the tetrahedral intermediate that loses chloride; the second equivalent acts as a base, neutralizing HCl as the alkylammonium salt.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_mec003","section":"OCHEM","topic":"mech-sne","difficulty":1,"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is dissolved in methanol (acting as both solvent and nucleophile) and heated gently. Which mechanism best describes the dominant substitution pathway under these conditions?","opts":["SN2, because methanol is a small nucleophile","SN1, because the tertiary carbocation is stabilized by the polar protic solvent","E2, because methanol acts as a strong base","Concerted addition with carbocation rearrangement","Radical chain substitution initiated by trace peroxides"],"correct":1,"why":"Tertiary substrates cannot undergo backside SN2 attack due to steric crowding. Polar protic solvents stabilize the carbocation and the leaving group, favoring ionization (SN1). Weak nucleophiles like methanol simply trap the cation after it forms.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Mechanisms"},{"id":"OC_alk073","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alkene-add","difficulty":3,"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with O3 in CH2Cl2 at -78 degrees C, then with Zn/AcOH workup. Identify the major product and explain why a ring-opened compound forms instead of a cyclic ketone.","opts":["Hexanedial; ozone cleaves the C=C bond, opening the ring into a chain with aldehydes at both ends","Cyclohexanone, by Wacker-type conversion","Hexanedioic acid via oxidative workup","Cyclohexanol after over-reduction","Cyclohexan-1,2-dione"],"correct":0,"q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_smiles":[null,"O=C1CCCCC1",null,"OC1CCCCC1"],"why":"Ozone breaks the C=C bond and reductive workup gives carbonyls at the aldehyde stage. Because cyclohexene's double bond is part of the ring, cleaving it converts a cyclic alkene into an open dialdehyde chain (hexanedial).","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"},{"id":"OC_alc136","section":"OCHEM","topic":"alc-eth","difficulty":2,"q":"When designing a Williamson ether synthesis to make a methyl alkyl ether, the alkyl halide should be the less hindered partner so that SN2 outcompetes E2. Which combination is best?","opts":["Primary alkyl halide + alkoxide nucleophile","Aldehyde + alcohol (no acid)","Tertiary alkyl halide + sodium alkoxide","Secondary alkyl halide + carboxylic acid","Aryl bromide + sodium ethoxide"],"correct":0,"why":"The Williamson ether synthesis is an SN2 reaction, so steric crowding kills it: tertiary halides give E2 elimination instead. The optimal pairing places the alkyl group on the halide side as primary (or methyl) and the bulkier group on the alkoxide. Aryl halides don't work either because SN2 doesn't proceed on sp2 aromatic carbon.","_rewritten":true,"_ada_category":"Chemical Synthesis"}]}}];